Guide.content._register("w01", {"id":"w01","order":1,"region":"foundations","title":"LLM Controls and APIs","short":"Controls and APIs","tagline":"Tokens, knobs and APIs: what you can control, and what the model quietly rejects.","story":"The question reaches the model. Before it answers, the request is cut into tokens, fitted into a window and shaped by controls that differ by vendor and by model family. Pick wrongly and you get half an answer, a 400 on a parameter that used to work, a bill that jumps at a hidden threshold or state you cannot delete.","oneLiner":"Learn the dials on the model itself, then choose the API, state model and knowledge strategy that survive production.","youWillLearn":["Budget tokens across input, output and reasoning, and count them on the model you will actually call","Choose between temperature, top_p, seed, penalties, logit_bias, logprobs and stop, and know which a reasoning model rejects","Set reasoning effort, and decide between a bigger model, more effort, a router or a cascade","Pick structured outputs, JSON mode or strict function calling, and know the schema limits per platform","Compare Chat Completions, Responses and Anthropic Messages on state ownership, tools, portability and retention","Decide between long context, RAG, cache-augmented generation and fine-tuning (LoRA, distillation) for a given corpus"],"minutes":120,"prereqs":["w00"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w01-fc-001","front":"What three things share one context window?","back":"Input tokens, visible output tokens and reasoning (thinking) tokens. Input and output also have separate caps, so check both.","tags":["tokens"]},{"id":"w01-fc-002","front":"Does `max_output_tokens` reserve tokens for the answer?","back":"No. It is a ceiling and it includes reasoning tokens. A low cap can return `status: incomplete` before any visible text appears.","tags":["tokens","reasoning"]},{"id":"w01-fc-003","front":"How much headroom do OpenAI and Azure advise when you first use a reasoning model?","back":"Reserve at least 25,000 tokens for reasoning and output, then tune after you measure `reasoning_tokens` in the usage object.","tags":["tokens","reasoning"]},{"id":"w01-fc-004","front":"Why recount tokens when you move a prompt to Claude Opus 4.7 or later?","back":"Tokenizers differ. Anthropic says the newer tokenizer gives roughly 30% more tokens for the same text, depending on content.","tags":["tokens"]},{"id":"w01-fc-005","front":"On GPT-6 models, what happens to the price above 272K input tokens?","back":"The whole request costs 2x the input and cache rates and 1.5x the output rate, not only the tokens above 272K.","tags":["tokens","cost"]},{"id":"w01-fc-006","front":"Name two things a `characters / 4` estimate gets wrong.","back":"Images and files, and tool definitions. Use a vendor count endpoint such as `POST /v1/responses/input_tokens` before you budget near a limit.","tags":["tokens"]},{"id":"w01-fc-007","front":"Which controls exist in Chat Completions but not in the Responses body?","back":"`seed`, `stop`, `n`, `frequency_penalty`, `presence_penalty` and `logit_bias`. Neither OpenAI API has `top_k`.","tags":["sampling","api"]},{"id":"w01-fc-008","front":"Is `seed` a determinism guarantee?","back":"No. It is best effort and marked Beta in Chat Completions. Log `system_fingerprint` to spot backend changes between calls.","tags":["sampling"]},{"id":"w01-fc-009","front":"Where does `top_k` exist?","back":"Not in OpenAI bodies. Anthropic Messages deprecated it and returns 400 on models released after Opus 4.6. Gemini uses `topK` in `generationConfig`.","tags":["sampling"]},{"id":"w01-fc-010","front":"What sampling values do Claude models released after Opus 4.6 accept?","back":"Only temperature 1.0 and top_p of 0.99 or higher. Any other value returns HTTP 400.","tags":["sampling","anthropic"]},{"id":"w01-fc-011","front":"What does OpenAI say to remove when reasoning effort is not `none`?","back":"`temperature`, `top_p` and `top_logprobs`, plus `logprobs` in Chat. Azure's page lists more as rejected, so test your deployment.","tags":["sampling","reasoning"]},{"id":"w01-fc-012","front":"Which max-token parameter belongs to which API?","back":"`max_completion_tokens` for Chat, `max_output_tokens` for Responses, `max_tokens` for Anthropic (required). All three include reasoning or thinking tokens.","tags":["tokens","api"]},{"id":"w01-fc-013","front":"What does `logit_bias` take?","back":"A map from token id to a bias between -100 and 100, in Chat Completions only. Values between -1 and 1 are the usual range.","tags":["sampling"]},{"id":"w01-fc-014","front":"Which effort values does GPT-6 Astra accept?","back":"`low` to `max`. `none` returns HTTP 400. GPT-6.1 Sol also drops `minimal`, while GPT-6 Sol and Luna accept `none`.","tags":["reasoning"]},{"id":"w01-fc-015","front":"How do you set effort and thinking on Anthropic?","back":"`output_config.effort` takes `low` to `max`, and `thinking` is `adaptive`. Manual `budget_tokens` is not accepted on the newest models.","tags":["reasoning","anthropic"]},{"id":"w01-fc-016","front":"JSON mode versus structured outputs?","back":"JSON mode only guarantees valid JSON. Structured outputs (`json_schema` with `strict: true`) guarantee the schema.","tags":["structured"]},{"id":"w01-fc-017","front":"Name three OpenAI strict-schema rules.","back":"Every field required (use a union with `null` for optional), `additionalProperties: false`, and an object root. Limits are 5,000 properties and 10 nesting levels.","tags":["structured"]},{"id":"w01-fc-018","front":"How do Azure structured output limits differ from OpenAI's?","back":"Azure allows up to 100 properties and 5 nesting levels, and lists `minLength`, `maxLength`, `pattern` and `format` as unsupported. Read the Azure page for Azure.","tags":["structured","azure"]},{"id":"w01-fc-019","front":"What breaks when you use Chat Completions with tools and reasoning on GPT-5.4 or later?","back":"Chat Completions does not support tool calling when `reasoning_effort` is not `none`. Use Responses.","tags":["api","tools"]},{"id":"w01-fc-020","front":"What is an Item in the Responses API?","back":"A typed unit such as `message`, `reasoning`, `function_call` or `function_call_output`. `output` can hold several, so never assume text sits at index 0.","tags":["api"]},{"id":"w01-fc-021","front":"Three ways to hold conversation state with OpenAI?","back":"Replay history yourself, chain with `previous_response_id`, or use the Conversations API, which is durable (no 30 day TTL) but not ZDR eligible.","tags":["state"]},{"id":"w01-fc-022","front":"What does `previous_response_id` cost you?","back":"Earlier input tokens bill again on every turn, responses expire after 30 days and top-level `instructions` are not carried over.","tags":["state"]},{"id":"w01-fc-023","front":"How does stateless mode work for reasoning models?","back":"With `store` false or ZDR, reasoning items carry `encrypted_content`. You replay every output item, encrypted reasoning included.","tags":["state","retention"]},{"id":"w01-fc-024","front":"Does `store: false` mean nothing is kept?","back":"No. Abuse monitoring logs can keep content up to 30 days unless ZDR or Modified Abuse Monitoring applies.","tags":["retention"]},{"id":"w01-fc-025","front":"What happened to the OpenAI Assistants API?","back":"Announced 2025-08-26, sunset 2026-08-26. Threads map to Conversations, Runs to Responses. Azure docs give the same date and point to Foundry Agent Service.","tags":["api","lifecycle"]},{"id":"w01-fc-026","front":"Is Anthropic Messages stateful?","back":"No. `POST /v1/messages` is stateless, so you send the full history every call. `max_tokens` is required and `stop_reason` replaces `finish_reason`.","tags":["api","anthropic"]},{"id":"w01-fc-027","front":"When does Anthropic say you can skip RAG?","back":"For a knowledge base under about 200,000 tokens (about 500 pages, an estimate): put it in the prompt and use prompt caching.","tags":["knowledge"]},{"id":"w01-fc-028","front":"Why is fine-tuning a weak way to add facts?","back":"Ovadia et al. found RAG beat unsupervised fine-tuning. Gekhman et al. found new facts are learned slowly and, once learned, raise hallucination.","tags":["knowledge","finetuning"]},{"id":"w01-fc-029","front":"What does LoRA change in a model?","back":"It freezes the base weights and trains low-rank matrices. Against full Adam fine-tuning of GPT-3 175B: 10,000x fewer trainable parameters, 3x less GPU memory, no added inference latency.","tags":["finetuning"]},{"id":"w01-fc-030","front":"What is distillation?","back":"Training a small model on a large model's outputs (Hinton et al., 2015). OpenAI's pattern: tune the prompt on a large model, then fine-tune a smaller one on its outputs.","tags":["finetuning"]},{"id":"w01-fc-031","front":"What are the Azure model router modes?","back":"Balanced (default, cheapest model within about 1% to 2% of top quality), Cost (about 5% to 6%) and Quality. The window is the smallest in the pool.","tags":["routing"]},{"id":"w01-fc-032","front":"What does batch processing give you, and what does it cost?","back":"50% off on OpenAI, Azure and Anthropic, with a 24 hour window. It is offline only, and OpenAI batches are not ZDR eligible.","tags":["routing","cost"]},{"id":"w01-t01-fc1","front":"Why can't you reuse a token count across vendors?","back":"Each model family has its own tokenizer. Claude Opus 4.7 and later yield about 30% more tokens than earlier Claude models for the same text.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t01"},{"id":"w01-t01-fc2","front":"Which OpenAI endpoint returns an exact input count?","back":"`POST /v1/responses/input_tokens`, which takes Responses-style input and includes role and boundary tokens.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t01"},{"id":"w01-t01-fc3","front":"What do tool definitions do to a request?","back":"They are injected as input text, count against context and bill as input tokens.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t01"},{"id":"w01-t02-fc1","front":"Does `max_output_tokens` reserve tokens?","back":"No. It is a ceiling. Reasoning tokens count against it, so a low value can leave no room for visible text.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t02"},{"id":"w01-t02-fc2","front":"How much headroom does OpenAI advise for reasoning and output at the start?","back":"At least 25,000 tokens, then tune after measuring reasoning token use.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t02"},{"id":"w01-t02-fc3","front":"Why can a model's listed window differ from what you get on Azure?","back":"Deployment type sets the limit. `gpt-4.1` allows 300,000 on standard and 128,000 on provisioned or batch, though the model supports 1,047,576.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t02"},{"id":"w01-t03-fc1","front":"Where do reasoning tokens show up in usage?","back":"`output_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` in Responses and `completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` in Chat Completions. They bill as output.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t03"},{"id":"w01-t03-fc2","front":"Which GPT-6 models reject `reasoning.effort: none`?","back":"GPT-6 Astra (HTTP 400) and GPT-6.1 Sol (no `none` or `minimal`). GPT-6 Sol and Luna accept `none` to `max`.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t03"},{"id":"w01-t03-fc3","front":"What do you start with when tuning effort?","back":"Start low and raise only when evals show a gain, since effort adds output tokens and latency.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t03"},{"id":"w01-t03-fc4","front":"What does Claude use instead of `budget_tokens` on new models?","back":"Adaptive thinking with `output_config.effort`. Manual `budget_tokens` is deprecated on Opus 4.6 and Sonnet 4.6 and not accepted on later models.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t03"},{"id":"w01-t04-fc1","front":"What does temperature do to the next-token distribution?","back":"It divides the logits before the softmax. Low values sharpen toward the top token and high values flatten the distribution.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t04"},{"id":"w01-t04-fc2","front":"What is top_p?","back":"Nucleus sampling: sample only from the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches p.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t04"},{"id":"w01-t04-fc3","front":"Which Claude models accept only temperature 1.0?","back":"Models released after Opus 4.6, including Opus 4.7. They also need top_p of 0.99 or higher, and other values return 400.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t04"},{"id":"w01-t05-fc1","front":"Which major APIs expose `top_k`?","back":"Not OpenAI Responses or Chat Completions. Anthropic lists it as deprecated and rejects it on models after Opus 4.6. Gemini has `topK` in `generationConfig`.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t05"},{"id":"w01-t05-fc2","front":"How reliable is `seed`?","back":"Best effort and Beta, on Chat Completions only. Log `system_fingerprint` to spot backend changes.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t05"},{"id":"w01-t05-fc3","front":"How do you test a flaky structured extraction?","back":"Pin the model snapshot, parse fields, run each case several times and assert a pass rate instead of exact strings.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t05"},{"id":"w01-t06-fc1","front":"What range do the penalties and `logit_bias` accept?","back":"Penalties -2.0 to 2.0, `logit_bias` -100 to 100. Values between -1 and 1 are the usual range for penalties.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t06"},{"id":"w01-t06-fc2","front":"How many stop sequences does OpenAI Chat accept?","back":"Up to 4. `stop` is not supported on o3 and o4-mini, and the Responses API has no `stop`.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t06"},{"id":"w01-t06-fc3","front":"Why is `logit_bias` fragile across vendors?","back":"It uses tokenizer-specific ids, word variants have different ids, and some APIs do not support it at all.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t06"},{"id":"w01-t07-fc1","front":"How do you turn a logprob into a probability?","back":"Apply `exp`. A logprob of -0.105 gives about 0.90.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t07"},{"id":"w01-t07-fc2","front":"What is the single-token label pattern?","back":"Ask for one label with a tiny token cap, read its probability and escalate below a threshold calibrated on labelled data.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t07"},{"id":"w01-t07-fc3","front":"Why prefer labels with distinct first tokens?","back":"Probability splits across variants that share a first token, which makes the comparison unreliable.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t07"},{"id":"w01-t08-fc1","front":"JSON mode vs structured outputs?","back":"JSON mode guarantees valid JSON only. Structured outputs with `strict: true` guarantee the output follows your schema.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t08"},{"id":"w01-t08-fc2","front":"Azure vs OpenAI schema limits?","back":"Azure allows 100 object properties and 5 nesting levels. OpenAI allows 5,000 properties and 10 levels.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t08"},{"id":"w01-t08-fc3","front":"How do you model an optional field in strict mode?","back":"Keep it in `required` and make its type a union with `null`.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t08"},{"id":"w01-t08-fc4","front":"Where do Anthropic structured outputs live?","back":"`output_config.format` with `type: \"json_schema\"`, plus `strict: true` on tools for schema-valid tool inputs.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t08"},{"id":"w01-t09-fc1","front":"Who executes a tool call?","back":"Your application. The model only returns the call, and you send back the result with the `call_id`.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t09"},{"id":"w01-t09-fc2","front":"What does `parallel_tool_calls: false` do on OpenAI?","back":"The model makes zero or one tool call per turn.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t09"},{"id":"w01-t09-fc3","front":"What is the OpenAI guidance for the number of tools?","back":"Fewer than 20 functions at the start of a turn (soft advice). Use `tool_search` with `defer_loading` for large catalogs on gpt-5.4 and later.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t09"},{"id":"w01-t10-fc1","front":"Why does cost grow over a long Chat Completions conversation?","back":"The API is stateless, so you resend the full history each turn and pay for those input tokens again.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t10"},{"id":"w01-t10-fc2","front":"Which cap parameter replaces `max_tokens` in Chat Completions?","back":"`max_completion_tokens`. `max_tokens` is deprecated and not compatible with o-series.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t10"},{"id":"w01-t10-fc3","front":"When must you leave Chat Completions for Responses?","back":"For reasoning with tools on GPT-5.4 and later, for GPT-6 Astra and 6.1 Sol tools, and for built-in tools or server-held state.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t10"},{"id":"w01-t11-fc1","front":"What is an Item in the Responses API?","back":"A typed object such as `message`, `reasoning`, `function_call` or `function_call_output`. `output` can contain several, so never assume text is at index 0.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t11"},{"id":"w01-t11-fc2","front":"What happens to `instructions` with `previous_response_id`?","back":"They are not carried over. Send them on every call.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t11"},{"id":"w01-t11-fc3","front":"What do you replay in stateless mode?","back":"Every output item, including reasoning items with `encrypted_content`, so the model keeps its reasoning context.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t11"},{"id":"w01-t11-fc4","front":"Is the Conversations API ZDR eligible?","back":"No. It was released on 2025-08-20, has no 30 day TTL and conflicts with no-retention policies.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t11"},{"id":"w01-t12-fc1","front":"Name the four dials for choosing Chat Completions or Responses.","back":"Model family, state ownership, portability across vendors and data retention.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t12"},{"id":"w01-t12-fc2","front":"When was the OpenAI Assistants API sunset?","back":"2026-08-26, after deprecation was announced on 2025-08-26. Threads map to Conversations and Runs to Responses.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t12"},{"id":"w01-t12-fc3","front":"How do you call Azure OpenAI with the v1 API?","back":"Use the `OpenAI()` client with a base URL ending `/openai/v1`. `api-version` is no longer required.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t12"},{"id":"w01-t13-fc1","front":"How long does ZDR background data stay on disk for polling?","back":"Roughly 10 minutes. Poll promptly and persist the result yourself.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t13"},{"id":"w01-t13-fc2","front":"Which Anthropic events carry tool input deltas?","back":"`content_block_delta` with `input_json_delta`, arriving one complete key and value at a time.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t13"},{"id":"w01-t13-fc3","front":"Which mode do you pick when nobody is waiting?","back":"Batch: 50% of Standard, a 24 hour window and a separate rate-limit pool.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t13"},{"id":"w01-t14-fc1","front":"What does fine-tuning change best?","back":"Behaviour: format, tone, tool use, shorter prompts and smaller models. It is weak for adding facts.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t14"},{"id":"w01-t14-fc2","front":"What is CAG and when does it fit?","back":"Cache-augmented generation preloads documents and caches runtime state. It fits a small, stable corpus, roughly under 200,000 tokens per Anthropic's guidance.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t14"},{"id":"w01-t14-fc3","front":"Name three studies behind the long-context caution.","back":"Chroma Context Rot (18 LLMs), RULER (17 models) and NoLiMa (13 models, 11 below 50% of baseline at 32K).","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t14"},{"id":"w01-t15-fc1","front":"When do you choose DPO over SFT?","back":"When you have preferred and rejected pairs but no single ideal answer per prompt. `beta` (0 to 2) sets how far the model moves from the base.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t15"},{"id":"w01-t15-fc2","front":"What did the QLoRA paper show?","back":"A 65B model fine-tuned on one 48GB GPU using 4-bit NormalFloat, double quantization and paged optimizers.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t15"},{"id":"w01-t15-fc3","front":"When does OpenAI stop new fine-tuning jobs for active customers?","back":"2027-01-06. Fine-tuned inference ends when the base model is deprecated.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t15"},{"id":"w01-t15-fc4","front":"Which methods does Azure offer for managed fine-tuning?","back":"SFT, DPO and RFT, with Standard, Data zone, Global and Developer training types.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t15"},{"id":"w01-t16-fc1","front":"What is distillation?","back":"Training a small student model on a large teacher's outputs (Hinton et al., 2015), so the student imitates the teacher at lower cost and latency.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t16"},{"id":"w01-t16-fc2","front":"How big is the price gap between GPT-6 Astra and GPT-6 Luna?","back":"About 100 times on both sides: $10 and $50 against $0.10 and $0.50 per 1M input and output tokens.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t16"},{"id":"w01-t16-fc3","front":"What should you compare, price per token or cost per task?","back":"Cost per task. A model with fewer output tokens or retries can cost less per accepted task.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t16"},{"id":"w01-t17-fc1","front":"Name four router types.","back":"Rules, a learned router, an LLM router and a cascade. Azure also offers a managed model router.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t17"},{"id":"w01-t17-fc2","front":"Which deployments support the Azure model router?","back":"Global Standard and Data Zone Standard only.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t17"},{"id":"w01-t17-fc3","front":"How do you evaluate a router?","back":"Replay the same traffic through always-small, always-large and the router, then compare quality at cost, escalation rate, router errors by segment, p95 latency and cache hit rate.","tags":[],"topic":"w01-t17"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"GPT-6 Astra model page","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6-astra","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Context, output, prices, endpoint and tool support in one page.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r2","title":"Using GPT-6","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/latest-model","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Migration rules: effort values, parameters to remove, async tools, cache option changes.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r3","title":"Reasoning models","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Effort, mode, summaries, reasoning context, encrypted reasoning and token headroom.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r4","title":"Responses API reference: create","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/responses/methods/create","type":"spec","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Authoritative body parameter list for Responses.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r5","title":"Chat Completions API reference","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/chat","type":"spec","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"seed, n, stop, penalties, logit_bias and token cap names.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r6","title":"Migrate to the Responses API","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/migrate-to-responses","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Items versus Messages, state options and tool limits on Chat Completions.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r7","title":"Conversation state","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/conversation-state","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"previous_response_id, the Conversations API and 30 day retention.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r8","title":"Structured outputs","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/structured-outputs","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Schema subset, limits and the comparison with JSON mode.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r9","title":"Function calling","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/function-calling","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"strict, tool_choice, parallel calls and tool search.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r10","title":"Data controls in the OpenAI platform","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/your-data","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"ZDR behaviour and the endpoint eligibility table.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r11","title":"OpenAI deprecations","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/deprecations","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Shutdown dates for models, Assistants, Evals, prompts and fine-tuning.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r12","title":"OpenAI API changelog","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/changelog","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Dated feature and model releases.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r13","title":"Foundry Models sold directly by Azure","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Azure context windows, versions and the fine-tuning model table.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r14","title":"Azure OpenAI v1 API and API version lifecycle","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/api-version-lifecycle","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The /openai/v1 base URL, no api-version, and how preview features are exposed.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r15","title":"Azure OpenAI reasoning models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/reasoning","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Feature tables, tools with reasoning and unsupported parameters.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r16","title":"Model router for Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/model-router","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Routing modes, subset, failover, context and caching limits. Session affinity is Preview as of 2026-10-13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r17","title":"Fine-tuning in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/fine-tuning/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Methods, training types and deployment options. Parts are Preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r18","title":"Claude models overview","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Model ids, windows, prices and retirement dates.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r19","title":"Claude Messages API reference","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/messages","type":"spec","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Body parameters, sampling rules, thinking and tool_choice.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r20","title":"Claude context windows","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"How thinking, tools, images and documents use the window.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r21","title":"Claude Platform release notes","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/release-notes/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Dated model launches and breaking changes.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"Context Rot: How Increasing Input Tokens Impacts LLM Performance","url":"https://research.trychroma.com/context-rot","type":"paper","publisher":"Chroma","note":"Report (2025-07-14) showing that longer inputs degrade results across 18 LLMs.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"RULER: What's the Real Context Size of Your Long-Context Language Models?","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.06654","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Benchmark showing claimed windows exceed effective windows.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r24","title":"Don't Do RAG: When Cache-Augmented Generation is All You Need for Knowledge Tasks","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.15605","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"The case for preloading small corpora.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r25","title":"QLoRA: Efficient Finetuning of Quantized LLMs","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Parameter-efficient fine-tuning on a single GPU.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r26","title":"Develop generative AI apps on Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/training/paths/create-custom-copilots-ai-studio/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Study path through models, prompts and APIs on Azure.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r27","title":"Finetuning Large Language Models","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/short-courses/finetuning-large-language-models/","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Study material on when and how to fine-tune.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r28","title":"Let's build the GPT Tokenizer","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zduSFxRajkE","type":"video","publisher":"Andrej Karpathy","note":"Study material on why tokenizers behave the way they do.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r29","title":"Counting tokens (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/token-counting","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"The input_tokens endpoint and what local tokenizers miss.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r30","title":"Token counting (Claude)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/token-counting","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"count_tokens, the newer tokenizer and its limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r31","title":"Effort (Claude)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"output_config.effort values and adaptive thinking.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r32","title":"Structured outputs (Claude)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/structured-outputs","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"output_config.format and strict tool use.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r33","title":"Structured outputs in Azure OpenAI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/structured-outputs","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Azure schema limits and unsupported keywords.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r34","title":"Azure OpenAI Responses API","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/responses","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Retention, background mode, compaction, built-in tools and MCP on Azure.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r35","title":"Background mode","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/background","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Polling, retention and ZDR behaviour for background responses.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r36","title":"OpenAI SDK compatibility (Claude)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/openai-sdk","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Which OpenAI parameters the compatibility layer silently ignores.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r37","title":"Batch API (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/batch","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"50% discount, 24h window, file limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r38","title":"Flex processing (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/flex-processing","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Cheaper, slower tier. Beta as of 2026-10-13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r39","title":"Message Batches (Claude)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Limits, expiry and retention for batch jobs.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r40","title":"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Accuracy is best at the start and end of the context.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r41","title":"NoLiMa: Long-Context Evaluation Beyond Literal Matching","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.05167","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Most long-context models fall below half their short-context score at 32K.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r42","title":"Introducing Contextual Retrieval","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"The 200,000 token rule of thumb and the retrieval failure reductions.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r43","title":"Fine-Tuning or Retrieval? Comparing Knowledge Injection in LLMs","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05934","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"RAG beat unsupervised fine-tuning for knowledge.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r44","title":"Does Fine-Tuning LLMs on New Knowledge Encourage Hallucinations?","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.05904","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"New facts are learned slowly and then raise hallucination.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r45","title":"LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"The low-rank adapter idea behind most parameter-efficient tuning.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r46","title":"Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18290","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Preference tuning without a separate reward model.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r47","title":"Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.02531","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"The original distillation paper.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r48","title":"Model optimization (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/model-optimization","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"What fine-tuning is for and the wind-down of self-serve tuning.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r49","title":"Azure OpenAI Assistants (classic)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry-classic/openai/how-to/assistant","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Retirement notice and migration to Foundry Agent Service.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"},{"id":"r50","title":"Implement tool use (Claude)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/implement-tool-use","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"tool_choice values, parallel tool use and strict tools.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r51","title":"Assistants migration guide","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/assistants/migration","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Maps Threads to Conversations and Runs to Responses.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r52","title":"Supervised fine-tuning (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/supervised-fine-tuning","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Minimum examples and supported models for SFT. The platform is winding down.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"},{"id":"r53","title":"Direct preference optimization (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/direct-preference-optimization","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"DPO data format and the beta parameter.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"},{"id":"r54","title":"Reinforcement fine-tuning (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reinforcement-fine-tuning","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Graders and supported models for RFT.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"},{"id":"r55","title":"Claude model deprecations","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/model-deprecations","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Notice period of at least 60 days for publicly released models.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr1","title":"Streaming API responses (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/streaming-responses","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Responses stream event names and how to consume them.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Streaming Messages (Claude)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/streaming","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Server-sent event types, delta types and the rule that tool input arrives one key and value at a time.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr3","title":"Gemini API generateContent reference","url":"https://ai.google.dev/api/generate-content","type":"spec","publisher":"Google","note":"Shows `topK` inside `generationConfig`, as the contrast with OpenAI and Anthropic.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr4","title":"tiktoken","url":"https://github.com/openai/tiktoken","type":"repo","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Local tokenizer library. The README names the `o200k_base` and `cl100k_base` encodings.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr5","title":"Azure OpenAI model retirement schedule","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/model-retirement-schedule","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Per-model retirement dates on Azure, which differ from the OpenAI API dates.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr6","title":"Async tool calling (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/async-tool-calling","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"`async: true` on tools with GPT-6: the model keeps working while your app runs the tool.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr7","title":"Why you get a 429 below your quota (Azure OpenAI, Dev Proxy)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-cloud/dev/dev-proxy/concepts/azure-openai-429-token-rate-limit","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Explains that the TPM check counts `max_tokens` in full, so an oversized cap causes 429s.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Token","def":"The unit a model reads and writes. A token is usually a word piece, not a word. Counts differ by tokenizer, so always count on the model you call."},{"term":"Context window","def":"The total tokens a model can hold in one request. Input, visible output and reasoning tokens share it, and input and output also carry separate caps."},{"term":"Reasoning tokens","def":"Hidden thinking tokens. They are billed as output, count against the window and the output cap, and appear in usage details."},{"term":"Nucleus sampling (top_p)","def":"Sample only from the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches `p`."},{"term":"Logprobs","def":"Log probabilities of the generated tokens and their top alternatives. Useful as a confidence signal for classification."},{"term":"Structured outputs","def":"A decoding mode that constrains output to a JSON schema (`strict: true`). JSON mode only guarantees syntactically valid JSON."},{"term":"Item","def":"The typed unit of the Responses API: `message`, `reasoning`, `function_call`, `function_call_output` and others."},{"term":"ZDR","def":"Zero data retention. An account-level control that forces `store` to false and limits which endpoints you may use."},{"term":"Compaction","def":"Shrinking a long conversation by summarizing or encrypting earlier turns. OpenAI offers client-side `/responses/compact` and server-side `context_management`."},{"term":"Cache-augmented generation (CAG)","def":"Preload a small, stable corpus into a long context and reuse its cached state instead of retrieving per question."},{"term":"LoRA","def":"Low-rank adaptation. Freeze the base weights and train small low-rank matrices, which cuts trainable parameters and memory."},{"term":"Distillation","def":"Train a small model to imitate a large model's outputs to keep most of the behaviour at lower cost."},{"term":"Model router","def":"A service that picks a model per request. Azure's version has Balanced, Cost and Quality modes and failover."},{"term":"Cascade","def":"Try a small model first and escalate to a larger one when a check fails, such as an invalid schema or low confidence."},{"term":"Temperature","def":"A value that divides the logits before the softmax. Low values sharpen the distribution toward the top token, and high values flatten it."},{"term":"top_k","def":"Limits sampling to the k most probable tokens. Not in OpenAI Responses or Chat Completions, and deprecated on Anthropic Messages."},{"term":"seed","def":"A Chat Completions parameter that asks for best-effort repeatable sampling. It is Beta and does not guarantee identical outputs."},{"term":"logit_bias","def":"A map from token id to a value between -100 and 100 added to that token's score. Ids are tokenizer specific."},{"term":"Stop sequence","def":"A string that ends generation when the model emits it. OpenAI Chat accepts up to 4. Anthropic reports `stop_reason: stop_sequence`."},{"term":"Strict mode","def":"A setting (`strict: true`) that makes tool arguments or structured outputs follow your JSON schema exactly."},{"term":"Tool call","def":"A model output that names a tool and its JSON arguments. Your application runs the tool and returns the result with the call id."},{"term":"Reasoning effort","def":"A control (`reasoning.effort`, `reasoning_effort`, `output_config.effort`) for how much hidden thinking a model does. Values depend on the model."},{"term":"System fingerprint","def":"A field that identifies the backend configuration behind a Chat Completions response. A change can explain different outputs for the same seed."},{"term":"previous_response_id","def":"A Responses parameter that chains a call to an earlier response. Earlier input tokens bill again and `instructions` are not carried over."},{"term":"Conversations API","def":"A durable conversation object for Responses, released 2025-08-20. It has no 30 day TTL and is not ZDR eligible."},{"term":"Encrypted reasoning","def":"Reasoning items returned with `encrypted_content` in stateless mode, which you replay so the model keeps its reasoning context."},{"term":"Background mode","def":"Setting `background: true` so a long Responses call runs on the server while you poll its status."},{"term":"SFT","def":"Supervised fine-tuning on input and ideal-output examples. OpenAI needs at least 10 examples and often sees gains at 50 to 100."},{"term":"DPO","def":"Direct preference optimization: training on preferred and rejected pairs with a classification loss, with a `beta` from 0 to 2."},{"term":"RFT","def":"Reinforcement fine-tuning, where a grader you write scores outputs. OpenAI lists `o4-mini` only."},{"term":"QLoRA","def":"LoRA applied on a 4-bit quantized base model, which let a 65B model be fine-tuned on one 48GB GPU in the paper."}],"levels":[{"id":"w01-l1","order":1,"title":"Count the budget before you spend it","kind":"concept","minutes":15,"brief":"A request fails on length, or worse, returns half an answer. Before you touch a sampling knob, work out what the token budget is made of and why the same text costs different amounts on different vendors.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"One budget, three consumers","level":2,"id":"one-budget-three-consumers"},{"t":"p","md":"A **token** is a word piece, not a word. Every request spends one budget: your input, the visible output and any hidden reasoning tokens all share the context window. Input and output also have separate caps, so having room in the window does not mean you may use all of it for either side. Azure states that `max_output_tokens` is a ceiling, not a reservation [[r13]]."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"GPT-6 family window","value":"1,050,000","note":"922,000 max input and 128,000 max output (OpenAI, checked 2026-10-13) [[r1]]"},{"label":"Azure GPT-5 era window","value":"400,000","note":"272,000 input and 128,000 output for gpt-5, 5.1, 5.2 and 5.4-mini class models [[r13]]"},{"label":"Claude 1M models","value":"1M","note":"128K sync output. Haiku 5.5 charges more above 100,000 prompt tokens [[r18]]"},{"label":"Long-prompt price step","value":"2x in, 1.5x out","note":"GPT-6 prompts above 272K input tokens: the whole request is repriced [[r1]]"}]},{"t":"table","caption":"What counts toward the window and how it bills","head":["Item","In the window","Billed as","What to know"],"rows":[["System prompt and messages","Yes","Input","Everything you send, including earlier turns you replay."],["Tool and function definitions","Yes","Input","OpenAI injects them into the system message. Its soft advice is fewer than 20 functions at the start of a turn, and `tool_search` with `defer_loading` for large catalogs on gpt-5.4 and later [[r9]]. Claude counts them too [[r20]]."],["Images and PDF pages","Yes","Input","A Claude request can carry up to 600 images or PDF pages, or 100 on 200k-window models [[r20]]."],["Reasoning or thinking tokens","Yes","Output","Reported in `output_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` (Responses) or `completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` (Chat) [[r3]]. Claude keeps earlier thinking blocks by default on recent models [[r20]]."]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"formula","title":"Window check","expr":"input_tokens + visible_output + reasoning_tokens <= context_window","vars":[{"k":"input_tokens","v":"everything you send, tools and images included"},{"k":"visible_output + reasoning_tokens","v":"what the model generates, capped separately by the output limit"},{"k":"context_window","v":"the limit of this deployment, which can be lower than the model's limit"}],"example":"`gpt-4.1` supports 1,047,576 tokens, but an Azure standard deployment allows 300,000 and provisioned or batch deployments allow 128,000 [[r13]]. A 310,000-token prompt fails on the Azure standard deployment and fits on the OpenAI API."},{"t":"h","text":"Count before you send","level":2,"id":"count-before-you-send"},{"t":"compare","title":"Four ways to count tokens","intro":"Each one is right for a different decision. Pick by how close to a limit you are and which model you will call.","options":[{"name":"Characters divided by 4","summary":"A back-of-envelope estimate for English text.","pros":["Free and instant"],"cons":["Wrong for images, files, tool definitions and many languages [[r29]]"],"bestWhen":"You are sizing a project or a rough cost.","avoidWhen":"You are within about 10% of a limit or pricing a long prompt.","meta":[{"k":"Accuracy","v":"low"},{"k":"Cost","v":"none"}]},{"name":"Local tokenizer","summary":"A library such as `tiktoken` run in your process.","pros":["Offline and fast","Good for chunk sizing"],"cons":["Plain text only","Wrong family for Claude models, which use a different tokenizer [[r30]]"],"bestWhen":"You size chunks for an OpenAI-family model at ingest time.","avoidWhen":"Prompts carry images, files or tools, or you call a Claude model.","meta":[{"k":"Accuracy","v":"medium"},{"k":"Cost","v":"CPU"}]},{"name":"Vendor count endpoint","tag":"GA","summary":"OpenAI `POST /v1/responses/input_tokens` returns the exact count. Claude `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens` returns an estimate.","pros":["Counts roles, tools and images on the real model","Pre-flight gate before an expensive call"],"cons":["Extra network call","Claude's endpoint errors on server tools and the MCP connector [[r30]]"],"bestWhen":"You must enforce a budget or choose between trimming and compaction.","avoidWhen":"A cheap local estimate already leaves a wide margin.","meta":[{"k":"Accuracy","v":"high"},{"k":"Cost","v":"one call"}]},{"name":"Read `usage` afterwards","summary":"The billed counts returned with every response.","pros":["Exact","Feeds cost attribution"],"cons":["Too late to prevent a failure"],"bestWhen":"You calibrate estimates and attribute spend.","avoidWhen":"You need to stop an oversized request before it is sent.","meta":[{"k":"Accuracy","v":"exact"},{"k":"Cost","v":"none"}]}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Token failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Empty or cut-off answer with `status: incomplete` and `incomplete_details.reason: max_output_tokens`","cause":"Reasoning tokens used the output cap, possibly before any visible text.","fix":"Reserve at least 25,000 tokens at first, read `reasoning_tokens` in `usage`, then raise the cap or lower effort."},{"symptom":"A prompt that fit on one vendor costs about 30% more tokens or overflows on another","cause":"Tokenizers differ. Claude Opus 4.7 and later give roughly 30% more tokens for the same text, depending on content [[r30]].","fix":"Recount on the target model with its own count endpoint and re-check every budget."},{"symptom":"A 400 error when history grows in Responses","cause":"`truncation` defaults to `disabled`, so overflow fails instead of dropping items [[r4]].","fix":"Trim or compact yourself, or choose `auto` only if silent dropping is acceptable."},{"symptom":"The bill jumps on long prompts while the model and output length are unchanged","cause":"On GPT-6 models a prompt above 272K input tokens reprices the whole request at 2x input and 1.5x output.","fix":"Keep prompts below the step with retrieval or compaction, or accept the step knowingly."},{"symptom":"Azure rejects a prompt that the OpenAI API accepts","cause":"The deployment type sets the window, for example 300,000 on `gpt-4.1` standard against 1,047,576 on the model.","fix":"Read the limit for your deployment type and test at your real prompt length."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-l1-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A request to `gpt-6-sol` sends 300,000 uncached input tokens and receives 4,000 output tokens. List prices are $2 per 1M input and $10 per 1M output tokens. Prompts above 272K input tokens cost 2x on input and 1.5x on output for the whole request. What does the request cost?","answer":1.26,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"USD","hint":"Apply both multipliers to the whole request, not only to the tokens above 272K.","explain":"Input: 0.3M tokens x $2 x 2 = $1.20. Output: 0.004M tokens x $10 x 1.5 = $0.06. Total $1.26. Ignoring the step gives $0.64. Applying it only to the 28,000 tokens above 272K is wrong because the vendor reprices the whole request.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w01-l1-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Users of a reasoning model report empty answers. A failing response shows the fields below. What is the most likely cause?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"status: incomplete\nincomplete_details: { reason: \"max_output_tokens\" }\nmax_output_tokens: 2048\nusage.output_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens: 2048\noutput_text: \"\""},"options":["A safety filter blocked the response before any text was returned","The prompt exceeded the context window and was silently truncated","Reasoning tokens used the whole output cap","A stop sequence matched on the very first generated token"],"answer":2,"explain":"Reasoning tokens count against `max_output_tokens`, and all 2,048 went to thinking, so no visible text was produced. A safety block would show a refusal or filter result, not this reason. An overlong prompt fails with a 400 when truncation is disabled. A stop sequence ends the response normally, not as incomplete.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r3"},{"id":"w01-l1-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every item that counts against the context window of one request.","options":["Tool and function definitions","Reasoning or thinking tokens","HTTP headers and the API key","Images and PDF pages","The billing tier of the account"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Tool definitions, thinking and images or documents all count in the window. Headers and the key are transport details, and the account tier sets rate limits and quota, not the window of a request.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r20"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r3","r4","r9","r13","r18","r20","r28","r29","r30"],"takeaways":["Input, visible output and reasoning tokens share one window, and each side also has its own cap","Count on the model you will call, with the vendor endpoint, before you budget near a limit","A reasoning model can burn the whole output cap and return nothing visible","Prices and windows can step at thresholds such as 272K tokens, and deployment type can lower the window"]},{"id":"w01-l2","order":2,"title":"Turn the right knob","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"Output is too random, too repetitive or too long, and someone says \"set temperature to 0\". Pick the control that acts on the actual failure, then check that your model and API still accept it.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"What each control does and where it exists","level":2,"id":"what-each-control-does-and-where-it-exists"},{"t":"table","caption":"Decoding controls by API (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Control","What it does","Range and notes","Where it exists"],"rows":[["`temperature`","Scales randomness. Lower is more peaked.","OpenAI 0 to 2, change this or `top_p`, not both. Anthropic 0.0 to 1.0, default 1.0 [[r4]] [[r19]].","Chat, Responses, Messages. Newer Claude models accept only 1.0."],["`top_p`","Nucleus sampling: keep the smallest token set whose cumulative probability reaches `p`.","Claude models released after Opus 4.6 accept only 0.99 or higher [[r19]].","Chat, Responses, Messages"],["`top_k`","Sample from the k most likely tokens.","Anthropic deprecated it and returns 400 on models released after Opus 4.6. Gemini calls it `topK` in `generationConfig`.","Not in OpenAI bodies"],["`seed`","Asks for repeatable sampling.","Best effort and Beta in Chat. Watch `system_fingerprint` [[r5]].","Chat only"],["`frequency_penalty`, `presence_penalty`","Penalize tokens by how often or whether they already appeared.","-2.0 to 2.0 [[r5]].","Chat only"],["`logit_bias`","Adds a bias to chosen token ids.","Token id to -100 to 100. Values between -1 and 1 are the usual range [[r5]].","Chat only"],["`logprobs`, `top_logprobs`","Returns log probabilities of generated tokens and top alternatives.","`top_logprobs` 0 to 20. In Chat it needs `logprobs: true`.","Chat and Responses. Not in Messages."],["`stop`, `stop_sequences`","Ends generation when a string appears.","Up to 4 sequences in Chat, not supported on o3 and o4-mini. Claude returns `stop_reason: \"stop_sequence\"` [[r19]].","Chat and Messages. Not in Responses."],["Max tokens","Caps generated tokens, reasoning included.","`max_completion_tokens` (Chat), `max_output_tokens` (Responses), `max_tokens` (Messages, required). Chat `max_tokens` is deprecated and incompatible with o-series.","All three APIs"]],"highlightCol":3},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Temperature 0 is not determinism","md":"Anthropic says temperature 0.0 is not fully deterministic [[r19]]. OpenAI marks `seed` as best effort and Beta, and the Responses body has no `seed` [[r5]] [[r4]]. Expect small variation. Make it harmless with a schema, and test flaky extraction with repeated runs instead of trusting one."},{"t":"h","text":"Pick the knob, then check the model accepts it","level":2,"id":"pick-the-knob-then-check-the-model-accepts-it"},{"t":"dials","id":"w01-l2-knobs","title":"Which knob for which goal","prompt":"Flip the goal, the model path and the API. Watch knobs lose points when the model or API rejects them.","dials":[{"id":"goal","label":"Goal","values":[{"id":"repeat","label":"Repeatable extraction"},{"id":"vary","label":"Varied creative copy"},{"id":"loop","label":"Stop loops or cut at a delimiter"},{"id":"conf","label":"Confidence signal for a classifier"}]},{"id":"path","label":"Model path","values":[{"id":"std","label":"Non-reasoning model"},{"id":"reason","label":"Reasoning, effort not none"}]},{"id":"api","label":"API","values":[{"id":"chat","label":"OpenAI Chat Completions"},{"id":"resp","label":"OpenAI Responses"},{"id":"msg","label":"Anthropic Messages, newer Claude"}]}],"options":[{"id":"temp","name":"Lower or raise temperature or top_p","summary":"Reshape the sampling distribution. Change one, not both."},{"id":"seed","name":"seed plus system_fingerprint","summary":"Ask for repeatable draws and log the backend fingerprint."},{"id":"pen","name":"Frequency and presence penalties","summary":"Push the model away from tokens it already used."},{"id":"bias","name":"logit_bias","summary":"Boost or ban specific token ids."},{"id":"lp","name":"logprobs and top_logprobs","summary":"Read token probabilities as a confidence signal."},{"id":"stop","name":"Stop sequences","summary":"End generation at a chosen string."}],"scores":{"temp":{"goal":{"repeat":{"s":2,"why":"A low temperature or top_p narrows sampling, which is the usual first step for extraction."},"vary":{"s":2,"why":"Raising temperature adds variety. OpenAI allows 0 to 2."},"loop":{"s":0,"why":"Temperature does not target loops directly."},"conf":{"s":-1,"why":"It reshapes the distribution but gives no confidence number."}},"path":{"std":{"s":1,"why":"Accepted on non-reasoning models."},"reason":{"s":-2,"why":"OpenAI says to remove temperature and top_p when effort is not none, and Azure lists them as rejected on most reasoning models."}},"api":{"chat":{"s":1,"why":"Accepted, range 0 to 2."},"resp":{"s":1,"why":"Accepted in the Responses body."},"msg":{"s":-1,"why":"Claude models released after Opus 4.6 accept only temperature 1.0 and top_p of 0.99 or higher."}}},"seed":{"goal":{"repeat":{"s":1,"why":"Same seed and parameters usually match, but it is best effort."},"vary":{"s":-1,"why":"A fixed seed removes variety instead of adding it."},"loop":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"conf":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"path":{"std":{"s":0,"why":"Available on Chat, marked Beta."},"reason":{"s":-1,"why":"Sources do not list it as a reasoning control, so do not rely on it."}},"api":{"chat":{"s":1,"why":"The only OpenAI body with `seed`. Check `system_fingerprint` for backend changes."},"resp":{"s":-2,"why":"The Responses body has no `seed`."},"msg":{"s":-2,"why":"Anthropic has no `seed`, and says even temperature 0.0 is not fully deterministic."}}},"pen":{"goal":{"repeat":{"s":-1,"why":"Penalties change word choice and do not make output repeatable."},"vary":{"s":1,"why":"Presence penalty nudges text toward tokens it has not used."},"loop":{"s":2,"why":"Frequency penalty (-2.0 to 2.0) directly discourages repeated tokens."},"conf":{"s":-1,"why":"They distort the probabilities you want to read."}},"path":{"std":{"s":0,"why":"Accepted on non-reasoning Chat models."},"reason":{"s":-2,"why":"Azure lists both penalties as rejected on reasoning models."}},"api":{"chat":{"s":1,"why":"Chat Completions only, -2.0 to 2.0."},"resp":{"s":-2,"why":"Not in the Responses body."},"msg":{"s":-2,"why":"Not in the Messages body. Anthropic's OpenAI compatibility layer ignores them silently."}}},"bias":{"goal":{"repeat":{"s":0,"why":"It can ban or boost token ids, but needs the tokenizer and is brittle."},"vary":{"s":-1,"why":"It narrows output rather than widening it."},"loop":{"s":1,"why":"It can ban a looping token, but it is a blunt tool."},"conf":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"path":{"std":{"s":0,"why":"Accepted on non-reasoning Chat models."},"reason":{"s":-2,"why":"Azure lists `logit_bias` as rejected on reasoning models."}},"api":{"chat":{"s":1,"why":"Chat only: token id to -100..100."},"resp":{"s":-2,"why":"Not in the Responses body."},"msg":{"s":-2,"why":"Not in the Messages body."}}},"lp":{"goal":{"repeat":{"s":0,"why":"It measures variation, it does not remove it."},"vary":{"s":-1,"why":"It does not add variety."},"loop":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"conf":{"s":2,"why":"Token log probabilities are a common confidence signal. `top_logprobs` returns 0 to 20 alternatives."}},"path":{"std":{"s":1,"why":"Available on non-reasoning models."},"reason":{"s":-2,"why":"OpenAI says to remove `logprobs` and `top_logprobs` when effort is not none."}},"api":{"chat":{"s":1,"why":"`logprobs: true` with `top_logprobs`."},"resp":{"s":1,"why":"`top_logprobs` with the `message.output_text.logprobs` include value."},"msg":{"s":-2,"why":"The Messages body has no logprobs."}}},"stop":{"goal":{"repeat":{"s":1,"why":"A delimiter ends the output cleanly for downstream parsing."},"vary":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"loop":{"s":2,"why":"Ends generation at a delimiter, up to 4 sequences in Chat."},"conf":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"path":{"std":{"s":1,"why":"Accepted on non-reasoning models."},"reason":{"s":-1,"why":"Not supported on o3 and o4-mini. Check each model."}},"api":{"chat":{"s":1,"why":"`stop` takes up to 4 sequences."},"resp":{"s":-2,"why":"No `stop` in the Responses body. Trim in code or use a schema."},"msg":{"s":1,"why":"`stop_sequences` is an array and `stop_reason` becomes `stop_sequence`."}}}},"takeaway":"A knob that fits the goal can still lose because the model path or the API rejects it. Check acceptance before you tune."},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"Chat Completions","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Chat Completions, non-reasoning model","code":"resp = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4.1\",\n    messages=messages,\n    temperature=0,\n    seed=7,\n    stop=[\"\\n###\"],\n    max_completion_tokens=800,\n)\nprint(resp.system_fingerprint)","caption":"Illustrative. Log the fingerprint with every seeded call."}]},{"label":"Responses","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Responses, non-reasoning model","code":"resp = client.responses.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4.1\",\n    input=messages,\n    temperature=0,\n    max_output_tokens=800,\n)","caption":"Illustrative. There is no seed, stop or penalty field in this body."}]},{"label":"Anthropic Messages","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Messages, newer Claude model","code":"msg = client.messages.create(\n    model=\"claude-sonnet-5-5\",\n    max_tokens=800,\n    stop_sequences=[\"\\n###\"],\n    messages=messages,\n)","caption":"Illustrative. No temperature: newer Claude models accept only the default 1.0. `max_tokens` is required."}]}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Control failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"A 400 appears after you turn reasoning on, naming `temperature`, `top_p` or `logprobs`","cause":"OpenAI says to remove temperature, top_p and top_logprobs when effort is not `none`, plus `logprobs` in Chat. Azure's reasoning page lists more as rejected, yet its GPT-6 table marks temperature, top_p and logprobs as supported [[r2]] [[r15]].","fix":"Gate sampling parameters on the effort value in code and test against your real deployment, because the two sources disagree."},{"symptom":"400 after moving a service to a Claude model released after Opus 4.6","cause":"Those models accept only temperature 1.0 and top_p of 0.99 or higher, and `top_k` is rejected.","fix":"Delete the non-default values and steer with the prompt, a schema and evals."},{"symptom":"Settings seem to work but never change the output","cause":"Anthropic's OpenAI SDK compatibility layer silently ignores seed, logprobs, penalties, logit_bias, response_format, reasoning_effort, store and strict [[r36]].","fix":"Use the native API in production. Treat the layer as a testing aid."},{"symptom":"Seeded calls return different text","cause":"`seed` is best effort and the backend can change, which shows up in `system_fingerprint`.","fix":"Log the fingerprint, compare runs with a tolerance and enforce a schema."},{"symptom":"Tuning feels random after changing both `temperature` and `top_p`","cause":"Both reshape the same distribution, so effects compound.","fix":"Change one, hold the other at its default and measure against a fixed eval set."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: what temperature and top_p do to the distribution","blocks":[{"t":"formula","title":"Softmax with temperature","expr":"p_i = exp(z_i / T) / sum_j exp(z_j / T)","vars":[{"k":"z_i","v":"the model's raw score (logit) for token i"},{"k":"T","v":"temperature. Below 1 sharpens the distribution, above 1 flattens it"}],"example":"At T = 0.5 the gap between two logits doubles in the exponent, so the top token dominates. Nucleus sampling then keeps the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches `top_p`. This is the textbook definition. Vendors do not publish their exact samplers."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: logprobs and logit_bias in practice","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Read the probability of the first generated token to build a confidence score for a classifier. Calibrate it against labelled data before you set a threshold. `logit_bias` needs token ids from the right tokenizer, so a ban written for one model can miss in another. Neither works on a reasoning path where OpenAI tells you to remove `logprobs` [[r2]]."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-l2-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A team calls a GPT-6 model with reasoning effort `medium` and also sends `temperature` and `top_p`. What should change?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"resp = client.responses.create(\n    model=\"gpt-6-sol\",\n    input=prompt,\n    reasoning={\"effort\": \"medium\"},\n    temperature=0.2,\n    top_p=0.9,\n)"},"options":["Move `reasoning` out of the body and into the `input` field","Remove `temperature` and `top_p` for this effort level","Raise `top_p` above 1 so the nucleus covers every token","Replace `input` with a messages array of role objects"],"answer":1,"explain":"OpenAI says to remove temperature, top_p and top_logprobs when effort is not none. `reasoning` is a valid Responses field, `top_p` cannot exceed 1, and `input` does not have to be an array of messages.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w01-l2-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every control that exists in the Chat Completions body but not in the Responses body.","options":["`seed`","`stop`","`temperature`","`logit_bias`","`top_logprobs`"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The Responses body has no seed, stop, n, penalties, logit_bias or top_k. `temperature` and `top_logprobs` exist in both.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r4"},{"id":"w01-l2-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each goal to the control that targets it.","pairs":[{"l":"Confidence signal for a classifier","r":"`logprobs` with `top_logprobs`"},{"l":"End output at a delimiter","r":"`stop` or `stop_sequences`"},{"l":"Best-effort repeatable sampling","r":"`seed` plus `system_fingerprint`"},{"l":"Discourage repeated tokens","r":"`frequency_penalty`"}],"explain":"Each control acts on one failure. Temperature changes randomness in general and does not do any of these four jobs directly.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w01-l2-q4","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A service tunes `temperature: 0.2` for extraction on a non-reasoning model, then moves to a Claude model released after Opus 4.6. What is the best response?","options":["Keep `temperature: 0.2` and also add `top_k: 40` for extra control","Set temperature to 0, because that is deterministic on Claude models","Remove the non-default sampling values and use schema and evals","Set `top_p` to 0.5 to compensate for the removed temperature"],"answer":2,"explain":"Those models accept only temperature 1.0 and top_p of 0.99 or higher, and `top_k` is rejected, so each distractor returns a 400. Anthropic also says temperature 0.0 is not fully deterministic.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r19"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r4","r5","r15","r19","r36"],"takeaways":["Name the failure first (randomness, repetition, length, confidence), then pick the control that acts on it","Change temperature or top_p, never both, and treat seed as best effort","Reasoning paths and newer Claude models reject sampling controls that older models accepted","Compatibility layers can ignore your settings silently, so test with the native API"]},{"id":"w01-l3","order":3,"title":"Dial the thinking, then pick the model","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"A harder task gets a better answer when the model thinks longer, and you pay for that thinking in latency and output tokens. Decide how much to think, with which model, and whether a router or your own cascade should choose per request.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"What effort buys and what it costs","level":2,"id":"what-effort-buys-and-what-it-costs"},{"t":"table","caption":"Effort controls by model (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Model or API","Control","Accepted values","Default and notes"],"rows":[["GPT-6 Astra","`reasoning.effort` (Responses) or `reasoning_effort` (Chat)","`low` to `max`","`none` returns HTTP 400 [[r2]]."],["GPT-6.1 Sol","Same","`low` to `max`","Default `medium`. No `none` or `minimal`."],["GPT-6 Sol and Luna","Same","`none` to `max`","Default `medium`."],["`gpt-5.5`","Same","`none`, `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`","Default `medium` [[r3]]."],["`gpt-5.4`","Same","Model dependent","`none` is the default."],["Claude Fable 5.1, Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Haiku 5.5","`output_config.effort`","`low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max`","GA, no beta header. Defaults: high, medium, high, medium in that order [[r18]] [[r31]]."],["Azure `gpt-chat-latest` (Preview)","`reasoning_effort`","Fixed nonzero level","The effort cannot be changed. Microsoft does not recommend preview models in production [[r13]]."]],"highlightCol":3},{"t":"compare","title":"Four ways to buy quality","intro":"They cost different things. Effort spends output tokens and time. A bigger model spends price per token.","options":[{"name":"Small non-reasoning model","summary":"Fast, cheap, and still accepts sampling controls.","pros":["Lowest latency","Sampling and logprobs available"],"cons":["Weak when the task needs several steps"],"bestWhen":"Extraction, classification and rewriting.","avoidWhen":"Planning, math or long tool loops.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"low"},{"k":"Cost per task","v":"low"}]},{"name":"Same model, higher effort","summary":"Raise `reasoning.effort` one level at a time.","pros":["One lever, same prompt and API"],"cons":["Reasoning tokens bill as output and add latency [[r3]]","More effort is not always better"],"bestWhen":"Evals show a measurable gain per step.","avoidWhen":"The latency budget is tight or the task is shallow.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"medium to high"},{"k":"Cost per task","v":"grows with effort"}]},{"name":"Pro mode","tag":"GPT-5.6 and GPT-6","summary":"`reasoning.mode` is `standard` (default) or `pro`, independent of effort [[r3]].","pros":["A second lever beside effort"],"cons":["Limited to the GPT-5.6 and GPT-6 families","Price and latency are not stated here, so measure them"],"bestWhen":"The hardest slice of traffic still fails evals at high effort.","avoidWhen":"You run older models or have no eval set to justify it.","meta":[{"k":"Scope","v":"GPT-5.6, GPT-6"}]},{"name":"Larger model","summary":"Move up a model tier and keep effort modest.","pros":["Higher ceiling on hard tasks"],"cons":["Higher price per token and usually slower"],"bestWhen":"Small models plus effort fail evals on the hard cases.","avoidWhen":"High-volume traffic where most requests are easy.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"medium"},{"k":"Cost per task","v":"high"}]}]},{"t":"h","text":"Pick the model, the effort and who decides","level":2,"id":"pick-the-model-the-effort-and-who-decides"},{"t":"dials","id":"w01-l3-model","title":"Model choice and routing","prompt":"Change the task, the latency budget, the mix of queries and the platform rules. Watch the winner and the reasons move.","dials":[{"id":"difficulty","label":"Task difficulty","values":[{"id":"shallow","label":"Shallow: extract, classify, rewrite"},{"id":"multi","label":"Multi-step reasoning"},{"id":"agentic","label":"Long tool loops"}]},{"id":"latency","label":"Latency budget","values":[{"id":"tight","label":"Tight, a user is waiting"},{"id":"loose","label":"Loose or offline"}]},{"id":"variance","label":"Query mix","values":[{"id":"uniform","label":"Uniform difficulty"},{"id":"mixed","label":"Easy and hard mixed"}]},{"id":"platform","label":"Platform rules","values":[{"id":"open","label":"Global deployment, flexible"},{"id":"strict","label":"Fixed region or very long prompts"}]}],"options":[{"id":"small","name":"Small non-reasoning model","summary":"One fast model, no thinking."},{"id":"lowfx","name":"Mid model, low effort","summary":"A little reasoning on every request."},{"id":"highfx","name":"Large model, high effort or pro","summary":"Think hard on every request."},{"id":"router","name":"Managed model router","summary":"Azure model router picks a model per request."},{"id":"cascade","name":"Own cascade","summary":"Small model first, escalate when a check fails."}],"scores":{"small":{"difficulty":{"shallow":{"s":2,"why":"A small non-reasoning model is fast, cheap and accepts sampling controls."},"multi":{"s":-2,"why":"Multi-step reasoning is where small non-reasoning models fall short. Confirm with evals."},"agentic":{"s":-1,"why":"Errors compound across long tool loops."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":2,"why":"No reasoning tokens, so the first visible token comes sooner."},"loose":{"s":0,"why":"Latency headroom is wasted on a model that cannot use it."}},"variance":{"uniform":{"s":1,"why":"If every query is easy, one small model is simplest."},"mixed":{"s":-1,"why":"The hard queries are underserved."}},"platform":{"open":{"s":0,"why":"Works anywhere."},"strict":{"s":0,"why":"Works anywhere, and you choose the region."}}},"lowfx":{"difficulty":{"shallow":{"s":0,"why":"Reasoning overhead buys little on shallow tasks."},"multi":{"s":1,"why":"Low effort already helps multi-step tasks."},"agentic":{"s":1,"why":"A little reasoning helps tool choice."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":1,"why":"Low effort keeps reasoning tokens small."},"loose":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"variance":{"uniform":{"s":1,"why":"One setting for similar queries is easy to evaluate."},"mixed":{"s":0,"why":"Easy queries overpay and hard ones may under-think."}},"platform":{"open":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"strict":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"highfx":{"difficulty":{"shallow":{"s":-2,"why":"You pay for thinking the task does not need."},"multi":{"s":2,"why":"High effort or pro mode targets hard problems."},"agentic":{"s":2,"why":"More reasoning improves planning across tool calls."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":-2,"why":"Reasoning tokens add latency and bill as output."},"loose":{"s":2,"why":"Offline work can absorb the extra time."}},"variance":{"uniform":{"s":0,"why":"Fine if every query is hard."},"mixed":{"s":-1,"why":"Easy queries pay hard-query prices."}},"platform":{"open":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"strict":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"router":{"difficulty":{"shallow":{"s":1,"why":"Easy requests can go to cheaper models."},"multi":{"s":0,"why":"Depends on which model the router picks."},"agentic":{"s":-1,"why":"Prompt caching helps only when the same model serves consecutive requests, and loops need that."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":0,"why":"Latency depends on the model chosen. Measure it."},"loose":{"s":1,"why":"Cost mode saves money when latency is not the constraint."}},"variance":{"uniform":{"s":-1,"why":"A fixed model is simpler and caches better."},"mixed":{"s":2,"why":"Per-request choice matches the model to the difficulty. Balanced mode targets the cheapest model within about 1% to 2% of top quality."}},"platform":{"open":{"s":1,"why":"Works on Global Standard and Data Zone Standard deployments."},"strict":{"s":-2,"why":"Only Global Standard and Data Zone Standard, and the usable window is the smallest in the pool."}}},"cascade":{"difficulty":{"shallow":{"s":1,"why":"Most requests stop at the small model."},"multi":{"s":0,"why":"Works if you can write a cheap pass check."},"agentic":{"s":-1,"why":"Open-ended tool work is hard to validate step by step."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":-1,"why":"Escalated requests pay for two calls."},"loose":{"s":1,"why":"Extra calls are tolerable offline."}},"variance":{"uniform":{"s":-1,"why":"No gain if every query needs the same model."},"mixed":{"s":2,"why":"Escalate only the hard cases."}},"platform":{"open":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"strict":{"s":1,"why":"You choose the models, so you keep control of region, window and data zone."}}}},"takeaway":"Effort, model size and routing are three separate levers. Start with the smallest setting that passes your evals and raise only where they fail."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w01-l3-cascade","title":"An own cascade","alt":"A request goes to a small model. A check passes the answer through or escalates to a larger model. Both paths end in an answer and the escalation rate is tracked.","src":"flowchart LR\n  R[Request] --> S[Small model]\n  S --> C{Check passes}\n  C -- yes --> A[Answer]\n  C -- no --> L[Larger model]\n  L --> A\n  A --> T[Track escalation rate]","caption":"The check is the product. Use schema validity, a groundedness test or logprobs, and evaluate the cascade as a whole."},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"Effort in code","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"OpenAI Responses","code":"resp = client.responses.create(\n    model=\"gpt-6-sol\",\n    input=prompt,\n    reasoning={\"effort\": \"low\", \"summary\": \"auto\"},\n    max_output_tokens=25000,\n)\nprint(resp.usage.output_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens)","caption":"Illustrative. The 25,000 token cap follows the advice to reserve headroom at first [[r3]]. Summaries come back only if you request `reasoning.summary`."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Anthropic Messages","code":"msg = client.messages.create(\n    model=\"claude-sonnet-5-5\",\n    max_tokens=16000,\n    thinking={\"type\": \"adaptive\"},\n    output_config={\"effort\": \"medium\"},\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n)","caption":"Illustrative. Manual `budget_tokens` is not accepted on the newest models, and `max_tokens` includes thinking [[r31]]."}]},{"label":"Azure model router","blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"Model router facts (checked 2026-10-13) [[r16]]","head":["Topic","Detail"],"rows":[["Versions","`2025-11-18` is active and updated in place, so the routed pool can change without a new version id. `2025-08-07` and `2025-05-19` are frozen."],["Modes","Balanced (default) picks the cheapest model within about 1% to 2% of top quality. Cost allows about 5% to 6%. Quality favours quality. Microsoft gives the percentages as examples."],["Pool","Spans OpenAI (including `gpt-6-astra`), Anthropic, xAI, DeepSeek, Meta and Fireworks models. Claude models must be deployed separately first."],["Subset and failover","Model subset limits the pool and needs at least two models for failover. Failover is on by default."],["Deployment types","Global Standard and Data Zone Standard only."],["Window and caching","The effective window is the smallest in the pool. Prompt caching helps only when the same model serves consecutive requests. Session affinity for Chat Completions is Preview."]]}]},{"label":"Batch and tiers","blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"Pay less for patience (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Option","Terms","Notes"],"rows":[["OpenAI Batch","50% discount. `completion_window` is only `24h`. Up to 50,000 requests and 200 MB per file. Separate rate-limit pool [[r37]].","Not ZDR eligible."],["OpenAI Flex","`service_tier: flex` at Batch rates. Slower and may be unavailable [[r38]].","Beta as of 2026-10-13."],["OpenAI priority or fast","2x price. `ultrafast` is access controlled.","Fast mode for GPT-6 Astra has no latency SLA."],["Azure Batch","24 hour target turnaround at 50% less than global standard, with separate quota [[r13]].","Offline only."],["Anthropic Message Batches","50% off. Up to 100,000 requests or 256 MB. Most finish within 1 hour and expire after 24 hours. Results are kept 29 days [[r39]].","No streaming."]]}]}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Reasoning and routing failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Azure `gpt-5.6-sol` on Chat Completions fails with \"Function tools with reasoning_effort are not supported\" although your code never sets effort","cause":"The default effort is `medium`, and Chat Completions does not support tools with effort other than `none` from GPT-5.4 onward [[r15]].","fix":"Call Responses for tools with reasoning, or set effort `none` where the model allows it."},{"symptom":"HTTP 400 after switching to GPT-6 Astra or GPT-6.1 Sol","cause":"Those models reject `reasoning.effort: none`, and GPT-6.1 Sol also lacks `minimal`.","fix":"Use `low` as the starting point in place of `minimal`, then tune from evals [[r2]]."},{"symptom":"Claude returns no thinking text after an upgrade to Fable 5","cause":"Adaptive thinking is the only mode and `thinking.display` defaults to `omitted` [[r21]].","fix":"Set `thinking.display` to `summarized` when your code reads the thinking."},{"symptom":"Requests to Claude Opus 5.5 fail when code tries to turn thinking off","cause":"Thinking cannot be disabled on Opus 5.5 and returns a 400.","fix":"Lower `output_config.effort` instead of disabling thinking."},{"symptom":"Long prompts fail only some of the time behind a managed router","cause":"The effective window is the smallest in the routing pool, and a large call works only if the router picks a larger-window model.","fix":"Restrict the model subset to models whose window fits your longest prompt."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-l3-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Query difficulty ranges from trivial to hard, latency is moderate, and the team can write a pass or fail check on answers. They need per-request model choice but must keep a fixed regional deployment that the managed router does not support. What fits best?","options":["The largest model at maximum effort for every single request","A small non-reasoning model for every request, with retries","A fine-tuned model that replaces model choice entirely","An own cascade with an escalation check"],"answer":3,"explain":"A cascade gives per-request choice without the managed router, which is limited to Global Standard and Data Zone Standard deployments. The largest model overpays on easy queries, a single small model underserves hard ones, and fine-tuning does not choose a model per request.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16"},{"id":"w01-l3-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After moving to Azure `gpt-5.6-sol`, a Chat Completions call with tools fails with \"Function tools with reasoning_effort are not supported\". The code never sets `reasoning_effort`. Why?","options":["The tool schema holds an unsupported keyword, so `strict` must be removed","Default effort is `medium`, which Chat Completions rejects with tools","The api-version string is too old, so the deployment drops tool support","The deployment type is Standard, and function tools need a provisioned deployment"],"answer":1,"explain":"The default is `medium`, and from GPT-5.4 on Chat Completions does not support tool calling when effort is not none. A bad schema would produce a schema error. The v1 API needs no api-version, and deployment type does not control this rule.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r15"},{"id":"w01-l3-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the steps for tuning reasoning effort in a sensible order.","items":["Build an eval set with pass or fail checks","Run at the lowest accepted effort and record quality, latency and reasoning tokens","Raise effort one level and rerun the same set","Stop when the quality gain no longer justifies the latency and cost, then set the output cap with headroom"],"explain":"Evals come first so every change is measured. Start low because effort costs output tokens and time, raise one step at a time to see the slope, and stop when the gain flattens. Set the cap last, from the measured reasoning tokens plus headroom.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r3"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r3","r13","r15","r16","r18","r21","r31","r37","r38","r39"],"takeaways":["Effort, model size and routing are separate levers with different costs","Effort values differ by model: some reject `none`, some reject `minimal`, and Claude uses `output_config.effort`","A managed router shrinks the usable window to the smallest in the pool and weakens cache reuse","A cascade is only as good as its check, so evaluate the whole system"]},{"id":"w01-l4","order":4,"title":"Make the output parse every time","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"A downstream parser breaks on one malformed response in a thousand. Decide how much structure to demand from the model, and whether the schema belongs on the answer or on a tool call.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Four ways to get structure","level":2,"id":"four-ways-to-get-structure"},{"t":"compare","title":"From a polite request to enforced schemas","intro":"Each step buys more guarantee and more rules to follow.","options":[{"name":"Prompt only","summary":"Ask for JSON in the instructions and parse what comes back.","pros":["Works on any model and API"],"cons":["No guarantee of valid JSON or the right fields"],"bestWhen":"Prototypes and cases where a retry is cheap.","avoidWhen":"A production parser sits behind the call.","meta":[{"k":"Guarantee","v":"none"}]},{"name":"JSON mode","tag":"GA","summary":"`{type: \"json_object\"}` asks for syntactically valid JSON.","pros":["Simple","Wide model support"],"cons":["Only valid JSON is guaranteed, not your schema [[r8]]"],"bestWhen":"You need JSON but the shape is loose or the model lacks schema support.","avoidWhen":"Specific fields and types must be present.","meta":[{"k":"Guarantee","v":"valid JSON"}]},{"name":"Structured outputs","tag":"GA","summary":"`text.format` with `type: \"json_schema\"` and `strict: true` (Chat: `response_format`).","pros":["Schema adherence","Safety refusals are detectable in the response"],"cons":["Schema subset rules","Azure limits are tighter than OpenAI's [[r33]]"],"bestWhen":"The model's final answer must match a schema.","avoidWhen":"Your schema needs features outside the supported subset.","meta":[{"k":"Guarantee","v":"schema"}]},{"name":"Strict function calling","tag":"GA","summary":"Tool arguments follow your schema. The model decides when to call and your code runs the function.","pros":["Schema-valid arguments","Natural fit for actions and lookups"],"cons":["Extra round trip","Reasoning plus tools has API-specific limits"],"bestWhen":"The model should trigger an action or fetch data.","avoidWhen":"You only want a formatted final answer, where structured outputs is simpler.","meta":[{"k":"Guarantee","v":"valid arguments"}]}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Limits and rules by platform (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Topic","OpenAI","Azure OpenAI","Anthropic"],"rows":[["Enforce a schema","`text.format` `{type: \"json_schema\", strict: true, schema}` in Responses, `response_format` in Chat [[r8]].","Same feature with tighter limits [[r33]].","`output_config.format` with `type: \"json_schema\"`, and `strict: true` on tools [[r32]]."],["Schema size","Up to 5,000 object properties and 10 nesting levels. Names and enum values total at most 120,000 characters. At most 1,000 enum values.","Up to 100 object properties and 5 nesting levels.","Not compared here. Read the Claude page."],["Keywords","`pattern`, `format`, `minimum`, `maximum`, `minItems`, `maxItems` supported, but not on fine-tuned models.","`minLength`, `maxLength`, `pattern` and `format` unsupported.","Read the Claude page for limits."],["Shape rules","Every field required (emulate optional with a union with `null`). `additionalProperties: false` is mandatory. Root is an object, not `anyOf`.","Root cannot be `anyOf`.","Not compared here."],["`tool_choice`","`auto` (default), `required`, a named function, `allowed_tools` or `none` [[r9]].","Not compared here.","`auto`, `any`, `tool` or `none`. `any` and `tool` return 400 on Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1, and with manual extended thinking [[r50]]."],["Parallel calls","`parallel_tool_calls: false` gives zero or one call. Strict works with parallel calls on non-fine-tuned models since 2025-05-20 [[r12]].","Docs say structured outputs are not supported with parallel calls, so set `parallel_tool_calls` to `false`.","`disable_parallel_tool_use` defaults to `false`."],["Reasoning plus tools in Chat","From GPT-5.4, Chat does not support tool calling when effort is not `none`. GPT-6 Astra and 6.1 Sol need Responses for tools [[r6]].","`gpt-5.6-sol` fails with \"Function tools with reasoning_effort are not supported\" even when effort is omitted [[r15]].","Not applicable."]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"diagram","id":"w01-l4-toolloop","title":"The function calling loop","alt":"The app sends tool schemas to the model, the model returns a function call, the app runs the tool, returns the output with the call id, and the model answers.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant App\n  participant Model\n  participant Tool\n  App->>Model: Request with tool schemas\n  Model-->>App: function_call with arguments\n  App->>Tool: Run the function\n  Tool-->>App: Result\n  App->>Model: function_call_output with call_id\n  Model-->>App: Final answer","caption":"Your app always runs the function. On GPT-6, `async: true` lets the model keep reasoning while the tool runs, and you return the result with the original `call_id` [[r2]]."},{"t":"h","text":"Same schema, three request shapes","level":2,"id":"same-schema-three-request-shapes"},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"OpenAI Responses","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Structured output in Responses","code":"resp = client.responses.create(\n    model=\"gpt-6-sol\",\n    input=prompt,\n    text={\"format\": {\n        \"type\": \"json_schema\",\n        \"name\": \"ticket\",\n        \"strict\": True,\n        \"schema\": {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"properties\": {\n                \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"billing\", \"bug\", \"other\"]},\n                \"priority\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n            },\n            \"required\": [\"category\", \"priority\"],\n            \"additionalProperties\": False,\n        },\n    }},\n)","caption":"Illustrative. `priority` is optional in spirit, but strict mode needs it required, so it is a union with null."}]},{"label":"Chat Completions","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"The same schema in Chat","code":"resp = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4.1\",\n    messages=messages,\n    response_format={\n        \"type\": \"json_schema\",\n        \"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"ticket\", \"strict\": True, \"schema\": ticket_schema},\n    },\n)","caption":"Illustrative. Responses replaces `response_format` with `text.format` [[r6]]."}]},{"label":"Anthropic Messages","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"The same schema on Claude","code":"msg = client.messages.create(\n    model=\"claude-sonnet-5-5\",\n    max_tokens=1024,\n    messages=messages,\n    output_config={\"format\": {\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"schema\": ticket_schema}},\n)","caption":"Illustrative. Strict tool use is a separate switch: `strict: true` on the tool definition [[r32]]."}]}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Structure failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"The parser fails on a rare malformed response","cause":"Prompt-only and JSON mode do not enforce your schema.","fix":"Move to structured outputs or strict tools, and alert on parse failures so you see the rate."},{"symptom":"A refusal sentence arrives where JSON should be","cause":"A safety refusal replaces the schema output, and OpenAI makes refusals detectable in the response [[r8]].","fix":"Check for a refusal before parsing and route it to a safe fallback."},{"symptom":"A schema that works on OpenAI returns 400 on Azure","cause":"Azure allows 100 properties and 5 nesting levels, and lists `minLength`, `maxLength`, `pattern` and `format` as unsupported.","fix":"Flatten or split the schema, drop the keywords, and test on the Azure deployment."},{"symptom":"The model skips the tool or picks the wrong one","cause":"`tool_choice` is `auto`, so the model may answer directly. On Claude Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1 a forced `any` or `tool` returns 400.","fix":"Use `required` or a named function on OpenAI. On those Claude models use `auto` with strict tools or structured outputs, and improve the tool descriptions."},{"symptom":"Tool arguments are schema-invalid from a fine-tuned model","cause":"A fine-tuned model that makes several calls in one turn loses strict mode [[r9]].","fix":"Validate arguments in your code and retry, or avoid parallel calls with that model."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Sources disagree on strict plus parallel calls","md":"OpenAI's changelog says strict mode works with parallel tool calls on non-fine-tuned models since 2025-05-20 [[r12]]. The Azure page still says structured outputs are not supported with parallel function calls [[r33]]. Follow the page for the platform you deploy on, and test."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-l4-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"This schema is sent to OpenAI with `strict: true`. What is wrong with it?","code":{"lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"category\": { \"type\": \"string\" },\n    \"priority\": { \"type\": \"string\" }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\"category\"]\n}"},"options":["Strict mode needs an array at the root of the schema","`type: \"string\"` is not allowed anywhere in strict mode","Nothing, since optional fields are allowed in strict mode if they are left out of `required`","Fields must all be required, and `additionalProperties` must be `false`"],"answer":3,"explain":"Strict mode needs all fields in `required` and `additionalProperties: false`, and optional values are emulated with a union with null. The root must be an object, not an array, and string types are fine.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r8"},{"id":"w01-l4-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every statement that is true for structured outputs on Azure OpenAI as of 2026-10-13.","options":["It allows up to 100 object properties and 5 nesting levels","`minLength`, `maxLength`, `pattern` and `format` are unsupported","The root can be an `anyOf`","Limits match OpenAI's 5,000 properties and 10 levels","Docs advise `parallel_tool_calls: false` with structured outputs"],"answers":[0,1,4],"explain":"Azure documents 100 properties, 5 levels, those unsupported keywords and the advice to disable parallel calls. The root cannot be `anyOf` on either platform, and the OpenAI limits are far higher than Azure's.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r33"},{"id":"w01-l4-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A service uses Chat Completions with function tools on GPT-5.4 or later at reasoning effort `medium`, and tool calls start failing. Which change is most justified?","options":["Use Responses, or set effort to `none` where allowed","Raise `max_completion_tokens` so the tool call has more room","Switch from strict mode to JSON mode for the arguments","Add a `seed` so that the tool call repeats reliably"],"answer":0,"explain":"From GPT-5.4, Chat Completions does not support tool calling when effort is not none, so the API surface or the effort must change. A larger cap, JSON mode or a seed do not change that rule.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r6"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r6","r8","r9","r12","r15","r32","r33","r50"],"takeaways":["JSON mode guarantees valid JSON only. Structured outputs and strict tools guarantee your schema","Schema limits and keywords differ sharply between OpenAI and Azure, so test on the platform you deploy","Check for refusals before parsing, and validate arguments from fine-tuned models","Reasoning plus tools pushes you to Responses on GPT-5.4 and later"]},{"id":"w01-l5","order":5,"title":"Decide who owns the conversation","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"A new assistant needs conversation memory, tools and a retention policy that legal can sign. The vendors disagree on who holds the state, so decide who owns it before you write the first request.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Three API surfaces","level":2,"id":"three-api-surfaces"},{"t":"compare","title":"Chat Completions, Responses and Anthropic Messages","intro":"OpenAI says Chat Completions stays supported and Responses is recommended for all new projects [[r6]]. The choice still depends on your dials.","options":[{"name":"Chat Completions","tag":"GA","summary":"An array of Messages in, a message out. Your app holds the history.","pros":["Widely mirrored by other vendors, and Azure's v1 API also routes chat completions calls to other providers [[r14]]","ZDR eligible endpoint"],"cons":["No built-in tools","From GPT-5.4 it rejects tools when effort is not `none`; GPT-6 Astra and 6.1 Sol need Responses for tools"],"bestWhen":"You target many OpenAI-compatible vendors, or run non-reasoning models with simple state in your app.","avoidWhen":"You need reasoning with tools, built-in tools or server-held state.","meta":[{"k":"State","v":"your app"},{"k":"Portability","v":"high"}]},{"name":"Responses","tag":"GA","summary":"Typed Items in and out (`message`, `reasoning`, `function_call`, `function_call_output`). `output` can hold several items.","pros":["Server state options","Built-in tools, background mode and encrypted reasoning"],"cons":["OpenAI-specific shape","Stored by default, so retention needs a decision","No `seed`, `stop`, penalties or `logit_bias`"],"bestWhen":"New builds, reasoning with tools, built-in tools or background jobs.","avoidWhen":"You must stay portable across many vendors.","meta":[{"k":"State","v":"either"},{"k":"Portability","v":"medium"}]},{"name":"Anthropic Messages","tag":"GA","summary":"`POST /v1/messages` is stateless. `system` is top level, `max_tokens` is required and content is typed blocks.","pros":["Native adaptive thinking, effort and strict tool use","1M context on current models"],"cons":["You send the full history every call","No stored-response parameter"],"bestWhen":"You build on Claude models and want its native features.","avoidWhen":"You need server-held threads or one code path across vendors.","meta":[{"k":"State","v":"your app"},{"k":"Portability","v":"low"}]}]},{"t":"h","text":"Flip the constraints","level":2,"id":"flip-the-constraints"},{"t":"dials","id":"w01-l5-api","title":"API and state design","prompt":"Change who owns state, the retention rule, the tool needs and the portability goal. Watch the ranking move.","dials":[{"id":"state","label":"State ownership","values":[{"id":"app","label":"My app owns history"},{"id":"server","label":"The server holds history"}]},{"id":"retention","label":"Retention rule","values":[{"id":"normal","label":"Normal retention is fine"},{"id":"zdr","label":"ZDR or no stored content"}]},{"id":"tools","label":"Tool needs","values":[{"id":"plain","label":"Own functions only"},{"id":"builtin","label":"Built-in tools, reasoning with tools or background"}]},{"id":"port","label":"Portability","values":[{"id":"multi","label":"Many vendors"},{"id":"single","label":"One vendor family"}]}],"options":[{"id":"chat","name":"Chat Completions, app history","summary":"Resend Messages each turn."},{"id":"rstate","name":"Responses, stateless","summary":"`store: false` and replay every item."},{"id":"rprev","name":"Responses with previous_response_id","summary":"Chain turns on the server."},{"id":"rconv","name":"Responses with Conversations","summary":"A durable server-side conversation."},{"id":"msgs","name":"Anthropic Messages","summary":"Stateless, full history per call."}],"scores":{"chat":{"state":{"app":{"s":1,"why":"You resend Messages and decide what to trim."},"server":{"s":-2,"why":"There is no conversation object to chain on."}},"retention":{"normal":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"zdr":{"s":1,"why":"`/v1/chat/completions` is ZDR eligible."}},"tools":{"plain":{"s":1,"why":"Function calling works for non-reasoning use."},"builtin":{"s":-2,"why":"No built-in tools, and from GPT-5.4 tools are rejected when effort is not none."}},"port":{"multi":{"s":2,"why":"Many vendors mirror the shape, and Azure's v1 API routes chat completions calls to other providers."},"single":{"s":0,"why":"No portability benefit when you stay in one family."}}},"rstate":{"state":{"app":{"s":2,"why":"You hold the items, encrypted reasoning included, and control trimming and compaction."},"server":{"s":-1,"why":"You rebuild what the server would hold."}},"retention":{"normal":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but you give up server convenience."},"zdr":{"s":2,"why":"ZDR forces `store` to false. Reasoning items carry `encrypted_content` that you replay."}},"tools":{"plain":{"s":1,"why":"Typed `function_call` and `function_call_output` items."},"builtin":{"s":2,"why":"Built-in tools such as `web_search`, `file_search`, `code_interpreter` and `mcp` live in Responses."}},"port":{"multi":{"s":-1,"why":"Items are OpenAI-specific. Azure supports Responses, and Anthropic uses its own shape."},"single":{"s":1,"why":"Fine inside the OpenAI and Azure family."}}},"rprev":{"state":{"app":{"s":-1,"why":"You lose trimming control, and earlier input tokens bill again every turn."},"server":{"s":2,"why":"Least code: send new input and the previous response id."}},"retention":{"normal":{"s":1,"why":"Responses are stored by default and kept 30 days."},"zdr":{"s":-2,"why":"ZDR forces `store` to false, so there is no stored response to chain on (inference)."}},"tools":{"plain":{"s":1,"why":"Works with functions."},"builtin":{"s":2,"why":"Built-in tools and background mode are native to Responses."}},"port":{"multi":{"s":-1,"why":"OpenAI-specific."},"single":{"s":1,"why":"Native to OpenAI and Azure."}}},"rconv":{"state":{"app":{"s":-1,"why":"The server owns the thread."},"server":{"s":2,"why":"A durable conversation object with no 30 day TTL."}},"retention":{"normal":{"s":1,"why":"Durable threads suit audit and long-lived conversations."},"zdr":{"s":-2,"why":"`/v1/conversations` is not ZDR eligible."}},"tools":{"plain":{"s":1,"why":"Works with functions."},"builtin":{"s":2,"why":"Same built-in tools as Responses."}},"port":{"multi":{"s":-2,"why":"An OpenAI-specific object."},"single":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral inside the family."}}},"msgs":{"state":{"app":{"s":1,"why":"Stateless by design. You send the full history every call."},"server":{"s":-2,"why":"The body has no conversation or stored-response parameter."}},"retention":{"normal":{"s":0,"why":"Retention terms are outside this world. Read Anthropic's data terms."},"zdr":{"s":0,"why":"Not compared here. Check the vendor's current terms."}},"tools":{"plain":{"s":1,"why":"`tool_use` and `tool_result` blocks. `tool_choice` rules vary by model."},"builtin":{"s":0,"why":"Server tools exist (web search, web fetch, code execution, tool search, MCP connector) with their own shapes."}},"port":{"multi":{"s":0,"why":"A distinct shape. The OpenAI SDK layer is for testing, not production."},"single":{"s":1,"why":"Native fit for Claude models."}}}},"takeaway":"Retention and state ownership decide more than API fashion. ZDR pushes you toward stateless replay, and durable threads push you toward a conversation object."},{"t":"table","caption":"State options on the OpenAI side (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Option","Who holds history","Retention","Trade-offs"],"rows":[["Replay it yourself","Your app","Yours","Full control of trimming and compaction, and it works with `store: false`. You resend tokens and a reorder can break the cached prefix [[r7]]."],["`previous_response_id`","OpenAI","Responses are kept 30 days","Least code. Earlier input tokens bill again each turn and top-level `instructions` are not carried over [[r7]]."],["Conversations API (released 2025-08-20)","OpenAI","No 30 day TTL on conversations and items","Durable and good for audit. Not ZDR eligible [[r10]]."],["Compaction","Either side","Follows your state choice","Client-side `/responses/compact` (2025-12-11) or server-side `context_management` with `compact_threshold` (2026-02-10). Azure says server-side compaction works with `store=false` [[r34]]."]]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w01-l5-state","title":"Choose where state lives","alt":"A decision tree. If no content may be stored, replay items yourself with store false. Otherwise if threads must outlive 30 days or be audited, use the Conversations API. Otherwise chain with previous_response_id.","src":"flowchart TB\n  Q{Content may be stored}\n  Q -- no --> R[Replay items yourself with store false]\n  Q -- yes --> D{Thread outlives 30 days or needs audit}\n  D -- yes --> C[Conversations API]\n  D -- no --> P[previous_response_id]","caption":"A starting rule. Add a permission, residency and cost check before you commit."},{"t":"failure","title":"State and API failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"The second turn ignores the system prompt","cause":"`previous_response_id` does not carry over top-level `instructions`.","fix":"Send `instructions` on every turn."},{"symptom":"Cost per turn climbs in a long chain","cause":"Earlier input tokens bill again on every chained turn.","fix":"Compact or trim history, and keep a stable prefix so caching can help."},{"symptom":"Code that reads the first output item breaks","cause":"`output` can hold several items, such as reasoning and function calls, so text is not always at index 0.","fix":"Iterate the items and select by type."},{"symptom":"An audit finds content although `store` is false","cause":"Abuse monitoring logs can keep content up to 30 days unless ZDR or Modified Abuse Monitoring applies. Conversations and files are not ZDR eligible [[r10]].","fix":"Agree the retention class first, then choose the API and the state option."},{"symptom":"A stateless reasoning loop loses its earlier thinking","cause":"Encrypted reasoning items were dropped when replaying history.","fix":"Replay every output item, encrypted reasoning included."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: built-in tools by platform","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["OpenAI Responses tools listed for GPT-6 Astra: `web_search`, `file_search`, `image_generation`, `code_interpreter`, `hosted_shell`, `apply_patch`, `skills`, `computer_use`, `mcp` and `tool_search` [[r1]].","Azure: remote MCP needs explicit approval by default, mTLS and Azure service tags are not supported for MCP client traffic, and Code Interpreter has extra charges with up to 1,000 files per container [[r34]].","Anthropic server tools: web search, web fetch, code execution and tool search, plus the MCP connector. The count endpoint rejects server tools and the MCP connector [[r30]]."]}]},{"title":"Go deeper: background mode","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Set `background: true` and poll while the status is `queued` or `in_progress` [[r35]]. ZDR projects run with `store=false`, and data sits on disk for roughly 10 minutes so you can poll. Modified Abuse Monitoring projects keep a background response past polling only if you set `store=true`. On Azure, cancel is idempotent, you resume a stream with `sequence_number`, and combining background with streaming has a known performance issue [[r34]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: Azure v1 API","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Since August 2025 you can opt in to the v1 API with the `OpenAI()` client and a base URL ending `/openai/v1`. A dated `api-version` is no longer required, and preview features use feature headers or an `alpha` path [[r14]]. Azure recommends Responses for Azure OpenAI models, keeps responses 30 days and lets you delete by ID [[r34]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: the Assistants sunset","blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"OpenAI announced deprecation on 2025-08-26 and sunset the API on 2026-08-26 [[r51]]","head":["Assistants concept","Replacement","Caveat"],"rows":[["Threads","Conversations","Conversations are not ZDR eligible."],["Runs","Responses","A run becomes a response with typed Items."],["Assistants","Prompts","Reusable prompts (`v1/prompts`) are themselves deprecated, with shutdown on 2026-11-30 [[r11]]."]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Azure status","md":"Microsoft's classic docs say Azure OpenAI Assistants retire on 2026-08-26 with migration to Foundry Agent Service [[r49]]. I found only pre-date notices, so the post-date Azure status is unverified as of 2026-10-13."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-l5-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A chat service chains turns with `previous_response_id`. Turn 1 follows the system prompt, turn 2 ignores it. The code sets `instructions` only on turn 1. What is the cause?","options":["Responses expire after a single turn and must be recreated","`store` defaulted to false on the second turn","`instructions` are not carried over by `previous_response_id`","The model forgot it because the context window filled up after one turn"],"answer":2,"explain":"Top-level `instructions` are not carried across `previous_response_id`, so send them each turn. Responses are kept 30 days and stored by default, and a full window would not hit on turn 2.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r7"},{"id":"w01-l5-q2","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"A team must run with zero data retention, uses reasoning models with tools, and wants conversations that survive restarts. Which design fits?","options":["Conversations API with `store: true` so threads survive restarts","`previous_response_id` chaining over responses stored on the server","The Assistants API with Threads, which keeps thread state for you","Stateless Responses with your own thread store and item replay"],"answer":3,"explain":"ZDR forces store to false and the Conversations endpoint is not ZDR eligible, so your own store plus replay is the fit. Chaining needs stored responses, and the Assistants API was sunset on 2026-08-26.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r10"},{"id":"w01-l5-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each Assistants API concept to the OpenAI replacement named in the migration mapping.","pairs":[{"l":"Threads","r":"Conversations"},{"l":"Runs","r":"Responses"},{"l":"Assistants","r":"Prompts, which are deprecated too"}],"explain":"Threads map to Conversations and Runs map to Responses. Assistants map to Prompts, but reusable prompts were deprecated on 2026-06-03 with shutdown on 2026-11-30, so treat that mapping as a stopgap and keep configuration in your own code or store.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r51"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r6","r7","r10","r11","r14","r30","r34","r35","r49","r51"],"takeaways":["Decide who owns state and what retention class applies before you pick an API","ZDR forces stateless replay, and the Conversations API is not ZDR eligible","`previous_response_id` is the least code and bills earlier input tokens again each turn","The Assistants API is sunset, and its Prompts replacement is deprecated too"]},{"id":"w01-l6","order":6,"title":"Give the model knowledge it was never trained on","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"The model must answer from information it was never trained on. You can paste it into the prompt, retrieve it per question, preload and cache it, or train it in. Decide with the corpus size, the change rate and the permission model on the table.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"What the evidence says about long inputs","level":2,"id":"what-the-evidence-says-about-long-inputs"},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Context Rot (Chroma, 2025-07-14)","value":"18 LLMs","note":"Performance grows unreliable as input grows, even on simple tasks [[r22]]"},{"label":"RULER","value":"17 models","note":"Only about half of those claiming 32K or more stayed satisfactory at 32K [[r23]]"},{"label":"NoLiMa","value":"11 of 13","note":"Models claiming 128K or more fell below 50% of their short-context baseline at 32K. GPT-4o went from 99.3% to 69.7% [[r41]]"},{"label":"Anthropic rule of thumb","value":"about 200,000 tokens","note":"About 500 pages (an estimate). Below that, the knowledge base can go in the prompt, with prompt caching [[r42]]"},{"label":"Contextual retrieval","value":"49% or 67%","note":"Fewer failed retrievals, or with reranking [[r42]]"}]},{"t":"compare","title":"Four ways to give the model knowledge","intro":"The studies used 2024 to 2025 models. A re-run on 2026 models was not found as of 2026-10-13, so test at your own prompt length.","options":[{"name":"Long context in the prompt","summary":"Paste the documents into every request.","pros":["No retrieval system","Whole-document reasoning is possible"],"cons":["Accuracy drops with length and with position: best at the start and end, worst in the middle [[r40]]","Cost and the 272K price step on GPT-6"],"bestWhen":"The corpus is small, changes rarely and has one audience.","avoidWhen":"The corpus is large, permissions differ by user, or accuracy must hold deep in the prompt.","meta":[{"k":"Update cost","v":"rewrite prompt"},{"k":"Per-user filter","v":"no"}]},{"name":"RAG","summary":"Retrieve the relevant chunks per question.","pros":["Scales to large corpora","Citations and per-user filters","Updates by re-indexing"],"cons":["Retrieval quality becomes your ceiling","Weak for whole-document reasoning"],"bestWhen":"The corpus is large or fast-changing, ACLs apply or citations are required.","avoidWhen":"The corpus is tiny and stable, so the machinery buys nothing.","meta":[{"k":"Update cost","v":"re-index"},{"k":"Per-user filter","v":"yes"}]},{"name":"Cache-augmented generation","summary":"Preload a small corpus into a long context and cache its state, with no retrieval step [[r24]].","pros":["No retrieval latency or retrieval errors","Cheap repeats when the cache hits"],"cons":["A changed corpus invalidates the cache","One shared context cannot be filtered per user"],"bestWhen":"A small, stable corpus serves many questions and latency matters.","avoidWhen":"Content updates often or exceeds the window.","meta":[{"k":"Update cost","v":"rebuild cache"},{"k":"Per-user filter","v":"no"}]},{"name":"Fine-tuning","summary":"Train behaviour into the weights.","pros":["Format, tone, shorter prompts, smaller models and tool-use behaviour [[r48]]"],"cons":["Weak for facts. RAG beat unsupervised fine-tuning [[r43]], and new facts are learned slowly and then raise hallucination [[r44]]"],"bestWhen":"You need a style, a format or a cheaper model.","avoidWhen":"You want to add facts or keep them current.","meta":[{"k":"Update cost","v":"retrain"},{"k":"Per-user filter","v":"no"}]}]},{"t":"h","text":"Flip the dials","level":2,"id":"flip-the-dials"},{"t":"dials","id":"w01-l6-ground","title":"Prompt, RAG, cache or fine-tune","prompt":"Change the corpus, the change rate, the access model and what you need from the model.","dials":[{"id":"corpus","label":"Corpus size","values":[{"id":"small","label":"Small, under about 200K tokens"},{"id":"large","label":"Large, millions of tokens"}]},{"id":"change","label":"Change rate","values":[{"id":"static","label":"Rarely changes"},{"id":"live","label":"Changes daily or faster"}]},{"id":"access","label":"Access model","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One audience sees everything"},{"id":"perdoc","label":"Per-user permissions or citations"}]},{"id":"need","label":"What you need","values":[{"id":"facts","label":"Answers from the documents"},{"id":"behavior","label":"A style, format or smaller model"}]}],"options":[{"id":"longctx","name":"Long context in the prompt","summary":"Paste it all in."},{"id":"rag","name":"RAG","summary":"Retrieve per question."},{"id":"cag","name":"Preload and cache (CAG)","summary":"Cache the corpus state."},{"id":"ft","name":"Fine-tuning","summary":"Train behaviour in."}],"scores":{"longctx":{"corpus":{"small":{"s":2,"why":"Under about 200,000 tokens Anthropic says the knowledge base can go straight into the prompt."},"large":{"s":-2,"why":"Window limits, context rot and the 272K price step on GPT-6."}},"change":{"static":{"s":1,"why":"A stable prompt is easy to keep."},"live":{"s":-1,"why":"Every change rewrites the prompt."}},"access":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"One audience, one prompt."},"perdoc":{"s":-2,"why":"Everything in the prompt is visible to every caller, so you cannot filter per user."}},"need":{"facts":{"s":1,"why":"The model reads the source text directly."},"behavior":{"s":0,"why":"Few-shot examples steer style but cost tokens on every call."}}},"rag":{"corpus":{"small":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but may be more machinery than needed."},"large":{"s":2,"why":"Retrieval scales past any window."}},"change":{"static":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"live":{"s":2,"why":"Re-index changed documents with no retraining."}},"access":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"perdoc":{"s":2,"why":"Per-user filters and citations are native strengths."}},"need":{"facts":{"s":2,"why":"RAG consistently beat unsupervised fine-tuning for knowledge."},"behavior":{"s":-1,"why":"Retrieval does not change style or format."}}},"cag":{"corpus":{"small":{"s":2,"why":"The paper says it suits a limited, manageable knowledge base."},"large":{"s":-2,"why":"The corpus must fit the window."}},"change":{"static":{"s":2,"why":"The cache pays off when content is stable."},"live":{"s":-2,"why":"A changed corpus invalidates the cached state."}},"access":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"One shared cached context for one audience."},"perdoc":{"s":-2,"why":"A shared cached context cannot be filtered per user."}},"need":{"facts":{"s":1,"why":"No retrieval step, so no retrieval misses."},"behavior":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"ft":{"corpus":{"small":{"s":-1,"why":"Little data to learn from, and the prompt can hold it."},"large":{"s":-1,"why":"Evidence says RAG beats fine-tuning for facts at any size."}},"change":{"static":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"live":{"s":-2,"why":"Facts go stale and retraining is slow."}},"access":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"perdoc":{"s":-2,"why":"Whatever is trained in is visible to every caller."}},"need":{"facts":{"s":-2,"why":"New facts are learned slowly and then raise hallucination."},"behavior":{"s":2,"why":"Format, tone, smaller models and tool-use behaviour are what tuning is for."}}}},"takeaway":"Facts belong in retrieval or the prompt. Behaviour belongs in tuning. Per-user permissions rule out any approach that puts the whole corpus into one shared context."},{"t":"table","caption":"Fine-tuning methods (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Method","Use it when","Facts to know"],"rows":[["SFT","You can show the right answer.","OpenAI: minimum 10 examples, gains often at 50 to 100, listed models are the `gpt-4.1` family [[r52]]."],["DPO","You have preferred and rejected pairs.","OpenAI: `beta` between 0 and 2, `gpt-4.1` family, added 2025-06-04 [[r53]]. DPO replaces the RLHF reward model and RL loop with a classification loss [[r46]]."],["RFT","You can write a reliable grader.","OpenAI: `o4-mini` only, launched 2025-05-07 [[r54]]. Azure: `o4-mini`, and `gpt-5` by invitation only [[r17]]."],["LoRA and QLoRA","You self-host open weights.","LoRA freezes weights and trains low-rank matrices: 10,000x fewer trainable parameters and 3x less GPU memory than full Adam fine-tuning of GPT-3 175B, with no added inference latency [[r45]]. QLoRA tunes a 65B model on one 48GB GPU [[r25]]."],["Distillation","You want a small model to copy a large one.","Train on the large model's outputs (Hinton et al., 2015) [[r47]]. OpenAI's pattern: tune a prompt on a large model, then fine-tune a smaller model on its outputs."]]},{"t":"failure","title":"Knowledge failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Answers are right in short tests and wrong deep in a long prompt","cause":"Accuracy depends on length and position, and drops for facts in the middle [[r40]].","fix":"Test at your real length, place key facts early or late, and retrieve less instead of pasting more."},{"symptom":"Answers go stale after the documents change","cause":"A cached or pasted corpus, or facts trained into weights, no longer matches the source.","fix":"Use RAG with re-indexing, or rebuild the cache on every change."},{"symptom":"A fine-tuned model states wrong facts with confidence","cause":"Training on new facts raises hallucination once the facts are learned [[r44]].","fix":"Move facts to retrieval and tune for format and behaviour only."},{"symptom":"A user sees content they should not","cause":"The whole corpus sits in one shared prompt, cache or set of weights.","fix":"Filter per user with retrieval and ACLs (see w07), or separate contexts per tenant."},{"symptom":"A planned OpenAI fine-tuning project cannot start","cause":"OpenAI is winding down self-serve fine-tuning. Orgs that never ran it could not create jobs from 2026-05-07 [[r11]].","fix":"Check eligibility first. Consider Azure managed fine-tuning or LoRA on open weights."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: fine-tuning platform status on 2026-10-13","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["OpenAI notice 2026-05-07. From that date orgs that never ran fine-tuning cannot create jobs. From 2026-07-02 orgs with no fine-tuned inference in 60 days cannot. On 2027-01-06 active customers can no longer create jobs. Fine-tuned inference ends only when the base model is deprecated, and several `ft-` models end 2026-10-23 [[r11]].","The GPT-6 Astra page marks the fine-tuning endpoint unsupported [[r1]].","Azure keeps managed SFT, DPO and RFT. Interactive training is Preview and needs approval. Training types are Standard, Data zone (US only), Global and Developer (preemptible, no SLA). Managed compute with LoRA adapters is Preview. Training on the `gpt-4.1` family ends no earlier than 2027-04-14 for existing customers [[r17]]."]}]},{"title":"Go deeper: why CAG still needs prompt caching","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"CAG is the idea. Prompt caching is one way to pay for it. Provider caches expire and key on exact prefixes, so a stable ordering and a refresh plan matter. World 2 covers cache keys, time to live and what breaks hit rates. Study material on tuning: [[r27]]. On tokenizers: [[r28]]. Azure path: [[r26]]."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-l6-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A cache-augmented setup serves a 150,000-token manual to one audience. Then documents become visible per user and updates arrive daily. What does the board flip to?","options":["A larger context window with the same cache","RAG with per-user filters and re-indexing","Fine-tuning on the manual","The same setup with a shorter cache lifetime"],"answer":1,"explain":"A shared cached context cannot be filtered per user, and daily updates invalidate it. RAG filters per user and re-indexes changes. A bigger window or shorter lifetime keeps the shared context, and fine-tuning is weak for current facts and exposes trained content to everyone.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r24"},{"id":"w01-l6-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A 200,000-token prompt is sent 1,000 times a day to `gpt-6-sol` at $2 per 1M input tokens, with no caching (below the 272K step). A RAG pipeline would send 6,000 input tokens per query instead. How many more dollars per day does the long prompt cost in input tokens?","answer":388,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"USD per day","hint":"Compute each side in millions of tokens, then subtract.","explain":"Long prompt: 200,000 x 1,000 = 200M tokens x $2 = $400. RAG: 6,000 x 1,000 = 6M tokens x $2 = $12. The difference is $388. Prompt caching changes this: reads at $0.2 per 1M would put the long prompt near $40 if every call hit the cache, which is why CAG can compete for small stable corpora.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w01-l6-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Select the evidence-backed reasons not to fine-tune a model in order to add facts.","options":["RAG beat unsupervised fine-tuning for old and new knowledge","Models learn new facts slowly and then hallucinate more","Fine-tuning always adds inference latency","Azure says fine-tuning does not replace retrieval for current information","Fine-tuned models cannot call tools"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Ovadia et al., Gekhman et al. and Azure's guidance all point away from fine-tuning for facts. LoRA adds no inference latency, and fine-tuned models can call tools, with strict mode lost only for fine-tuned models that make several calls in one turn.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r43"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r11","r17","r22","r23","r24","r25","r26","r27","r28","r40","r41","r42","r43","r44","r45","r46","r47","r48","r52","r53","r54"],"takeaways":["Long inputs degrade with length and position, so a big window is not a big usable window","Facts belong in the prompt or retrieval, behaviour belongs in tuning","Per-user permissions rule out shared contexts, caches and trained-in knowledge","OpenAI self-serve fine-tuning is winding down, so check platform eligibility before you plan on it"]},{"id":"w01-l7","order":7,"title":"Boss: choose the API and model strategy","kind":"boss","minutes":20,"brief":"A team is about to build a new assistant and asks you to pick the API and the model strategy. The dials are not given. Ask for them, offer options and defend a choice.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Map every dial to a decision","level":2,"id":"map-every-dial-to-a-decision"},{"t":"scenario","title":"The interviewer's setup","known":["A new text assistant that answers questions and may call tools","The team wants one recommendation with reasons, not a survey","Nothing is fixed yet: vendor, retention rule, corpus, traffic or budget"],"ask":["Which platforms are allowed, and do residency or zero data retention rules apply?","Does it call tools, and does it need reasoning with those tools?","How big and how fast-changing is the knowledge, and who may see which part?","How hard and how mixed are the queries, and what is the latency budget per turn?","Who consumes the output: a person or a parser, and is repeatability required?"],"tension":"A single answer needs dials you do not have yet. The skill is to ask for them in the right order and say what would change your pick."},{"t":"table","caption":"Dial to decision map for this world","head":["Dial","Decision it moves","Where you met it"],"rows":[["Prompt length and tool count","Token budget, price steps, whether to compact or retrieve","Level 1"],["Repeatability and consumer of the output","Sampling controls, schema enforcement","Levels 2 and 4"],["Query difficulty mix and latency budget","Effort, model size, router or cascade, batch or Flex","Level 3"],["Retention rule, tools with reasoning, portability","API surface and who owns state","Levels 4 and 5"],["Corpus size, change rate, permission model","Long context, RAG, cache-augmented generation or tuning","Level 6"],["Model lifecycle","Pin versions, keep evals, plan replacements before retirement dates","Levels 3 and 5"]],"highlightCol":1}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-l7-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the decisions for an API and model strategy in a sensible dependency order.","items":["Fix retention, residency and vendor constraints","Choose the API surface and who owns state","Choose the knowledge approach: prompt, RAG, cache or tuning","Pick model, effort and routing against an eval set","Set output structure and sampling controls, then roll out"],"explain":"Constraints such as ZDR decide which APIs and state options are even allowed. Knowledge strategy decides prompt size and cost, which feeds model and effort choice. Output structure and sampling depend on the model you chose, and rollout comes last.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r10"},{"id":"w01-l7-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"A service upgrades to a newer Claude model and starts returning 400 for the same payload. Select every field that can cause the error.","code":{"lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"model\": \"claude-sonnet-5-5\",\n  \"max_tokens\": 4096,\n  \"temperature\": 0.3,\n  \"top_k\": 40,\n  \"thinking\": { \"type\": \"enabled\", \"budget_tokens\": 2000 },\n  \"stop_sequences\": [\"END\"],\n  \"messages\": [{ \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"...\" }]\n}"},"options":["`temperature: 0.3`","`top_k: 40`","`thinking` with `budget_tokens`","`max_tokens: 4096`","`stop_sequences`"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Models released after Opus 4.6 accept only temperature 1.0 and top_p of 0.99 or higher, reject `top_k`, and no longer accept manual `budget_tokens`. `max_tokens` is required and valid, and `stop_sequences` is supported.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r19"},{"id":"w01-l7-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Query difficulty is mixed, a global Azure deployment is acceptable, prompts stay under 100K tokens, and the team wants the least operational work with automatic failover. Which strategy fits?","options":["Azure model router in Balanced mode","A hand-built cascade with its own validation service","The largest model at maximum effort for every request","A fine-tuned small model used for all traffic"],"answer":0,"explain":"The managed router chooses per request, fails over by default and runs on Global Standard. A cascade adds work you must evaluate yourself, the largest model overpays on easy queries, and a fine-tuned small model does not choose per request.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16"},{"id":"w01-l7-q4","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"After launch, a customer contract requires zero data retention. The assistant used the Conversations API for threads. What must change?","options":["Hold threads yourself, set `store` to false and replay all items","Nothing, because ZDR only affects how logs are written","Switch the threads to the Assistants API, which stores state for you","Chain turns with `previous_response_id` on stored responses instead"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Conversations endpoint is not ZDR eligible and ZDR forces store to false, so you hold state yourself and replay items. Chaining needs stored responses, and the Assistants API was sunset on 2026-08-26.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r10"}],"defend":{"prompt":"A team is building a new assistant and asks you to choose the API and the model strategy. Walk me through how you would decide.","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["Which platforms and vendors are allowed, and do residency or zero data retention rules apply?","Does the assistant call tools, and does it need reasoning together with tools?","How large and how fast-changing is the knowledge, and who may see which part?","How hard and how mixed are the queries, and what is the latency budget per turn?","Who consumes the output, a person or a parser, and is repeatability required?","What are the expected volume and the budget per task?"],"options":[{"name":"Responses with server state","when":"New build, reasoning with tools, built-in tools, background jobs, and normal retention.","tradeoff":"Least code, but earlier input tokens bill again on chained turns and ZDR rules it out."},{"name":"Responses, stateless replay","when":"ZDR or strict retention, or you want full control over trimming and compaction.","tradeoff":"You own the history store and replay every item, encrypted reasoning included."},{"name":"Chat Completions with app-held history","when":"Many OpenAI-compatible vendors, non-reasoning models, simple state.","tradeoff":"No built-in tools, and tools with reasoning are rejected on GPT-5.4 and later."},{"name":"Anthropic Messages","when":"Claude models, adaptive thinking, 1M context, strict tool use.","tradeoff":"Stateless and a different shape, so a second code path if you also run OpenAI models."},{"name":"Router or cascade on top","when":"Query difficulty is mixed and a quality check exists.","tradeoff":"A router shrinks the window to the smallest in the pool and weakens caching. A cascade needs a trustworthy check."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** I would ask which platforms are allowed and whether residency or zero data retention applies, whether the assistant calls tools and needs reasoning with them, how big and how fast-changing the knowledge is and who may see which part, how hard and how mixed the queries are, what consumes the output, and the budget per task."},{"t":"p","md":"**2. List the options, including the boring one.** The boring option is one mid-size model behind Chat Completions with history in the app. The alternatives are below."},{"t":"table","head":["Choice","Wins when","Loses when"],"rows":[["Chat Completions, app history","Portability across vendors, non-reasoning models","Tools with reasoning on GPT-5.4 and later, built-in tools"],["Responses, server state","New builds, reasoning with tools, durable threads","ZDR, cross-vendor portability"],["Responses, stateless","ZDR, control of trimming","You carry the plumbing and resend tokens"],["Knowledge: prompt or CAG","Small, stable, one audience","Large corpus or per-user permissions"],["Knowledge: RAG","Large or changing corpus, ACLs, citations","Tiny stable corpus"],["Model: router or cascade","Mixed difficulty","Uniform queries, fixed region, long prompts"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Pick for the stated dials, and say what flips it.** With no more information I would assume Azure or OpenAI, normal retention, tools with reasoning, a corpus in the millions of tokens with per-user access, and mixed query difficulty. I would choose Responses with `previous_response_id` or Conversations for state, RAG with per-user filters for knowledge, a mid model at low effort, structured outputs for the consumer, and a cascade for the hard slice. Fine-tuning would only be for format or tool behaviour, never to teach facts."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["ZDR required: stateless Responses with replay, no Conversations API.","Corpus under about 200,000 tokens, stable, one audience: put it in the prompt with prompt caching.","Pure extraction: a small non-reasoning model with low temperature and a strict schema.","Many vendors must work: Chat Completions with app-held history.","Fixed region or very long prompts: skip the managed router."]},{"t":"p","md":"**4. Name failure modes and how I would detect them.** Reasoning tokens eat the output cap, shown as `status: incomplete` and a rising share of empty answers. A model upgrade rejects sampling values or budgets, shown as 400s by parameter, so I pin versions and run contract tests. Prompts cross 272K tokens on GPT-6 models, shown in the p99 of input tokens and a cost jump. Schema refusals reach the parser, shown in a refusal rate. A router picks a small-window model, shown as intermittent long-prompt failures."},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Name metrics and the rollout.** I would build an eval set with pass or fail checks first, then track quality per task, p95 latency, tokens per task including reasoning, cost per task, schema failure rate and cascade escalation rate. Start at the lowest effort that passes. Roll out in shadow, then canary, and watch retirement dates so replacements are tested before the old model goes."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about retention, tools with reasoning, knowledge size and permissions before naming an API","w":3},{"pt":"Compares at least two API surfaces and says who owns state in each","w":2},{"pt":"Chooses the knowledge approach from corpus size, change rate and permissions, and rejects fine-tuning for facts","w":2},{"pt":"Treats effort, model size and routing as separate levers and starts low against evals","w":2},{"pt":"Names concrete failure modes with detection signals, such as incomplete status, parameter 400s and token price steps","w":2},{"pt":"States explicit flip conditions, such as ZDR, tiny stable corpus or multi-vendor need","w":3},{"pt":"Names metrics, a rollout plan and model lifecycle handling","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"Legal requires zero data retention after launch. What changes?","a":"Move to stateless Responses: `store` false, your own thread store, replay every output item with encrypted reasoning. Drop the Conversations API, which is not ZDR eligible, and do not rely on response chaining. Re-check every endpoint you use, because batches and files are not eligible either."},{"q":"After you raised reasoning effort, p95 latency doubled and some answers are empty. What do you check?","a":"Compare `reasoning_tokens` with the output cap and look for `status: incomplete`. Lower effort, raise the cap with headroom, and consider a cascade so only hard requests pay for deep reasoning. Verify the effort default, because omitting it can mean `medium`."},{"q":"The model you chose retires in six months. What is your plan?","a":"OpenAI gives at least 6 months of notice for GA models [[r11]] and Anthropic at least 60 days [[r55]], and Azure sets its own dates. Pin versions, keep an eval set, test the replacement early, and check for changed parameters or tokenizer behaviour before the cutover."}],"redFlags":["Names a model or an API before asking about retention, tools and permissions","Says temperature 0 gives determinism, or relies on `seed` as a guarantee","Proposes fine-tuning to teach the model company documents","Treats the context window as fully usable and ignores output and reasoning tokens","Treats Chat Completions and Responses as the same API under a new name"]},"refs":["r10","r11","r16","r19","r29","r55"],"takeaways":["Ask for the dials in the order they constrain each other: retention first, then state, knowledge, model and output","Offer the boring option, then show the dial that flips to each alternative","Name failure modes with a detection signal, not only a fix","End with metrics, a rollout plan and a lifecycle plan"]}],"topics":[{"id":"w01-t01","order":1,"group":"Model basics","title":"Tokens and tokenization","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Models read and bill in tokens, which are pieces of text produced by a tokenizer. Counts differ by model family, language, content type and tool definitions.","useIf":"You budget cost, context or latency, migrate between model families, or debug a prompt that is longer than it looks.","avoidIf":"You only need a rough idea and the margin of error does not matter. Even then, never reuse a count from another model family.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **token** is a unit the model reads and writes. A tokenizer, usually based on byte-pair encoding, splits text into pieces learned from a training corpus [[r28]]. Common words become one token. Rare words, numbers, code and many non-English scripts split into more pieces, so the same meaning can cost more tokens in another language. Treat that as something to measure, not to assume."},{"t":"p","md":"Counts are **not portable**. Claude Opus 4.7 and later use a newer tokenizer that produces roughly 30% more tokens for the same text, and the exact rise depends on the content [[r30]]. On that tokenizer 1M tokens is about 555,000 words or 2.5M Unicode characters, against about 750,000 words before (estimates). Input, output and reasoning tokens all bill, and tool definitions, images and documents count too [[r20]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Ways to count tokens","head":["Method","Exact?","Covers images, files, tools?","Watch for"],"rows":[["OpenAI `POST /v1/responses/input_tokens`","Exact, includes role and boundary tokens","Yes, takes Responses-style input","Counts for the model you name only [[r29]]"],["Anthropic `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens`","Estimate, can differ slightly","Yes, but errors for server tools and the MCP connector","May include system-added tokens that are not billed [[r30]]"],["Local `tiktoken`","Exact for its encodings","Plain text only","`o200k_base` and `cl100k_base` exist. Which one GPT-5 and GPT-6 use is unverified [[tr4]]"],["Characters divided by 4","No","No","Wrong for images, files and tools"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Tool definitions are input tokens","md":"OpenAI injects function definitions into the system message, so they count against context and bill as input. The function-calling guide suggests fewer than 20 functions at the start of a turn (soft advice) and `tool_search` with `defer_loading` for large catalogs on gpt-5.4 and later [[r9]]."}],"whereToUse":["Cost forecasts: multiply counted tokens by the price per million tokens, input and output separately","Context budgeting before you assemble retrieved chunks, history and tool definitions","Migrations between model families, where the tokenizer changes the budget","Rate-limit planning, because limits are usually expressed in tokens per minute","Debugging unexpectedly long prompts, such as a pasted table or an image-heavy file"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You must predict cost or fit a window and need an exact count","Your workload mixes languages, code or numbers","You pass tools, images or PDFs, which a text counter ignores","You switch vendors or model generations"],"avoid":["Using a local tokenizer count for a vendor that does not publish that tokenizer","Counting once and reusing the number after the prompt template changes","Spending hours on tokenizer detail when a 10% error does not change the decision"],"flips":["The workload is image or file heavy: use the vendor's counting endpoint, not a text tokenizer","A prompt must fit a hard window: count on the target model with production-shaped payloads","Volume is small and cost is tiny: an estimate is enough"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Choosing a counting method","head":["Method","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Vendor counting endpoint","You need the exact figure for tools, images and files","You count millions of items, since each count is a network call","Latency, rate limits, and Anthropic server tools are rejected"],["Local tokenizer library","Plain text at volume, offline","The model is not covered or content is not text","Silent error for other vendors"],["Read `usage` from real responses","You want billed truth after the fact","You must know before you send","Only available after the spend"],["Characters divided by 4","A back-of-envelope sizing","Any decision with a margin under 30%","Misleads for non-English text, code, images and tools"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A prompt that fit on one vendor overflows after migration","cause":"The new tokenizer produces more tokens for the same text","fix":"Recount real prompts on the target model and leave headroom, about 30% for the Claude change"},{"symptom":"Cost forecast is off by a wide margin","cause":"The estimate ignored tool definitions, system text and reasoning tokens","fix":"Count full requests, then compare with the `usage` field from sampled production calls"},{"symptom":"Counting a request with a server tool fails on Anthropic","cause":"`count_tokens` returns `invalid_request_error` for web search, web fetch, code execution and tool search","fix":"Count the request without those tools, then add an allowance based on observed usage"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t01-s1","title":"Migrating a long prompt to a newer Claude model","situation":"A prompt of 780,000 tokens on an earlier Claude model must run on Claude Opus 5.5, which has a 1M window. Quality tests pass.","constraints":["Window on the target is 1M tokens","Prompts vary in content","A production release is two weeks away"],"unknowns":["How much do real prompts grow on the new tokenizer?","Is the long-context price step applicable on the target?"],"question":"How do you confirm it fits?","options":[{"name":"Recount a sample of real prompts on the target with its counting endpoint and size headroom from the worst case","verdict":"best","why":"The newer tokenizer can add roughly 30% and the rise depends on content, so only target-model counts settle it [[r30]].","tradeoff":"Costs a batch of count calls and some engineering time."},{"name":"Assume it fits because the window is larger","verdict":"poor","why":"A 30% rise on 780,000 tokens is about 1,014,000, which already exceeds 1M.","tradeoff":"Fine only when prompts are far below the window and growth cannot reach it."},{"name":"Count locally with `tiktoken` and add 10%","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and offline.","tradeoff":"`tiktoken` is not the Claude tokenizer, so the number is only a rough proxy."}],"flips":[{"if":"Prompts are at most 300,000 tokens","then":"A 30% rise still fits with room to spare, so an estimate is enough."},{"if":"Prompts contain images or PDFs","then":"Use the counting endpoint, since local text counters ignore them."}],"takeaway":"Count on the model you will call, with production-shaped input."},{"id":"w01-t01-s2","title":"Forecasting a nightly summarisation job","situation":"A batch job will summarise two million documents a night. Finance wants a monthly estimate in a week. Documents are plain text in three languages.","constraints":["Plain text only","High volume","Three languages","Estimate needed within a week"],"unknowns":["Token counts per language for the chosen model","Output length target"],"question":"How do you build the estimate?","options":[{"name":"Characters divided by 4 for all three languages","verdict":"poor","why":"Languages and scripts split into different numbers of tokens, so one ratio misstates at least two of them.","tradeoff":"Acceptable as a first sketch before any sample exists."},{"name":"Sample documents per language, count with a local tokenizer that matches the model, extrapolate with a margin, then check against `usage` in a pilot","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap at volume and language aware.","tradeoff":"Only as accurate as the match between the tokenizer and the model."},{"name":"Sample per language, count with the vendor endpoint for the target model, extrapolate, then verify with a one-night pilot","verdict":"best","why":"Per-language samples capture the language effect and the vendor endpoint matches billing.","tradeoff":"More calls and a pilot to run before the final number."}],"flips":[{"if":"Several model families are candidates","then":"Repeat the sample count per model, since tokenizers differ."},{"if":"Volume is small","then":"Use a single estimate and revisit after real usage appears."}],"takeaway":"Sample by language, count on the target, confirm with a pilot."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t01-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A prompt is 600,000 tokens on an earlier Claude tokenizer. The newer tokenizer yields about 30% more tokens for the same text. How many tokens should you plan for?","answer":780000,"tolerancePct":3,"unit":"tokens","explain":"600,000 x 1.30 = 780,000 tokens. The 30% is a vendor estimate that varies with content, so size headroom above this figure [[r30]]."},{"id":"w01-t01-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which statement about counting tokens for a request that includes images and tool definitions is correct?","options":["A local text tokenizer gives the exact count","Characters divided by 4 is accurate enough","The vendor's counting endpoint for the target model is the reliable source","Images do not count toward context"],"answer":2,"explain":"Images, files and tool definitions count, and a text-only tokenizer ignores them. Characters divided by 4 is wrong for them as well."},{"id":"w01-t01-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"A prompt is longer than expected. Which are likely causes?","options":["Tool definitions were added to the request","A non-English or code-heavy section tokenizes into more pieces","Temperature was raised","A newer tokenizer produces more tokens for the same text"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Tool definitions, script and content type, and a tokenizer change all raise counts. Temperature affects sampling, not input length."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why can't you reuse a token count across vendors?","back":"Each model family has its own tokenizer. Claude Opus 4.7 and later yield about 30% more tokens than earlier Claude models for the same text."},{"front":"Which OpenAI endpoint returns an exact input count?","back":"`POST /v1/responses/input_tokens`, which takes Responses-style input and includes role and boundary tokens."},{"front":"What do tool definitions do to a request?","back":"They are injected as input text, count against context and bill as input tokens."}],"related":["w01-t02","w01-t09","w01-l1","w02-t11","w16-t01"],"refs":["r28","r29","r30","r20","r9","tr4"]},{"id":"w01-t02","order":2,"group":"Model basics","title":"Context windows and output limits","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"The context window is one shared budget for input, reasoning and output. A separate cap limits output, and each API names its cap differently.","useIf":"You size prompts, set a max token parameter, or hit truncation, empty answers or 400 errors.","avoidIf":"You think a bigger advertised window means the model uses all of it equally well. See the long-context topic for accuracy.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The **context window** is the most tokens a request can hold: system text, history, tool definitions, retrieved content, images, reasoning and the answer. Input and output also have **separate caps** that you cannot always use together. A model can list a 1,050,000 token window, 922,000 max input and 128,000 max output, and output counts inside the window [[r1]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"The shared budget","expr":"input + reasoning + visible output <= window, and visible output + reasoning <= max output","vars":[{"k":"input","v":"Everything you send, including tool definitions and images"},{"k":"reasoning","v":"Hidden thinking tokens on reasoning models, billed as output"},{"k":"max output","v":"Ceiling set by the model and by your `max_output_tokens` or `max_tokens`"}],"example":"A request with 900,000 input tokens on a 1,050,000 window leaves at most 150,000 tokens, but the model's own output cap of 128,000 still applies. A ceiling does not reserve tokens [[r13]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Plan the budget in three steps. Reserve the output first (your cap plus reasoning headroom), subtract fixed overhead such as the system prompt and tool definitions, and give what remains to retrieved content and history. Count tokens on the target model, because a window is measured in that model's tokens, and re-check when the model or deployment type changes."},{"t":"table","caption":"Windows on the check date 2026-10-13","head":["Model or deployment","Window","Max input","Max output"],"compact":true,"rows":[["OpenAI `gpt-6-astra`, `gpt-6.1-sol`, `gpt-6-sol`, `gpt-6-luna`","1,050,000","922,000","128,000"],["Azure `gpt-5.4-mini`, `gpt-5.2`, `gpt-5`","400,000","272,000","128,000"],["Azure `gpt-4.1`, standard deployment","300,000 allowed (model supports 1,047,576)","300,000 window","32,768"],["Azure `gpt-4.1`, provisioned or batch","128,000 allowed","128,000 window","32,768"],["Claude Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Haiku 5.5","1M","within the window","128K sync, 300K on batches with a beta header"]]},{"t":"table","caption":"The output cap has a different name per API","head":["API","Parameter","Notes"],"compact":true,"rows":[["OpenAI Responses","`max_output_tokens`","Includes reasoning tokens. Hitting it gives `status: incomplete` with `incomplete_details.reason: max_output_tokens` [[r3]]"],["OpenAI Chat Completions","`max_completion_tokens`","`max_tokens` is deprecated and not compatible with o-series [[r5]]"],["Anthropic Messages","`max_tokens` (required)","Includes thinking tokens [[r19]]"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Long prompts can change the price","md":"On the GPT-6 pages, a prompt over 272,000 input tokens costs 2x the input and cache rates and 1.5x the output rate for the whole request [[r1]]. Claude Haiku 5.5 charges more above 100,000 prompt tokens, while other 1M Claude models use standard pricing across the window [[r20]]."}],"whereToUse":["Sizing the retrieval budget: how many chunks fit after instructions, history and tools","Setting `max_output_tokens` so reasoning models can finish thinking and still answer","Choosing a deployment type on Azure, because the allowed window depends on the deployment","Diagnosing truncated or empty answers and 400 errors on overflow","Estimating cost steps for long prompts"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You build prompts from several sources and must cap each part","You use a reasoning model, where hidden tokens eat the output budget","Prompts approach the window on a given platform"],"avoid":["Setting a very large output cap by habit: on Azure the TPM check counts `max_tokens` in full [[tr7]]","Filling the window because it exists: accuracy and cost both worsen with length","Copying a window figure from a model page without checking your deployment type"],"flips":["Reasoning effort is above `none`: reserve at least 25,000 tokens for reasoning and output when starting out [[r3]]","Prompts exceed the deployment window: change deployment or model, or retrieve instead of stuffing","Latency matters: a lower output cap also shortens the worst case"]},"alternatives":{"title":"What to do when content is bigger than the budget","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Bigger window or model","The task needs all content at once","Content grows without bound","Long-prompt price step, slower first token"],["Retrieval (RAG)","Only part of the content matters per question","The task spans the whole document","Retrieval quality and ingestion work"],["Summarise or compact history","A chat grows turn by turn","Exact earlier wording matters","Lossy, and compaction can drop details [[r12]]"],["Truncate the oldest content","Old turns hold little value","Early instructions or facts matter","Silent loss of content you may need"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A reasoning model returns no visible text and `status: incomplete`","cause":"Hidden reasoning used the whole `max_output_tokens` before any text was written","fix":"Raise the cap, reserve about 25,000 tokens at first, and read `incomplete_details.reason`"},{"symptom":"HTTP 400 when the prompt grows","cause":"Responses defaults to `truncation: disabled`, which fails on overflow instead of trimming","fix":"Count before sending, trim deliberately, or set `truncation` to `auto` knowing it drops content"},{"symptom":"429 responses while metrics show spare quota","cause":"The Azure TPM check counts `max_tokens` in full, so a 4,000 cap uses 4,000 tokens of limit even for a 200 token answer","fix":"Set the cap near the real need and keep headroom for bursts"},{"symptom":"A 330,000 token prompt fails on a model that lists a 1M window","cause":"The deployment type sets a lower limit, for example 300,000 on `gpt-4.1` standard","fix":"Check the deployment limit on the platform page, not only the model card"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t02-s1","title":"Empty answers from a reasoning model","situation":"A support tool on a reasoning model sometimes shows a blank answer. Logs show `status: incomplete`. The team capped output at 1,500 tokens to control cost.","constraints":["Reasoning effort is `medium`","Answers are about 300 tokens","Cost control matters","Responses API"],"unknowns":["How many reasoning tokens does a typical request use?","Does effort `low` keep quality?"],"question":"What do you change first?","options":[{"name":"Set `truncation` to `auto`","verdict":"poor","why":"That setting trims input on overflow. It does not raise the output cap that was hit.","tradeoff":"It is right when the prompt, not the output, exceeds the window."},{"name":"Raise `max_output_tokens` to leave room for reasoning, then measure `reasoning_tokens` and tune","verdict":"best","why":"Both reasoning and visible text count against the cap, and the guidance is to reserve about 25,000 tokens first and tighten after measuring [[r3]].","tradeoff":"Worst-case spend per request rises until you tune the cap."},{"name":"Lower the reasoning effort to `low`","verdict":"good","why":"It shrinks hidden spend and latency, so the same cap may suffice.","tradeoff":"Quality can drop on hard questions, so confirm with an eval."}],"flips":[{"if":"Evals show effort `low` is as accurate","then":"Lower the effort, which cuts both cost and latency."},{"if":"Requests are all trivial lookups","then":"Use a non-reasoning model and keep the small cap."}],"takeaway":"A cap on a reasoning model must cover thinking plus the answer."},{"id":"w01-t02-s2","title":"A 330,000 token document on a standard deployment","situation":"An Azure `gpt-4.1` standard deployment rejects a 330,000 token prompt. The model card says it supports about 1M tokens. The task is a cross-reference check across the whole document.","constraints":["The whole document must be visible at once","Moderate budget","Azure deployment"],"unknowns":["Is a different model allowed?","How often will this run?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Use a model whose deployment window covers the prompt, and budget for the long-prompt price step","verdict":"best","why":"The standard `gpt-4.1` deployment allows 300,000 tokens, while models such as `gpt-5.4` list 1,050,000 [[r13]]. The task needs the whole document, so retrieval would lose cross references.","tradeoff":"Requests above 272,000 input tokens cost more, and long inputs can lose accuracy deep in the text."},{"name":"Trim or retrieve the document to fit under 300,000 tokens","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps the current model and cost.","tradeoff":"Cross references outside the retrieved part are missed, which the task forbids."},{"name":"Move to a provisioned deployment to get the full 1,047,576 window","verdict":"poor","why":"Provisioned and batch deployments of `gpt-4.1` allow only 128,000 tokens, which is smaller.","tradeoff":"Provisioned capacity helps with steady throughput, not with window size."}],"flips":[{"if":"Questions need only a few sections","then":"Retrieval is cheaper and often more accurate."},{"if":"The job runs once a day offline","then":"Use Batch or Flex pricing to offset the long-prompt cost."}],"takeaway":"The deployment type, not the model card, sets your window."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t02-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each API to its output cap parameter.","pairs":[{"l":"OpenAI Responses","r":"`max_output_tokens`"},{"l":"OpenAI Chat Completions (current)","r":"`max_completion_tokens`"},{"l":"Anthropic Messages","r":"`max_tokens` (required)"},{"l":"Chat Completions (deprecated name)","r":"`max_tokens`"}],"explain":"Responses uses `max_output_tokens`. Chat Completions replaced `max_tokens` with `max_completion_tokens`, and the old name is not compatible with o-series. Anthropic requires `max_tokens`, which includes thinking tokens."},{"id":"w01-t02-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A Responses call with the default `truncation` returns HTTP 400 when the conversation grows. Why?","options":["`truncation` defaults to `auto` and failed","The output cap was too high","`store` was false","`truncation` defaults to `disabled`, which fails on overflow"],"answer":3,"explain":"The default `disabled` fails with 400 when input exceeds the window. `auto` would trim instead. The output cap and `store` do not cause this error."},{"id":"w01-t02-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A request has 900,000 input tokens on a 1,050,000 token window with a 128,000 token output cap. What is the largest visible plus reasoning output the request can produce?","answer":128000,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"tokens","explain":"The window leaves 150,000 tokens, but the output cap of 128,000 is lower, so 128,000 is the limit. Both limits apply and the smaller one wins."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Does `max_output_tokens` reserve tokens?","back":"No. It is a ceiling. Reasoning tokens count against it, so a low value can leave no room for visible text."},{"front":"How much headroom does OpenAI advise for reasoning and output at the start?","back":"At least 25,000 tokens, then tune after measuring reasoning token use."},{"front":"Why can a model's listed window differ from what you get on Azure?","back":"Deployment type sets the limit. `gpt-4.1` allows 300,000 on standard and 128,000 on provisioned or batch, though the model supports 1,047,576."}],"related":["w01-t01","w01-t03","w01-t14","w02-t11","w16-t04"],"refs":["r1","r13","r3","r5","r19","r20","r12","tr7"]},{"id":"w01-t03","order":3,"group":"Model basics","title":"Reasoning models and reasoning effort","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Reasoning models spend hidden thinking tokens before they answer. An effort setting trades quality against latency and cost, and some sampling parameters are rejected.","useIf":"You use a reasoning model, tune effort, or see 400 errors after switching models.","avoidIf":"The task is simple extraction or classification where a small non-reasoning model passes your evals.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **reasoning model** generates internal thinking before the visible answer. Those **reasoning tokens** bill as output, share the context window, and appear in `output_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` (Responses) or `completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` (Chat Completions) [[r3]]. You cannot read the raw thinking. On OpenAI you receive a summary only if you ask for `reasoning.summary` (`auto`, `concise` or `detailed`)."},{"t":"p","md":"**Effort** controls how much the model thinks. OpenAI uses `reasoning.effort` in Responses and `reasoning_effort` in Chat Completions. Anthropic uses `output_config.effort`, which is GA, with `thinking: {\"type\": \"adaptive\"}` so the model decides how much to think [[r31]]. Higher effort costs output tokens and latency, so start low and raise it only when evals show a gain. Verbosity (`text.verbosity` or `verbosity`, values `low`, `medium`, `high`) is a separate control over answer length."},{"t":"table","caption":"Effort values on the check date 2026-10-13","head":["Model","Accepted values","Default","Note"],"compact":true,"rows":[["OpenAI `gpt-6-astra`","`low` to `max`","Not recorded","`none` returns HTTP 400"],["OpenAI `gpt-6.1-sol`","`low` to `max`","`medium`","No `none` or `minimal`"],["OpenAI `gpt-6-sol`, `gpt-6-luna`","`none` to `max`","`medium`","On GPT-6 use `low` where you used `minimal`"],["OpenAI `gpt-5.5`","`none`, `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`","`medium`","Source: [[r3]]"],["OpenAI `gpt-5.4`","Includes `none`","`none`","Chat Completions rejects tools when effort is not `none`"],["Claude (`output_config.effort`)","`low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max`","Per model","Opus 5.5 cannot disable thinking [[r21]]"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Sources disagree on rejected parameters","md":"OpenAI says that when effort is not `none` you should remove `temperature`, `top_p` and `top_logprobs`, plus `logprobs` in Chat [[r2]]. The Azure page lists a longer rejected set for reasoning models, yet its GPT-6 table marks `temperature`, `top_p` and `logprobs` as supported on all four GPT-6 models [[r15]]. Test your own deployment."},{"t":"p","md":"Other reasoning controls: `reasoning.mode` is `standard` (default) or `pro` on GPT-5.6 and GPT-6, independent of effort. `reasoning.context` is `auto`, `current_turn` or `all_turns`, and GPT-5.6 defaults to `all_turns`. A `configuration_update` input item changes effort mid-conversation without changing the cached prefix [[r3]]."}],"whereToUse":["Multi-step analysis, planning, math and code where a wrong first guess is costly","Agents that call tools in a loop and must recover from errors","Offline jobs where latency is irrelevant and accuracy pays, often through Batch or Flex","Interactive products at `low` effort when a small quality gain matters more than a few hundred milliseconds","Replacing complicated chain-of-thought prompts that the model handles internally"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The task fails often with a non-reasoning model on your evals","Errors are expensive and latency budget is generous","The task needs planning across tool results"],"avoid":["Classification, extraction and formatting where a small model already passes","Strict latency targets such as a spoken reply, unless effort is `none` or `low`","Code that relies on `temperature`, `top_p` or `logprobs` for behaviour"],"flips":["Evals show no gain from `high` over `low`: stay at `low`","The cap leaves no room for thinking: raise `max_output_tokens` before raising effort","You move to GPT-6 Astra or 6.1 Sol: stop sending `none`, use `low` and re-evaluate"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to buy more accuracy","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Small non-reasoning model","Task is narrow and evals pass","Multi-step reasoning is needed","Cheapest and fastest, lowest ceiling"],["Small model at `low` effort","A little planning helps","Hard multi-step problems","Modest extra tokens"],["Large model at `medium` or `high` effort","Hard tasks and agent loops","Tight latency or cost per task","Hidden tokens, longer time to first visible text"],["`pro` mode","Maximum quality on rare hard tasks","Volume or cost sensitivity","Highest cost and latency"],["Router that picks a model per request","Traffic mixes easy and hard","You lack evals per route","See the routing topic"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"HTTP 400 after upgrading to GPT-6 Astra","cause":"The code sends `reasoning.effort: none`, which Astra rejects","fix":"Use `low` as the starting point and rerun evals"},{"symptom":"400 for `temperature` or `top_p` on a reasoning call","cause":"Sampling parameters are removed when effort is not `none`","fix":"Drop them and steer with effort, verbosity, schemas and evals"},{"symptom":"Code that prints thinking text shows nothing on Claude Fable 5","cause":"`thinking.display` defaults to `omitted` on that model","fix":"Set `thinking.display` to `summarized` if you need a summary"},{"symptom":"Bills rise while answers look the same length","cause":"Hidden reasoning tokens bill as output","fix":"Track reasoning tokens per request and cap with `max_output_tokens`"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t03-s1","title":"Live chat, easy intent routing","situation":"A customer chat widget routes each message to one of eight queues. The team wants a 3 second p95 and low cost at high volume.","constraints":["p95 latency of 3 seconds","High volume","Eight fixed labels","Wrong routes are cheap to fix"],"unknowns":["Accuracy of a small model on the labelled set","Peak requests per second"],"question":"Which setup do you start with?","options":[{"name":"Large model at `xhigh` effort","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds seconds and large cost for a narrow task that does not need deep reasoning.","tradeoff":"It is right for rare, hard, high-stakes tasks with a loose latency budget."},{"name":"Small non-reasoning model, or a small model at `low` effort, chosen by evals","verdict":"best","why":"A narrow label task fits a small model, and low effort keeps latency inside the budget.","tradeoff":"A harder edge case may fail and need an escalation path."},{"name":"Large model at `low` effort","verdict":"good","why":"Higher quality ceiling at modest latency.","tradeoff":"Costs more per request than a small model that already passes."}],"flips":[{"if":"Misroutes are costly, for example legal escalation","then":"Add a cascade to a larger model for low-confidence cases."},{"if":"The small model misses the accuracy bar","then":"Raise effort or move up a model size and re-run evals."}],"takeaway":"Match effort to difficulty and latency, and let evals justify any increase."},{"id":"w01-t03-s2","title":"Overnight contract analysis","situation":"A legal team runs deep comparisons across contracts each night. Results are reviewed in the morning. Errors are costly and latency does not matter.","constraints":["Offline, results needed by morning","High stakes","Moderate volume"],"unknowns":["Does `high` beat `medium` on a labelled set?","Which service tier is allowed for this data?"],"question":"How do you configure it?","options":[{"name":"`high` effort with a generous output cap, run through Batch or Flex for the 50% discount","verdict":"best","why":"Quality matters and latency does not, and Batch and Flex run at 50% of Standard [[r37]] [[r38]].","tradeoff":"Jobs can take up to 24 hours, and Flex is Beta and may be unavailable."},{"name":"`low` effort synchronously to save money","verdict":"poor","why":"It saves tokens on the one workload where thinking pays, and stakes are high.","tradeoff":"Low effort is right for latency-bound, easy tasks."},{"name":"`high` effort synchronously at Standard price","verdict":"good","why":"Same quality, simpler to run.","tradeoff":"Pays about twice the batch price for latency nobody needs."}],"flips":[{"if":"Data may not use Batch under your retention policy","then":"Run synchronously and accept the price."},{"if":"Evals show `medium` equals `high`","then":"Use `medium` and save tokens."}],"takeaway":"When latency is free, spend it on effort and take the batch discount."},{"id":"w01-t03-s3","title":"400 after a model upgrade","situation":"A service sets `reasoning.effort: none` for speed and `temperature: 0.2`. After switching the deployment to GPT-6 Astra it returns HTTP 400.","constraints":["The service must keep working this week","Latency target is unchanged","Responses API"],"unknowns":["Which parameter causes the 400?","Does `low` effort meet the latency target?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Retry the same request with a delay","verdict":"poor","why":"The error is deterministic. The request is invalid for that model.","tradeoff":"Retries suit 429 and 5xx errors, not validation errors."},{"name":"Remove `temperature`, replace `none` with `low`, then rerun evals and a latency check","verdict":"good","why":"It fixes both rules at once and keeps the test loop.","tradeoff":"`low` adds some reasoning latency."},{"name":"Route this task to a model that supports `none`, such as GPT-6 Sol or Luna, and keep the controls","verdict":"best","why":"The task wanted no reasoning, and those models accept `none`, so quality and latency stay stable [[r2]].","tradeoff":"You depend on a different model and must still drop `temperature` when effort is above `none`."}],"flips":[{"if":"Astra quality is required","then":"Use `low` on Astra and tune from there."},{"if":"The deployment is on Azure","then":"Test each rejected parameter, since the pages disagree."}],"takeaway":"Treat a 400 as a contract change and read the model page."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t03-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A reasoning call returns `status: incomplete` with no visible text. What is the likeliest cause?","options":["The output cap was consumed by reasoning tokens","Temperature was too high","`store` was true","The model refused"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reasoning tokens count against `max_output_tokens`, so a low cap can end the response before any text. Temperature and `store` do not produce this result, and a refusal is a different signal."},{"id":"w01-t03-q2","type":"multi","tag":"flip","q":"Which changes can justify raising reasoning effort?","options":["Evals show a measurable gain on hard cases","The task is multi-step with tool results","You want a shorter answer","Errors are expensive and latency is loose"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Gains on evals, multi-step tool tasks and costly errors justify more effort. A shorter answer is a verbosity setting, not an effort setting."},{"id":"w01-t03-q3","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"Code sends `reasoning.effort: none` to GPT-6 Astra. What happens?","options":["It works and skips thinking","The model silently uses `low`","The model uses `max`","HTTP 400"],"answer":3,"explain":"Astra accepts `low` to `max`, and `none` returns HTTP 400 [[r2]]. It does not fall back to another value."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Where do reasoning tokens show up in usage?","back":"`output_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` in Responses and `completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` in Chat Completions. They bill as output."},{"front":"Which GPT-6 models reject `reasoning.effort: none`?","back":"GPT-6 Astra (HTTP 400) and GPT-6.1 Sol (no `none` or `minimal`). GPT-6 Sol and Luna accept `none` to `max`."},{"front":"What do you start with when tuning effort?","back":"Start low and raise only when evals show a gain, since effort adds output tokens and latency."},{"front":"What does Claude use instead of `budget_tokens` on new models?","back":"Adaptive thinking with `output_config.effort`. Manual `budget_tokens` is deprecated on Opus 4.6 and Sonnet 4.6 and not accepted on later models."}],"related":["w01-t02","w01-t04","w01-t09","w01-t17","w01-l3","w18-t01"],"refs":["r2","r3","r15","r31","r21","r37","r38"]},{"id":"w01-t04","order":4,"group":"Sampling and decoding","title":"Temperature and top_p","minutes":6,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Temperature reshapes the next-token probabilities, and top_p cuts the candidate list to the most probable tokens. Newer reasoning and Claude models reject non-default values.","useIf":"You tune randomness on a non-reasoning model, or debug a 400 about sampling after a model change.","avoidIf":"The model is a reasoning model with effort above `none`, or a recent Claude model. Control behaviour with prompts, schemas and effort instead.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"At each step the model scores every token. **Temperature** divides those scores before they become probabilities, so low values sharpen the distribution toward the top token and high values flatten it. **top_p** (nucleus sampling) keeps only the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches `p`, then samples from that set. Both reduce or widen variety. OpenAI advises changing one, not both [[r4]]. For most tasks pick one knob, leave the other at its default, and compare outputs on a fixed set of prompts rather than on a few samples."},{"t":"formula","title":"Temperature in the softmax","expr":"p_i = exp(z_i / T) / sum_j exp(z_j / T)","vars":[{"k":"z_i","v":"Score (logit) of token i"},{"k":"T","v":"Temperature. Near 0 approaches always picking the top token"}],"example":"Two tokens with scores 2.0 and 1.0. At T = 1 the probabilities are 0.731 and 0.269. At T = 0.5 they become 0.881 and 0.119, so the top token is chosen more often."},{"t":"table","caption":"Where the controls apply (check date 2026-10-13)","head":["API or model","temperature","top_p","Rule"],"compact":true,"rows":[["OpenAI Responses and Chat","0 to 2","Yes","Change `temperature` or `top_p`, not both [[r4]]"],["OpenAI reasoning, effort not `none`","Removed","Removed","Send neither, per the GPT-6 guide [[r2]]"],["Anthropic, older models","0.0 to 1.0, default 1.0","Yes","Lower for analytical tasks [[r19]]"],["Anthropic, models after Opus 4.6","Only 1.0","0.99 or higher","Any other value returns 400. Opus 4.7 (2026-04-16) already did this [[r21]]"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Common starting ranges from practice, not vendor rules: 0 to 0.3 for extraction and classification, 0.2 to 0.5 for summaries, around 0.7 for conversation, 0.8 to 1.0 for brainstorming. Validate with evals. Temperature 0 does not guarantee identical outputs, as Anthropic states [[r19]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"When not to touch them","md":"If the model is a reasoning model, or a recent Claude model, leave sampling at defaults. Steer with the prompt, strict schemas, effort and verbosity, and measure variance with repeated runs."}],"whereToUse":["Extraction and classification on non-reasoning models, where low temperature reduces wobble","Creative drafting and brainstorming, where higher values give variety","A/B tests of prompt variants, where you hold sampling fixed to compare fairly","Migration reviews, to find code that sends sampling values a new model rejects","Self-consistency setups that sample several answers on purpose and vote"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You use a non-reasoning OpenAI model and need more or less variety","The task has one right answer and you want fewer random deviations","You need diverse candidates, for example several drafts to rank"],"avoid":["Changing both temperature and top_p at once, which makes effects hard to attribute","Relying on temperature 0 for reproducibility","Sending non-default values to models that reject them"],"flips":["Model is a reasoning model at effort above `none`: remove the parameters","Target is a Claude model after Opus 4.6: remove them and test variance by repeated runs","Outputs are flat and repetitive: raise temperature a little before touching penalties"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to control variety","head":["Lever","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Lower temperature","One right answer, non-reasoning model","Reasoning or new Claude models reject it","Less diversity, still not deterministic"],["Lower top_p","You want to cut the unlikely tail","You already changed temperature","Interaction makes tuning hard"],["Strict schema or enum","Output shape must be exact","Free text is wanted","Constrains format, not wording"],["Prompt and examples","Behaviour must be stable across models","The prompt is already long","Costs tokens, needs evals"],["Sample several and vote","Accuracy matters more than cost","Latency or cost is tight","Multiplies cost, and `n` bills for all choices"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"HTTP 400 on a Claude call that worked on an older model","cause":"Models after Opus 4.6 accept only temperature 1.0 and top_p of 0.99 or higher","fix":"Remove the parameters and control behaviour with prompt, schema and effort"},{"symptom":"Results still differ between runs at temperature 0","cause":"Temperature 0 is not fully deterministic","fix":"Test with repeated runs and tolerances, and store outputs when you need an audit trail"},{"symptom":"Tuning has no clear effect","cause":"Both temperature and top_p were changed together","fix":"Change one, measure on a fixed eval set, then decide"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t04-s1","title":"Extracting fields from invoices","situation":"A pipeline extracts eight fields from invoices using a non-reasoning model. Outputs vary slightly between runs and break a downstream match step.","constraints":["Non-reasoning OpenAI model","One correct answer per field","Downstream code needs stable values"],"unknowns":["Is the variance in values or in formatting?","Can the output use a strict schema?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Use a strict schema, set a low temperature and leave top_p alone, then measure agreement over repeated runs","verdict":"best","why":"A schema fixes the shape, low temperature reduces value wobble, and changing one sampling control keeps the test clean.","tradeoff":"Variance may remain, so the downstream step should tolerate small differences."},{"name":"Set temperature 2 and top_p 0.1 so the two cancel out","verdict":"poor","why":"The effects interact and are hard to predict. High temperature adds the randomness you want to remove.","tradeoff":"Sampling a wide pool is right for brainstorming, not extraction."},{"name":"Set temperature 0 and assume it is deterministic","verdict":"good","why":"Cuts most randomness.","tradeoff":"Output can still differ between runs, and nothing checks the shape."}],"flips":[{"if":"The model is a reasoning model","then":"Drop sampling parameters and use a schema plus effort."},{"if":"Auditors need exact reproduction","then":"Store the extracted output with inputs and model version."}],"takeaway":"Fix the shape with a schema, narrow randomness with one control, and measure."},{"id":"w01-t04-s2","title":"Porting a prompt to a recent Claude model","situation":"An internal tool sends `temperature: 0.2` to an older model. The team switches to Claude Opus 5.5 and every call returns HTTP 400.","constraints":["Low-risk internal tool","Deadline this week","Output stability still matters"],"unknowns":["Which parameters does the request send?","How much variance does the tool tolerate?"],"question":"How do you proceed?","options":[{"name":"Catch the 400 and retry with random sampling values until one works","verdict":"poor","why":"Models after Opus 4.6 accept only temperature 1.0 and top_p of 0.99 or higher, so guessing wastes calls and hides the rule.","tradeoff":"Trial and error is acceptable only against an undocumented API, and this one is documented."},{"name":"Remove the sampling parameters, tighten the prompt and use a strict schema, then check variance with repeated runs","verdict":"good","why":"Matches the documented rule and keeps output stable by other means.","tradeoff":"More prompt work and an eval pass."},{"name":"Remove the sampling parameters, use `output_config` structured outputs and effort settings, and add a small repeat-run test to the release checklist","verdict":"best","why":"It follows the rule and uses the controls that remain supported, with a test that guards against regression [[r19]] [[r32]].","tradeoff":"Needs a schema and a repeat-run harness."}],"flips":[{"if":"You need low temperature behaviour exactly","then":"Use a model family that still accepts it, and record why."},{"if":"The tool is used for creative output","then":"Defaults of 1.0 may be fine, so keep them and rank several drafts."}],"takeaway":"A rejected parameter is a contract. Replace the control, do not guess values."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t04-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Two tokens have logits 2.0 and 1.0. At temperature 0.5, what is the probability of the higher-scoring token?","answer":0.881,"tolerancePct":2,"explain":"Divide scores by 0.5 to get 4 and 2. exp(4) = 54.6 and exp(2) = 7.39, so p = 54.6 / 61.99 = 0.881."},{"id":"w01-t04-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A request to a Claude model released after Opus 4.6 sends `temperature: 0.3`. What happens?","options":["It is clamped to 1.0","It is ignored silently","It returns HTTP 400","It works but only for the first turn"],"answer":2,"explain":"These models accept only temperature 1.0 and top_p of 0.99 or higher, and other values return 400 [[r19]]. They are not clamped or ignored."},{"id":"w01-t04-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which statements about temperature and top_p are correct?","options":["Change one, not both, when tuning","Temperature 0 guarantees identical outputs","Low temperature sharpens the distribution toward the top token","Reasoning calls with effort above `none` should drop both"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"OpenAI advises changing one, low temperature sharpens, and OpenAI says to remove both when effort is not `none`. Temperature 0 does not guarantee determinism."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does temperature do to the next-token distribution?","back":"It divides the logits before the softmax. Low values sharpen toward the top token and high values flatten the distribution."},{"front":"What is top_p?","back":"Nucleus sampling: sample only from the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches p."},{"front":"Which Claude models accept only temperature 1.0?","back":"Models released after Opus 4.6, including Opus 4.7. They also need top_p of 0.99 or higher, and other values return 400."}],"related":["w01-t03","w01-t05","w01-t06","w01-l2","w14-t12"],"refs":["r4","r2","r19","r21","r32"]},{"id":"w01-t05","order":5,"group":"Sampling and decoding","title":"top_k, seed and determinism","minutes":6,"difficulty":"core","summary":"top_k exists on a few APIs only, seed gives best-effort repeatability on OpenAI Chat Completions, and no major API guarantees identical outputs.","useIf":"You need repeatable behaviour for tests, audits or debugging, or you port code that sets top_k or seed.","avoidIf":"You expect bit-for-bit reproducibility from a hosted model. Design tests and audits around variance instead.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**top_k** limits sampling to the k most probable tokens. It is not in the OpenAI Responses or Chat Completions bodies. Anthropic Messages still lists `top_k`, deprecated, and models released after Opus 4.6 reject it with 400. Gemini exposes `topK` inside `generationConfig` [[r19]] [[tr3]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**seed** asks the service to sample deterministically. Only Chat Completions has it, and it is marked Beta. Repeated requests with the same seed and parameters should usually match, but determinism is not guaranteed, so watch `system_fingerprint` for backend changes [[r5]]. The Responses API has no `seed`, and Anthropic documents none and says temperature 0.0 is not fully deterministic [[r19]]. Treat determinism as something you measure: run the same request several times, compare the parsed results and track the agreement rate, instead of assuming a flag delivers it."},{"t":"table","caption":"Sources of variation and what controls them","head":["Source","What you can do","Limit"],"rows":[["Sampling randomness","Lower temperature, or use a strict schema or enum","Reasoning and new Claude models reject temperature"],["Moving model aliases","Pin a dated snapshot or version","Snapshots retire on published dates [[r11]]"],["Backend changes","Send `seed`, log `system_fingerprint`","Chat Completions only, Beta, best effort"],["Prompt or tool-order changes","Version prompts and tool lists","Needs process discipline"],["Need for exact replay","Store the output with inputs and model version","Storage and privacy handling"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Compatibility layers drop controls","md":"Anthropic's OpenAI SDK layer silently ignores `seed`, `logprobs`, `top_logprobs`, `presence_penalty`, `frequency_penalty`, `logit_bias`, `response_format`, `reasoning_effort` and `store`. Anthropic says it is for testing, not production [[r36]]."}],"whereToUse":["Regression tests for prompts, where you compare behaviour across versions","Debugging an odd answer: replay with the same seed and parameters to see if it recurs","Audit and dispute handling, where you must show what the system said","Porting code between vendors, to remove parameters one side ignores","Evaluation design, to decide how many repeated runs a metric needs"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You call Chat Completions on a non-reasoning model and want fewer surprises in tests","You can log `system_fingerprint` and the exact parameters","You treat repeatability as a goal to measure, not a guarantee"],"avoid":["Building a compliance claim on `seed` or temperature 0","Sending `top_k` to OpenAI or recent Claude models","Asserting exact string equality on free text in CI"],"flips":["You move to the Responses API: there is no `seed`, so test by repeated runs and store outputs","You must prove past behaviour: store the output, not the recipe","Variance is acceptable for the product: spend effort on quality metrics instead"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to get stable behaviour","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["`seed` plus fixed parameters","Chat Completions and debugging replays","Responses, Anthropic, reasoning models","Best effort only, Beta"],["Strict schema or enum output","Shape and label sets are fixed","Free-form answers","Constrains format, not wording"],["Pinned snapshot","You need the same model over months","The snapshot retires","Forced migration on the retirement date"],["Store outputs and replay from storage","Audit and user-visible history","You want fresh answers","Storage, retention and privacy duties"],["Repeated runs with a pass-rate threshold","Testing prompts and evals","A single run must be exact","Multiplies test cost"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A test with `seed` and temperature 0 fails intermittently","cause":"`seed` is Beta and best effort, and backends change","fix":"Assert on parsed fields with a tolerance over repeated runs, and log `system_fingerprint`"},{"symptom":"400 error mentioning `top_k` on Claude","cause":"`top_k` is deprecated and rejected on models after Opus 4.6","fix":"Remove it and control behaviour with prompt, schema and effort"},{"symptom":"`seed` has no effect after a migration","cause":"The Responses API and the Anthropic compatibility layer do not support it","fix":"Remove it and use stored outputs or repeated-run tests"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t05-s1","title":"A flaky extraction test in CI","situation":"An extraction prompt is tested in CI by comparing the full output string to a saved file. The test fails about one run in ten with no code change.","constraints":["Non-reasoning model","CI must stay fast","The team wants reliable red and green signals"],"unknowns":["Which fields differ between runs?","Is the model alias pinned?"],"question":"How do you fix the test?","options":[{"name":"Set `seed` and temperature 0 and keep exact string equality","verdict":"poor","why":"Both are best effort, so some runs still differ and the test stays flaky.","tradeoff":"It helps debugging replays, not CI gating."},{"name":"Record golden outputs and review diffs by hand","verdict":"good","why":"Catches real regressions and needs no randomness assumptions.","tradeoff":"Manual effort on every change."},{"name":"Pin the model snapshot, parse fields, and assert a pass rate over several runs per case","verdict":"best","why":"It tolerates harmless variance and fails when quality falls, which is what the gate should measure.","tradeoff":"Several calls per test case raise CI time and cost."}],"flips":[{"if":"Outputs are a fixed enum","then":"Exact equality on the parsed label is fine, with a small retry budget."},{"if":"CI time is tight","then":"Run the repeated-run suite nightly and a single-run smoke test per commit."}],"takeaway":"Gate on measured pass rates, not on exact strings from a sampled model."},{"id":"w01-t05-s2","title":"Showing a regulator what the assistant said","situation":"A regulated team must show exactly what the assistant told a customer last quarter. A colleague suggests regenerating the answer at audit time with `seed` and temperature 0.","constraints":["Exact record required","Models and backends change over a quarter","Regulated domain"],"unknowns":["Retention period for chat data","Whether the model alias has moved"],"question":"What is the sound approach?","options":[{"name":"Store inputs, model version, parameters and the output, and show the stored record","verdict":"best","why":"A stored record is exact, while regeneration depends on a model and backend that may have changed.","tradeoff":"You take on storage, access control and retention duties."},{"name":"Send `seed`, log `system_fingerprint`, and regenerate if the fingerprint matches","verdict":"good","why":"A matching fingerprint raises confidence in a replay.","tradeoff":"Still best effort, and the snapshot may be retired by then."},{"name":"Regenerate at audit time with temperature 0","verdict":"poor","why":"Temperature 0 is not fully deterministic and the model may have changed.","tradeoff":"Fine for debugging a recent session, not as evidence."}],"flips":[{"if":"Data retention rules forbid storing outputs","then":"Store a hash and the minimum metadata, and consult legal on what proof is acceptable."},{"if":"Only an informal internal review is needed","then":"A seeded replay may be enough."}],"takeaway":"Evidence is a record, not a recipe you hope to replay."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t05-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each API to its status for `seed`.","pairs":[{"l":"OpenAI Chat Completions","r":"Has `seed`, Beta, best effort"},{"l":"OpenAI Responses","r":"No `seed` parameter"},{"l":"Anthropic Messages","r":"No `seed`, temperature 0.0 not fully deterministic"},{"l":"Anthropic OpenAI SDK layer","r":"Accepts `seed` but ignores it"}],"explain":"Only Chat Completions exposes `seed`, and it is Beta. The Responses API and the Anthropic Messages API have none, and the Anthropic compatibility layer silently ignores it."},{"id":"w01-t05-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"What is the most defensible way to prove what an assistant said last quarter?","options":["Regenerate with temperature 0","Regenerate with the same seed","Store the output with inputs and model version","Re-run the prompt on the newest model"],"answer":2,"explain":"A stored record is exact. Regeneration depends on a model and backend that may have changed, and `seed` and temperature 0 are best effort."},{"id":"w01-t05-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Which field helps you notice that the backend changed between two requests with the same seed?","options":["`system_fingerprint`","`finish_reason`","`logprobs`","`n`"],"answer":0,"explain":"`system_fingerprint` identifies the backend configuration, so a change explains different outputs. The other fields do not describe the backend."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which major APIs expose `top_k`?","back":"Not OpenAI Responses or Chat Completions. Anthropic lists it as deprecated and rejects it on models after Opus 4.6. Gemini has `topK` in `generationConfig`."},{"front":"How reliable is `seed`?","back":"Best effort and Beta, on Chat Completions only. Log `system_fingerprint` to spot backend changes."},{"front":"How do you test a flaky structured extraction?","back":"Pin the model snapshot, parse fields, run each case several times and assert a pass rate instead of exact strings."}],"related":["w01-t04","w01-t08","w14-t12","w14-t14","w01-l2"],"refs":["r5","r19","r36","r11","tr3"]},{"id":"w01-t06","order":6,"group":"Sampling and decoding","title":"Penalties, logit_bias and stop sequences","minutes":6,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Penalties discourage repeated tokens, logit_bias shifts the odds of specific token ids, and stop sequences end generation at a delimiter. Availability differs by API.","useIf":"You fight repetition, must ban or favour specific tokens, or need generation to end at a known delimiter.","avoidIf":"A prompt change, a schema or a stop sequence would solve it. Penalties and bias are blunt and tokenizer specific.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"On Chat Completions, **frequency_penalty** lowers a token's odds in proportion to how often it has already appeared, and **presence_penalty** lowers it once it has appeared at all. Both range from -2.0 to 2.0, and values between -1 and 1 are the usual range [[r5]]. Positive values discourage repetition, negative values encourage it. Penalties also hurt text where repeats are correct, such as code, JSON keys and names."},{"t":"p","md":"**logit_bias** maps a token id to a value from -100 to 100 that is added to that token's score. Large negative values act like a ban and large positive values like a force. Ids belong to a tokenizer, and variants of a word (leading space, capital letter, plural) are different tokens, so a bias on one id misses its siblings. **Stop sequences** end generation when the model emits a match. OpenAI Chat accepts up to 4 and does not support `stop` on o3 and o4-mini. Anthropic's `stop_sequences` is an array, and a match sets `stop_reason: \"stop_sequence\"` [[r19]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Availability on the check date 2026-10-13","head":["Control","OpenAI Chat","OpenAI Responses","Anthropic Messages"],"compact":true,"rows":[["`frequency_penalty`, `presence_penalty`","Yes, -2.0 to 2.0","No","No"],["`logit_bias`","Yes, -100 to 100","No","No"],["`stop`","Yes, up to 4 sequences","No","`stop_sequences`"],["Via Anthropic's OpenAI compatibility layer","Penalties and `logit_bias` ignored","n/a","Not for production [[r36]]"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Design stop sequences that cannot occur by accident","md":"A stop string that can appear in valid output truncates answers silently. Prefer unique delimiters such as a speaker label with a newline, pair the stop with a token cap, and check the stop reason in tests."}],"whereToUse":["Long creative or marketing generation on a non-reasoning Chat model that loops on phrases","Dialogue prompts where the model may continue as the next speaker","Forcing a one-token label on a legacy classifier path, with the cost of tokenizer coupling","Banning a small set of unwanted tokens when a prompt cannot do it","Ending output at a delimiter in a custom template format"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Repetition persists after you fix the prompt, on a non-reasoning model","A delimiter reliably marks the end of the part you need","You can test the exact tokenizer behaviour of the biased tokens"],"avoid":["Penalties on code, structured output or anything that needs repeated tokens","logit_bias across several vendors, since ids and support differ","Stop sequences that can occur inside valid answers"],"flips":["You use Responses or Anthropic: penalties and bias do not exist, so use prompts and schemas","You need a label from a fixed set: use structured outputs with an enum instead of bias","You are on a reasoning model: expect rejected or ignored controls and test"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to shape generation","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Small frequency or presence penalty","Prose that repeats itself","Code, JSON, names","Can distort wording, tune with evals"],["`logit_bias`","A few known token ids to ban or favour","Variants and multiple vendors","Tokenizer coupling, easy to miss siblings"],["Stop sequence","A clear delimiter ends the useful text","The delimiter occurs in valid output","Silent truncation"],["Strict schema or enum","The output shape or label set is fixed","Free-form prose","Needs schema design"],["Prompt change","Instructions are unclear","The model ignores them anyway","Cheapest first step"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Answers get stranger as the penalty grows","cause":"High penalties push the model away from correct repeated words","fix":"Stay within -1 to 1, try 0.2 to 0.5 first, and verify with evals"},{"symptom":"A banned word still appears","cause":"`logit_bias` covered one token id, but the word has variants with other ids","fix":"Tokenize each variant with the right tokenizer or use a post-check"},{"symptom":"Answers end mid-sentence","cause":"A stop sequence occurs inside valid text","fix":"Choose a rarer delimiter and check `finish_reason` or `stop_reason`"},{"symptom":"Parameters have no effect after a port","cause":"The target API or compatibility layer ignores them","fix":"Check the body reference for the API and remove unsupported controls"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t06-s1","title":"A long draft that keeps looping","situation":"A non-reasoning model on Chat Completions writes 800 word product descriptions. Some drafts repeat the same phrase many times.","constraints":["Chat Completions","Prose, no code or JSON","A prompt rewrite has already been tried"],"unknowns":["Which phrases repeat?","Does a token cap hide the loops?"],"question":"What do you try first?","options":[{"name":"Apply a small frequency penalty, cap tokens, and compare drafts on an eval set","verdict":"best","why":"Prose tolerates mild penalties, and a cap limits damage while you tune.","tradeoff":"Too high a value changes word choice, so measure quality."},{"name":"Set `logit_bias` of -100 on the token of the repeated word","verdict":"good","why":"Stops that word.","tradeoff":"Variants of the word still loop, and the word may be needed elsewhere."},{"name":"Set both penalties to 2.0","verdict":"poor","why":"The maximum values distort wording across the whole draft and can break grammar.","tradeoff":"Strong penalties suit rare cases such as generating a list of unique items."}],"flips":[{"if":"The output is code or JSON","then":"Avoid penalties and use schemas or a different model."},{"if":"The target moves to the Responses API","then":"Penalties are absent, so use prompt rules and a length cap."}],"takeaway":"Use small penalties for prose and measure the effect."},{"id":"w01-t06-s2","title":"A chatbot that writes the user's next line","situation":"A roleplay prompt uses a single block of text. The model sometimes continues with `User:` and invents the user's reply.","constraints":["Free-form dialogue","The label `User:` marks the next speaker","Cost matters"],"unknowns":["Can the prompt use the chat message format instead?","Does `User:` ever appear in valid replies?"],"question":"How do you stop it?","options":[{"name":"Cut the text after generation with a regex","verdict":"good","why":"It works on any API.","tradeoff":"You still pay for and wait on the extra tokens."},{"name":"Add `User:` as a stop sequence and cap output tokens","verdict":"best","why":"Generation ends at the delimiter, saving tokens and latency, and the cap bounds worst cases.","tradeoff":"The delimiter must not occur in valid replies, so test the stop reason."},{"name":"Set `logit_bias` of -100 on the token for `User`","verdict":"poor","why":"Token variants slip through and the model may need the word in valid answers.","tradeoff":"Bias suits banning a few known tokens, not a speaker label."}],"flips":[{"if":"You can use proper chat roles","then":"Use message roles and drop the label hack."},{"if":"The target is Anthropic","then":"Use `stop_sequences`, and check `stop_reason`."}],"takeaway":"End generation at a unique delimiter and verify the stop reason."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t06-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What is the difference between frequency and presence penalty?","options":["Frequency scales with how often a token appeared, presence applies once it appeared","Presence scales with count, frequency applies once","They are the same parameter","Frequency applies only to rare tokens"],"answer":0,"explain":"Frequency penalty grows with the number of earlier occurrences, and presence penalty is a flat reduction after any occurrence. They are separate parameters."},{"id":"w01-t06-q2","type":"multi","tag":"bug","q":"Which are reasons a `logit_bias` ban can fail?","options":["Variants of the word have different token ids","The target API does not support `logit_bias`","The ids came from another vendor's tokenizer","Bias values above 100 are required"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Token variants, unsupported APIs and mismatched tokenizers all defeat a ban. The valid range is -100 to 100, so values above 100 are not required."},{"id":"w01-t06-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"An answer ends abruptly and `stop_reason` is `stop_sequence`. What is the likely cause?","options":["The output cap was reached","The model refused","The context window was full","A stop string occurred in the text"],"answer":3,"explain":"A match sets `stop_reason` to `stop_sequence` on Anthropic. A cap gives a length-type reason, and a refusal or a full window show different signals."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What range do the penalties and `logit_bias` accept?","back":"Penalties -2.0 to 2.0, `logit_bias` -100 to 100. Values between -1 and 1 are the usual range for penalties."},{"front":"How many stop sequences does OpenAI Chat accept?","back":"Up to 4. `stop` is not supported on o3 and o4-mini, and the Responses API has no `stop`."},{"front":"Why is `logit_bias` fragile across vendors?","back":"It uses tokenizer-specific ids, word variants have different ids, and some APIs do not support it at all."}],"related":["w01-t04","w01-t07","w01-t08","w01-t10"],"refs":["r5","r19","r36"]},{"id":"w01-t07","order":7,"group":"Sampling and decoding","title":"Logprobs and confidence signals","minutes":6,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Logprobs expose the log probability of each generated token and its top alternatives. They are a usable but uncalibrated signal for classification confidence and abstention.","useIf":"You classify with a non-reasoning model and want a threshold to escalate or abstain.","avoidIf":"You treat a high token probability as proof that a fact is true, or your model path drops logprobs.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"With `logprobs` on, the API returns the log probability of each chosen token and, with `top_logprobs` (0 to 20), the most likely alternatives. In Chat Completions set `logprobs: true` and `top_logprobs`. In Responses set `top_logprobs` and add `message.output_text.logprobs` to `include` [[r4]] [[r5]]. A log probability converts to a probability with `exp`, so -0.105 is about 0.90."},{"t":"formula","title":"From logprob to probability","expr":"p = exp(logprob)","vars":[{"k":"logprob","v":"Natural-log probability returned for a token, always 0 or negative"},{"k":"p","v":"Probability of that token given the preceding text"}],"example":"A one-token label `refund` with logprob -0.105 has p = exp(-0.105) = 0.90. If the top alternative `cancel` has -2.3, its p is about 0.10."},{"t":"p","md":"The standard pattern is a **classifier with a single-token label**: ask for one label, set a tiny token cap, read the label's probability, and escalate to a human or a larger model below a threshold. Pick labels whose first tokens differ, or probability splits across variants."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Availability depends on the model path","md":"OpenAI says that when effort is not `none` you should remove `logprobs` and `top_logprobs`, and remove `message.output_text.logprobs` from `include` [[r2]]. The Azure page marks `logprobs` as supported on the GPT-6 models while its reasoning page lists it as rejected [[r15]]. Anthropic Messages has no `logprobs`, and the OpenAI compatibility layer ignores it [[r36]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Confidence signals compared","head":["Signal","Strength","Weakness"],"compact":true,"rows":[["Token logprob of the label","Free, per request, fast","Uncalibrated, absent on some paths"],["Model's stated confidence","Works on any API","Verbal numbers are often over-confident"],["Agreement across sampled answers","Catches unstable answers","Multiplies cost and latency"],["Retrieval score or citation coverage","Tied to evidence","Says nothing about reasoning errors"],["Verifier model or rule check","Targets specific failures","Extra call, can share biases"]]}],"whereToUse":["Routing and triage classifiers that must escalate uncertain cases","Cascades: accept the small model's answer only above a probability threshold","Highlighting low-confidence tokens in an extraction review tool","Ranking candidate completions or comparing label options","Monitoring drift: a shift in the average label probability can flag a model or data change"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The output is a short label from a small set","You can build a labelled set to calibrate thresholds","The model path supports logprobs"],"avoid":["Trusting raw probabilities across models or after a model update","Using logprobs on free-form long answers as a truth score","Reasoning paths that drop the parameter"],"flips":["You move to a reasoning model or Claude: use sampled agreement, a verifier or retrieval signals","Labels share first tokens: rename them or compare full-sequence probabilities","The cost of a wrong accept is high: add a second signal before acting"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Choosing a confidence signal","head":["Signal","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Logprob of a one-token label","Non-reasoning Chat or Responses classifier","Reasoning model or Claude","Needs calibration per model"],["Self-reported confidence","No logprobs available","You need reliable ranking","Often poorly calibrated"],["Sampled agreement","Any model, unstable answers","Latency or cost is tight","n times the cost"],["Verifier or judge model","Specific failure types matter","Judge shares the generator's blind spots","Extra call and evals"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A 0.95 probability label is wrong often","cause":"Raw token probabilities are not calibrated against accuracy","fix":"Plot accuracy by probability bucket on labelled data and set thresholds from that curve"},{"symptom":"Probability splits across near-identical labels","cause":"Several labels start with the same token or have variants","fix":"Use labels with distinct first tokens or sum variants before comparing"},{"symptom":"400 or missing logprobs after enabling reasoning","cause":"The reasoning path removes `logprobs` and `top_logprobs`","fix":"Use effort `none` where supported, or switch signals"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t07-s1","title":"Escalating uncertain tickets","situation":"A non-reasoning model labels support tickets into six queues. Wrong routes waste agent time. The team has 3,000 labelled tickets.","constraints":["Single-token labels","Labelled data exists","Escalation to a human is available"],"unknowns":["How accurate is the model at each probability level?","What share of tickets may be escalated?"],"question":"How do you decide when to escalate?","options":[{"name":"Ask the model for a confidence number in the answer","verdict":"poor","why":"Verbal confidence is rarely calibrated, and it costs extra output tokens.","tradeoff":"It is the fallback when logprobs are unavailable."},{"name":"Read the label's logprob and set a threshold from an accuracy-by-probability curve on the labelled set","verdict":"best","why":"It is free, per request and calibrated on your own data, which fits a labelled, single-token task.","tradeoff":"Recalibrate after model updates, and the signal vanishes on reasoning paths."},{"name":"Run five samples and escalate on disagreement","verdict":"good","why":"Works on any model and catches unstable answers.","tradeoff":"Five times the cost and added latency."}],"flips":[{"if":"The model path drops logprobs","then":"Use sampled agreement or a verifier."},{"if":"Volume is tiny and cost is not a concern","then":"Sampled agreement is simpler to explain."}],"takeaway":"Calibrate a free signal on your labels before you trust it."},{"id":"w01-t07-s2","title":"Confidence on a reasoning model","situation":"A team moved a classifier to a reasoning model at effort `medium` and the request that asks for `logprobs` fails. They still need an escalation signal.","constraints":["Reasoning at `medium` effort improves accuracy","Escalation signal still required","Responses API"],"unknowns":["Does accuracy hold at effort `none`?","Is extra latency acceptable for a second check?"],"question":"What is the best path?","options":[{"name":"Keep `logprobs` and ignore the error","verdict":"poor","why":"The call fails or drops the field when effort is not `none`, so there is no signal.","tradeoff":"Valid only on a path where the model supports the parameter."},{"name":"Use sampled agreement or a lightweight verifier check on low-stakes labels and escalate disagreements","verdict":"good","why":"Works with reasoning models and gives a usable signal.","tradeoff":"Costs more calls and needs evals."},{"name":"Test whether a non-reasoning model at effort `none` keeps accuracy, and use its logprobs if it does","verdict":"best","why":"It restores the free per-request signal if accuracy holds, which an eval can show.","tradeoff":"If accuracy drops, you need the agreement or verifier path."}],"flips":[{"if":"Accuracy at `none` is too low","then":"Keep the reasoning model and add the agreement check."},{"if":"Decisions are high stakes","then":"Add a verifier or human review regardless of the signal."}],"takeaway":"Pick the signal your model path supports, then validate it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t07-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A label token has logprob -0.105. What is its probability, to two decimals?","answer":0.9,"tolerancePct":2,"explain":"p = exp(-0.105) = 0.90. Logprobs are natural logs, so exponentiate to get a probability."},{"id":"w01-t07-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Why is a raw token probability a weak stand-alone confidence score?","options":["It is always below 0.5","It is not calibrated to accuracy and reflects token likelihood, not truth","It costs extra tokens","It only works on images"],"answer":1,"explain":"A token can be likely yet wrong. Calibrate thresholds on labelled data. The other options are false."},{"id":"w01-t07-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"On which paths should you not expect `logprobs`?","options":["Anthropic Messages","OpenAI reasoning calls with effort not `none`","OpenAI Chat Completions on a non-reasoning model","Anthropic's OpenAI SDK compatibility layer"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Anthropic Messages has no logprobs, OpenAI removes them when effort is not `none`, and the compatibility layer ignores them. A non-reasoning Chat call supports them."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How do you turn a logprob into a probability?","back":"Apply `exp`. A logprob of -0.105 gives about 0.90."},{"front":"What is the single-token label pattern?","back":"Ask for one label with a tiny token cap, read its probability and escalate below a threshold calibrated on labelled data."},{"front":"Why prefer labels with distinct first tokens?","back":"Probability splits across variants that share a first token, which makes the comparison unreliable."}],"related":["w01-t03","w01-t17","w05-t06","w14-t09"],"refs":["r4","r5","r2","r15","r36"]},{"id":"w01-t08","order":8,"group":"Structured interaction","title":"Structured outputs vs JSON mode","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"JSON mode guarantees valid JSON. Structured outputs with a strict schema guarantee that the JSON matches your schema, within each platform's schema limits.","useIf":"Code parses the model output and a missing field or wrong type causes a failure.","avoidIf":"The output is free text for a human. A schema adds constraints and failure paths you do not need.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**JSON mode** (`{type: \"json_object\"}`) guarantees syntactically valid JSON and nothing about its shape. **Structured outputs** (`text.format: {type: \"json_schema\", strict: true, schema}` in Responses, `response_format` in Chat Completions) guarantee the output follows your schema. OpenAI recommends structured outputs whenever possible, and they need `gpt-4o-mini`, `gpt-4o-2024-08-06` or later. Safety refusals are detectable in the response [[r8]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"A strict schema (the value of `text.format`)","code":"{\n  \"type\": \"json_schema\",\n  \"name\": \"ticket\",\n  \"strict\": true,\n  \"schema\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n      \"queue\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"billing\", \"tech\", \"other\"] },\n      \"due_date\": { \"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"] }\n    },\n    \"required\": [\"queue\", \"due_date\"],\n    \"additionalProperties\": false\n  }\n}","caption":"Every field is required, and an optional field becomes a union with null."},{"t":"table","caption":"Schema limits differ by platform (check date 2026-10-13)","head":["Rule","OpenAI","Azure OpenAI"],"compact":true,"rows":[["Object properties","Up to 5,000","Up to 100"],["Nesting levels","10","5"],["String keywords","`pattern`, `format`, `minimum`, `maximum`, `minItems`, `maxItems` supported, not on fine-tuned models","`minLength`, `maxLength`, `pattern`, `format` unsupported"],["Required fields and extras","All required, `additionalProperties: false` mandatory, root is an object","Root cannot be `anyOf`"],["Parallel tool calls","Strict works on non-fine-tuned models since 2025-05-20","Page says set `parallel_tool_calls` to `false`"]]},{"t":"p","md":"On Anthropic, `output_config.format` with `type: \"json_schema\"` gives schema-valid output and `strict: true` on tools gives schema-valid tool inputs. Both are GA on the Claude API, Google Cloud and Microsoft Foundry, while Bedrock covers Opus 4.6 and earlier on its legacy integration [[r32]]. Use the Azure page for Azure limits [[r33]]. Design schemas for the model as well as the parser: use enums for categories, add short field descriptions, keep nesting shallow and put cross-field rules in application code, because a schema cannot express them."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Valid JSON is not a valid answer","md":"A strict schema fixes shape, not truth. A refusal, a response cut off by the output cap, or a plausible wrong value still reaches your code. Check for refusal and incomplete status before you parse, and validate business rules afterwards."}],"whereToUse":["Extraction of fields from documents into database rows","Classification with an enum label that downstream code switches on","Agent steps that produce a plan object for a workflow engine","Tool arguments, through strict function calling","Any pipeline where a parse failure would page someone"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Code consumes the output and needs every field","You want to delete retry-and-repair parsing loops","A label must come from a fixed set"],"avoid":["Free-form prose for people, where a schema forces odd structure","Schemas larger than the platform allows, especially on Azure","Treating schema validity as a quality guarantee"],"flips":["You run on Azure with a large schema: split it into steps or flatten it to fit 100 properties and 5 levels","The model is fine-tuned: expect fewer supported keywords","Streaming UIs need early fields: stream, but act only on the final validated object"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to get machine-readable output","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Prompt only","A prototype, any model","Production parsing","Brittle, needs repair loops"],["JSON mode","You need valid JSON and the shape is loose","Field names and types must match","Valid JSON can still miss fields"],["Structured outputs (strict schema)","The shape must match exactly","The schema exceeds platform limits","Schema design effort, refusal handling"],["Strict tool or function calling","Output is arguments for an action","No action is taken","Tool overhead in tokens"],["Constrained decoding in self-hosted stacks","You run open-weight models","You use a hosted API","Operational burden"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Azure rejects a schema that works on OpenAI","cause":"Azure allows 100 properties and 5 levels and drops several string keywords","fix":"Check the Azure limits page, flatten or split the schema, and move rules into application validation"},{"symptom":"Parsing fails on a long answer","cause":"The output cap cut the JSON mid-object","fix":"Check for an incomplete status first and raise the cap or shrink the schema"},{"symptom":"A field is empty because the model refused","cause":"A safety refusal replaces the schema output","fix":"Branch on the refusal signal before you parse"},{"symptom":"An optional field breaks the schema","cause":"Strict mode needs every field in `required`","fix":"Model optional fields as a union with `null`"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t08-s1","title":"A large schema on Azure","situation":"A contract-extraction schema has 260 properties across 7 nesting levels. It works on the OpenAI API but Azure rejects it.","constraints":["Azure OpenAI deployment is required","Every extracted field is needed","Accuracy is audited"],"unknowns":["Can fields be grouped into independent passes?","Which fields can be validated in code?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Raise a support ticket to lift the 100 property limit","verdict":"poor","why":"The limits are documented platform rules, not a setting you can raise.","tradeoff":"Asking is sensible only when a limit is not documented."},{"name":"Use JSON mode with the full schema in the prompt","verdict":"good","why":"No schema size limit, and fields are described in the prompt.","tradeoff":"Shape is no longer guaranteed, so you need validation and retries."},{"name":"Split extraction into several strict-schema passes under 100 properties and 5 levels, and merge in code","verdict":"best","why":"It keeps guaranteed shape per pass and fits the Azure limits [[r33]].","tradeoff":"More calls, higher cost and a merge step to test."}],"flips":[{"if":"The deployment can move to the OpenAI API","then":"One strict schema fits within 5,000 properties and 10 levels."},{"if":"Latency is tight","then":"Run the passes in parallel."}],"takeaway":"Know the platform's schema limits before you design the schema."},{"id":"w01-t08-s2","title":"Rare broken parses at volume","situation":"A prompt-only extractor processes 500,000 documents a day. About 2% of outputs fail to parse, and a repair loop retries them.","constraints":["High volume","Retries add cost and latency","OpenAI API, non-fine-tuned model"],"unknowns":["Are failures truncations or malformed JSON?","What is the cost of a retry?"],"question":"What is the best change?","options":[{"name":"Switch to structured outputs with a strict schema, and handle refusal and incomplete status explicitly","verdict":"best","why":"It removes malformed output and the repair loop, and the remaining failures are visible signals you can route.","tradeoff":"One-time schema design and tests."},{"name":"Lower the temperature and keep the repair loop","verdict":"good","why":"Reduces some randomness and is a small change.","tradeoff":"Does not guarantee shape, and retries stay in the pipeline."},{"name":"Switch to JSON mode","verdict":"poor","why":"It fixes syntax but not field names or types, so downstream failures remain.","tradeoff":"It suits loose shapes or models without schema support."}],"flips":[{"if":"Failures are truncations","then":"Raise the output cap or shorten the schema before changing format."},{"if":"The model lacks structured output support","then":"Use JSON mode plus a validator and bounded retries."}],"takeaway":"Guarantee the shape in the API, then route the rest."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t08-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does JSON mode guarantee?","options":["The output matches your schema","All fields are present","The output is factually correct","The output is syntactically valid JSON"],"answer":3,"explain":"JSON mode guarantees valid JSON only. Schema adherence needs structured outputs, and neither guarantees truth."},{"id":"w01-t08-q2","type":"multi","tag":"bug","q":"Which are true for strict structured outputs on OpenAI?","options":["All fields must be required","`additionalProperties: false` is mandatory","The root can be `anyOf`","Optional fields are modelled as a union with `null`"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Strict mode needs every field required and `additionalProperties: false`, and optional fields become a union with null. The root must be an object, not `anyOf`."},{"id":"w01-t08-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the checks your code should make on a structured response.","items":["Check for refusal","Check for incomplete status","Parse the JSON","Validate business rules"],"explain":"A refusal or truncation makes parsing meaningless, so check them first. Parse, then apply rules that a schema cannot express."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"JSON mode vs structured outputs?","back":"JSON mode guarantees valid JSON only. Structured outputs with `strict: true` guarantee the output follows your schema."},{"front":"Azure vs OpenAI schema limits?","back":"Azure allows 100 object properties and 5 nesting levels. OpenAI allows 5,000 properties and 10 levels."},{"front":"How do you model an optional field in strict mode?","back":"Keep it in `required` and make its type a union with `null`."},{"front":"Where do Anthropic structured outputs live?","back":"`output_config.format` with `type: \"json_schema\"`, plus `strict: true` on tools for schema-valid tool inputs."}],"related":["w01-t09","w01-t13","w01-l4","w08-t05","w06-t01"],"refs":["r8","r33","r32","r12"]},{"id":"w01-t09","order":9,"group":"Structured interaction","title":"Function and tool calling","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"The model returns a tool call with JSON arguments, your code runs it, and you send the result back. Strict schemas, tool_choice and loop limits decide how reliable that cycle is.","useIf":"The model must fetch data or take actions, and you need arguments that your code can trust.","avoidIf":"A single structured answer is enough. A schema-constrained output needs no tool loop.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"You describe each **tool** with a name, a description and a JSON schema. The model never runs anything. It returns a `function_call` item (Responses) or a tool call (Chat) with arguments, **your application** executes it, and you return the result tagged with the `call_id`. The loop repeats until the model answers in text [[r9]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w01-t09-d1","title":"The tool-calling loop","alt":"The app sends messages and tool schemas to the model, which returns a tool call. The app runs the tool and returns the result to the model, which produces the final answer.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant App\n  participant Model\n  participant Tool\n  App->>Model: messages and tool schemas\n  Model-->>App: tool call with arguments\n  App->>Tool: run with arguments\n  Tool-->>App: result or error\n  App->>Model: result with call id\n  Model-->>App: final answer"},{"t":"table","caption":"Controls (check date 2026-10-13)","head":["Control","OpenAI","Anthropic"],"compact":true,"rows":[["Choice","`tool_choice`: `auto` (default), `required`, a named function, `allowed_tools`, `none`","`tool_choice`: `auto`, `any`, `tool`, `none`"],["Parallel calls","`parallel_tool_calls: false` gives zero or one call","`disable_parallel_tool_use`, default `false`"],["Strict arguments","`strict: true` recommended. Chat stays non-strict by default","`strict: true` on the tool"],["Limits","Responses `max_tool_calls`","Forced `any` or `tool` returns 400 on Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1, and with manual extended thinking [[r50]]"]]},{"t":"p","md":"In Responses, omitting `strict` makes the API try to normalise the schema to strict and fall back to non-strict, which then shows `strict: false` [[r9]]. A fine-tuned model that makes several calls in one turn loses strict mode. **Custom tools** take free text instead of JSON. Definitions count as input tokens, the guide suggests fewer than 20 functions at the start of a turn, and `tool_search` with `defer_loading` loads large catalogs on demand on gpt-5.4 and later."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Reasoning plus tools needs Responses on newer OpenAI models","md":"From GPT-5.4, Chat Completions does not support tool calling with `reasoning_effort` other than `none`. GPT-6 Astra and 6.1 Sol need Responses for tools, and Azure `gpt-5.6-sol` fails with \"Function tools with reasoning_effort are not supported\" even when you omit effort, because the default is `medium` [[r6]] [[r2]] [[r15]]."},{"t":"p","md":"GPT-6 adds **async tool calling**: set `async: true` on a tool and the model keeps working while your application runs it. You return the result with the original `call_id`, and your application still runs the tool [[tr6]]."}],"whereToUse":["Retrieval from private systems, such as order status, tickets or inventory","Actions: create a ticket, send a message, update a record, with approval gates","Calculators and code execution for tasks models do poorly in text","Agent loops that plan, call tools and read results","Wrapping an internal API with a validated argument schema"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The answer needs live data or a side effect","You can describe each tool with a clear schema and purpose","You can validate arguments and authorise inside the tool"],"avoid":["Large catalogs loaded at once, which waste tokens and confuse selection","Letting the model decide authorisation, since tools must check permissions themselves","Unbounded loops with no iteration cap"],"flips":["Catalog exceeds about 20 tools: use `tool_search` with `defer_loading` or a pre-selection step","Reasoning plus tools on a newer OpenAI model: use Responses","A tool takes minutes: use async tool calling or a task pattern"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to connect a model to capabilities","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Native function calling","A few tools, one application","Many apps share the same tools","Schemas live in each app"],["Built-in tools (web search, file search, code interpreter)","The platform already offers the capability","You need custom systems","Platform coupling and extra charges"],["MCP tools","Tools are shared across hosts and teams","A single private function suffices","Server operations and auth"],["Structured output only","You want a typed answer, no side effect","The model needs fresh data","No retrieval or action"],["Hard-coded workflow","Steps are known and fixed","Steps depend on what the model finds","Rigid code"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Arguments fail validation in some calls","cause":"Strict mode is off, or a fine-tuned model lost it after multi-call turns","fix":"Set `strict: true`, validate again in code and return errors as tool output"},{"symptom":"The model picks the wrong tool from a long list","cause":"Many similar tools and long descriptions crowd the context","fix":"Shrink the catalog, sharpen descriptions, or load tools on demand with `tool_search`"},{"symptom":"Forced tool choice returns 400 on a recent Claude model","cause":"`any` and `tool` are not accepted on Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1","fix":"Use `auto` with a strict tool or structured outputs"},{"symptom":"The loop runs for dozens of calls","cause":"No iteration cap and tool errors are swallowed","fix":"Cap calls, return concise errors, and stop on repeated failures"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t09-s1","title":"A catalog of 120 internal tools","situation":"An internal assistant exposes 120 tools. Selection errors rise and every request pays for the full list of definitions.","constraints":["Tools vary widely by team","Model supports `tool_search` (gpt-5.4 or later)","Cost and accuracy both matter"],"unknowns":["How often are most tools unused?","Can tools be grouped by task?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Use `tool_search` with `defer_loading` so only relevant tools are loaded per turn","verdict":"best","why":"It cuts input tokens and confusion, and the guide recommends it for large catalogs on gpt-5.4 and later [[r9]].","tradeoff":"Adds a search step, and weak tool descriptions make search miss."},{"name":"Send all 120 tools and lower temperature","verdict":"poor","why":"Definitions stay in every request and temperature does not fix selection.","tradeoff":"Passing every tool is fine for a catalog under about 20."},{"name":"Pre-select a subset with your own retriever before each call","verdict":"good","why":"Works on any model and you control the logic.","tradeoff":"You build and maintain the selector and its evals."}],"flips":[{"if":"The model does not support `tool_search`","then":"Use your own pre-selection by task or embeddings."},{"if":"Tools are shared across hosts","then":"Consider MCP with a gateway."}],"takeaway":"Load only the tools a turn needs."},{"id":"w01-t09-s2","title":"Forcing structured behaviour on a recent Claude model","situation":"A workflow sets `tool_choice` to a named tool so the model always returns structured data. After moving to Claude Sonnet 5.5 every call returns 400.","constraints":["Output must always be structured","Claude Sonnet 5.5","No side effects are needed"],"unknowns":["Is a tool needed at all, or only a typed answer?"],"question":"How do you fix it?","options":[{"name":"Retry with `tool_choice: any`","verdict":"poor","why":"`any` and `tool` both return 400 on Sonnet 5.5.","tradeoff":"Forced choice still works on models that accept it."},{"name":"Use `tool_choice: auto` with a strict tool, or structured outputs through `output_config.format`","verdict":"best","why":"No side effect is needed, so structured outputs give a typed answer with schema validity and no forced choice [[r50]] [[r32]].","tradeoff":"You must design a schema and handle refusals."},{"name":"Pin the old model to avoid the change","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps current behaviour.","tradeoff":"Defers the migration to a retirement date."}],"flips":[{"if":"A real tool call must run","then":"Keep `auto`, a strict tool and a clear description."},{"if":"You must keep forced choice","then":"Use a model that accepts it, and document the dependency."}],"takeaway":"When no action is needed, use structured outputs, not a forced tool."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t09-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the steps of one tool-calling round trip.","items":["App sends messages and tool schemas","Model returns a tool call with arguments","App runs the tool","App returns the result with the call id","Model writes the final answer"],"explain":"The model only proposes calls. Your application executes them and returns results tagged with the call id before the model answers."},{"id":"w01-t09-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"Azure `gpt-5.6-sol` fails with 'Function tools with reasoning_effort are not supported' even though the request omits effort. Why?","options":["The default effort is `medium`, which Chat Completions cannot use with tools","The effort parameter is required","Strict mode is off","The tool list is too long"],"answer":0,"explain":"Omitting effort applies the model default of `medium`, and Chat Completions does not support tools with effort other than `none`. Use Responses or set effort to `none`."},{"id":"w01-t09-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which practices keep tool calling safe?","options":["Authorise inside each tool using the caller's identity","Trust the arguments because the schema is strict","Cap the number of tool calls","Return tool errors as text so the model can recover"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"Tools must check permissions, loops need caps and errors should be returned. A strict schema ensures shape, not that the arguments are safe."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Who executes a tool call?","back":"Your application. The model only returns the call, and you send back the result with the `call_id`."},{"front":"What does `parallel_tool_calls: false` do on OpenAI?","back":"The model makes zero or one tool call per turn."},{"front":"What is the OpenAI guidance for the number of tools?","back":"Fewer than 20 functions at the start of a turn (soft advice). Use `tool_search` with `defer_loading` for large catalogs on gpt-5.4 and later."}],"related":["w01-t08","w01-t11","w08-t02","w08-t15","w08-t17","w01-l4"],"refs":["r9","r6","r2","r15","r50","r32","tr6"]},{"id":"w01-t10","order":10,"group":"API styles","title":"Chat Completions API","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Chat Completions takes an array of role-tagged messages, returns choices, and keeps no state: your application sends the full history on every call.","useIf":"You target many OpenAI-compatible vendors, run non-reasoning models, or want to own conversation state.","avoidIf":"You need reasoning models with tools on recent OpenAI models, built-in tools or server-held state. Use the Responses API.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **Chat Completions** request carries a `model` and a `messages` array. Each message has a role such as `system`, `user`, `assistant` or `tool`. The response holds one or more `choices`. The API is **stateless**: nothing is remembered between calls, so your code resends the system prompt and the conversation each time and decides what to trim. OpenAI says Chat Completions stays supported while Responses is recommended for all new projects [[r6]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"A minimal request","code":"{\n  \"model\": \"gpt-6-sol\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    { \"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Answer in one sentence.\" },\n    { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is a token?\" }\n  ],\n  \"max_completion_tokens\": 300\n}"},{"t":"flow","title":"Cost grows because history is resent","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Turn 1","md":"System, user 1. Pays for 2 messages."},{"title":"Turn 2","md":"System, user 1, reply 1, user 2. Pays for 4."},{"title":"Turn 3","md":"System, user 1, reply 1, user 2, reply 2, user 3. Pays for 6."}]},{"t":"table","caption":"What the Chat Completions body offers (check date 2026-10-13)","head":["Feature","Detail"],"compact":true,"rows":[["Choices","`n` returns several choices and bills for all of them"],["Stop and seed","`stop` up to 4 sequences (not on o3 and o4-mini), `seed` Beta"],["Penalties and bias","`frequency_penalty`, `presence_penalty` (-2.0 to 2.0), `logit_bias`"],["Logprobs","`logprobs` with `top_logprobs` 0 to 20"],["Output cap","`max_completion_tokens`. `max_tokens` is deprecated and not compatible with o-series"],["Reasoning and format","`reasoning_effort`, `verbosity`, `response_format`"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Chat completions are stored by default for new accounts, and `store: false` turns that off. The endpoint `/v1/chat/completions` is eligible for zero data retention [[r10]]. Limits on newer models: from GPT-5.4 tool calling is not supported with `reasoning_effort` other than `none`, GPT-6 Astra and 6.1 Sol need Responses for tools, and GPT-6 Sol and Luna allow function calling in Chat only with `none` [[r6]] [[r2]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Why portability matters","md":"Many vendors implement a Chat-style API, and the Azure v1 API also routes chat completions calls to other providers such as DeepSeek and Grok [[r14]]. Anthropic's OpenAI compatibility layer silently ignores several controls and is for testing, not production [[r36]]."}],"whereToUse":["Gateways and SDKs that must talk to several vendors through one message format","Non-reasoning models where simple stateless chat is enough","Applications that need `n`, `stop`, `seed`, penalties or `logit_bias`","Legacy code and tutorials, where migration is not yet justified","ZDR environments where your application controls what is stored"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You own conversation state and want to control trimming","Portability across OpenAI-compatible APIs matters more than platform features","The model is non-reasoning, or reasoning without tools"],"avoid":["Reasoning with tools on GPT-5.4 and later, GPT-6 Astra and 6.1 Sol","Built-in web search, file search, code interpreter, computer use or remote MCP","Background runs and server-held reasoning state"],"flips":["You adopt a reasoning model with tools: move the loop to Responses","You need built-in tools or durable server state: move to Responses","You must stay vendor-neutral: keep Chat behind an adapter and isolate platform features"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Chat Completions against its neighbours","head":["API","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Chat Completions","Portability, simple stateless chat, `seed`, penalties","Reasoning with tools, built-in tools","Your code owns state and trimming"],["Responses","New builds, reasoning plus tools, built-in tools, server state","You must target many OpenAI-compatible vendors","Items model to learn, retention choices"],["Anthropic Messages","Claude models, adaptive thinking, native `strict` tools","You need one format across vendors","Different body, stateless as well"],["Assistants API","Nothing, it is retired on OpenAI","Always","Sunset on 2026-08-26"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Cost and latency climb across a long chat","cause":"The full history is resent every turn","fix":"Trim or summarise old turns, keep a stable prefix for caching and cap tool output sizes"},{"symptom":"Tool calls fail after a model upgrade","cause":"Newer models do not support tools with reasoning effort other than `none` on Chat Completions","fix":"Use Responses, or set effort to `none` if reasoning is not needed"},{"symptom":"`max_tokens` is rejected on a reasoning model","cause":"`max_tokens` is deprecated and incompatible with o-series","fix":"Use `max_completion_tokens`"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t10-s1","title":"One endpoint, four providers","situation":"A platform team exposes one internal chat endpoint that routes to OpenAI, Azure and two open-weight hosts. Models are non-reasoning and applications keep their own history.","constraints":["Vendor neutrality is a requirement","Non-reasoning models","Apps own conversation state"],"unknowns":["Which controls do all providers honour?","Do any apps need built-in tools?"],"question":"Which interface do you standardise on?","options":[{"name":"Route Claude traffic through Anthropic's OpenAI compatibility layer in production","verdict":"poor","why":"The layer ignores `seed`, penalties, `logit_bias`, `response_format` and more, and Anthropic says it is for testing.","tradeoff":"Fine in a test harness to compare outputs quickly."},{"name":"A Chat-style message interface at the gateway, with per-vendor adapters and a documented list of controls that are honoured","verdict":"best","why":"Every vendor supports a message list, and adapters hide differences while the control list sets expectations.","tradeoff":"You maintain adapters and some features, such as server state, stay out of reach."},{"name":"Responses everywhere","verdict":"good","why":"Richer features on OpenAI and Azure.","tradeoff":"Other vendors may lack the API, so the gateway ends up translating anyway."}],"flips":[{"if":"Only OpenAI and Azure remain","then":"Standardise on Responses and use its built-in tools."},{"if":"A team needs reasoning with tools on GPT-6","then":"Give that route a Responses adapter."}],"takeaway":"Standardise on the common denominator and isolate platform features behind adapters."},{"id":"w01-t10-s2","title":"A 40-turn support chat that keeps getting dearer","situation":"A support chat averages 40 turns. Cost per conversation grows faster than linearly and late turns feel slower.","constraints":["Chat Completions, non-reasoning model","Quality must hold on late turns","Cost per conversation is monitored"],"unknowns":["Which turns carry facts that must stay verbatim?","How large are tool outputs in the history?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Move to `previous_response_id` expecting a lower bill","verdict":"poor","why":"Chained calls bill all earlier input tokens again, so cost does not fall.","tradeoff":"Chaining saves code and state handling, not tokens."},{"name":"Summarise old turns with a cheap model call and keep recent turns verbatim","verdict":"good","why":"Shrinks the history.","tradeoff":"Summaries are lossy and add a call."},{"name":"Keep a stable prefix for caching, trim stale turns, cap tool outputs and keep key facts in a pinned note","verdict":"best","why":"It cuts tokens, keeps the cache prefix intact and preserves the facts that matter.","tradeoff":"Needs rules for what is pinned and what is trimmed."}],"flips":[{"if":"Conversations must be audited in full","then":"Store the full history outside the prompt and send only the trimmed view."},{"if":"You adopt a reasoning model with tools","then":"Move to Responses and use compaction where available."}],"takeaway":"In a stateless API the history is your cost, so manage it deliberately."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t10-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does 'stateless' mean for Chat Completions?","options":["Your application sends the history on every call","The API stores the conversation for 30 days and replays it","The model has no memory of its training data","Responses are never stored"],"answer":0,"explain":"The API keeps no conversation state between calls, so your code resends what the model should see. Storage of completions is a separate setting."},{"id":"w01-t10-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Which are true about Chat Completions on newer OpenAI models?","options":["Tools with `reasoning_effort` other than `none` are not supported from GPT-5.4","GPT-6 Astra and 6.1 Sol need Responses for tools","`max_tokens` is the recommended cap on o-series","The endpoint is eligible for ZDR"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Tools with reasoning need Responses from GPT-5.4, and GPT-6 Astra and 6.1 Sol need Responses for tools. `max_tokens` is deprecated for o-series. `/v1/chat/completions` is ZDR eligible."},{"id":"w01-t10-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which need is the strongest reason to stay on Chat Completions?","options":["Built-in web search","Reasoning with tools on GPT-6 Astra","Targeting several OpenAI-compatible vendors with one format","Server-held conversation state"],"answer":2,"explain":"Portability is Chat's strength. The other needs favour Responses."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why does cost grow over a long Chat Completions conversation?","back":"The API is stateless, so you resend the full history each turn and pay for those input tokens again."},{"front":"Which cap parameter replaces `max_tokens` in Chat Completions?","back":"`max_completion_tokens`. `max_tokens` is deprecated and not compatible with o-series."},{"front":"When must you leave Chat Completions for Responses?","back":"For reasoning with tools on GPT-5.4 and later, for GPT-6 Astra and 6.1 Sol tools, and for built-in tools or server-held state."}],"related":["w01-t11","w01-t12","w01-l5","w01-t09","w16-t01"],"refs":["r5","r6","r2","r10","r14","r36"]},{"id":"w01-t11","order":11,"group":"API styles","title":"Responses API","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Responses takes and returns typed Items, can hold state on the server, and adds built-in tools, background runs and encrypted reasoning. OpenAI recommends it for new projects.","useIf":"You build new work on OpenAI or Azure OpenAI, use reasoning models with tools, or want built-in tools and server-held state.","avoidIf":"You must target many OpenAI-compatible vendors with one format, or a retention policy forbids server-held state and you have not designed a stateless path.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"`POST /v1/responses` works with **Items**, typed objects such as `message`, `reasoning`, `function_call` and `function_call_output`. The `output` array can hold several items, so never assume the text sits at index 0. System text goes in top-level `instructions`, and `text.format` replaces `response_format`. OpenAI reports, from internal tests, a 3% SWE-bench gain and 40% to 80% better cache use against Chat Completions (vendor claims) [[r6]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Three ways to carry state","head":["Option","How","Retention and cost notes"],"compact":true,"rows":[["Replay yourself","Send the items each turn, with `store: false` if needed","Works under ZDR. You manage trimming. Keep ordering stable for caching"],["`previous_response_id`","Reference the prior response","Responses are stored 30 days. Earlier input tokens bill again. `instructions` are not carried over"],["Conversations API","Attach a conversation object","Released 2025-08-20. No 30 day TTL. Not ZDR eligible [[r7]] [[r10]]"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Chaining with previous_response_id","code":"r1 = client.responses.create(\n    model=\"gpt-6-sol\",\n    instructions=\"Be brief.\",\n    input=\"Summarise this ticket: ...\",\n)\nr2 = client.responses.create(\n    model=\"gpt-6-sol\",\n    instructions=\"Be brief.\",  # not carried over, so repeat it\n    input=\"Draft a reply.\",\n    previous_response_id=r1.id,\n)","caption":"Billing counts the earlier input tokens again on the second call."},{"t":"p","md":"**Built-in tools** listed for GPT-6 Astra include `web_search`, `file_search`, `image_generation`, `code_interpreter`, `hosted_shell`, `apply_patch`, `skills`, `computer_use`, `mcp` and `tool_search` [[r1]]. On Azure, remote MCP needs explicit approval by default, Code Interpreter has extra charges and a container holds up to 1,000 files [[r34]]."},{"t":"p","md":"In **stateless mode** (`store: false`, or a ZDR organisation) reasoning items carry `encrypted_content`. Replay every output item, encrypted reasoning included, so the model keeps its reasoning context [[r3]]. Other parameters include `truncation` (`auto` or `disabled`, default `disabled`, which fails with 400 on overflow), `max_tool_calls`, `background`, `service_tier` and `prompt_cache_key` [[r4]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Store defaults surprise people","md":"Responses are stored by default, response objects live 30 days, and `store: false` does not mean nothing is kept: abuse monitoring logs can hold content up to 30 days unless ZDR or Modified Abuse Monitoring applies [[r10]]. `/v1/conversations` and `/v1/files` are not ZDR eligible."}],"whereToUse":["New agents on OpenAI or Azure OpenAI, especially reasoning models with tools","Assistants migrations: threads become Conversations and runs become Responses","Applications that want built-in web search, file search, code interpreter or remote MCP","Long-running jobs that use background mode","ZDR environments that replay items with encrypted reasoning"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Reasoning with tools on GPT-5.4 and later, GPT-6 Astra and 6.1 Sol","You want fewer lines of state code and can accept server-held history","You use built-in tools or background mode"],"avoid":["Cross-vendor gateways that need one message format","Server state when a no-retention policy applies and you have not planned a stateless replay","Assuming `output[0]` is the text answer"],"flips":["ZDR applies: set `store` false, replay items and keep encrypted reasoning","Threads must live for months with audit: use the Conversations API after a retention review","A short chat with low risk: `previous_response_id` is the least code"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Responses against its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Responses","Reasoning plus tools, built-in tools, server state","One format across many vendors","Items model and retention choices to learn"],["Chat Completions","Portability and simple stateless chat","Reasoning with tools on newer models","You own all state"],["Anthropic Messages","Claude features, adaptive thinking","You need server-side state","Stateless, your app holds history"],["Assistants API","Nothing","Always","Sunset on 2026-08-26"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Code reads empty text from `output[0]`","cause":"`output` can start with a reasoning or tool item","fix":"Iterate items and pick the `message` item, or use the SDK's output text helper"},{"symptom":"The second turn ignores the system prompt","cause":"`previous_response_id` does not carry over `instructions`","fix":"Send `instructions` on every call"},{"symptom":"Reasoning context is lost in a ZDR deployment","cause":"Encrypted reasoning items were not replayed","fix":"Replay every output item, including `encrypted_content`"},{"symptom":"A stored conversation breaks a no-retention policy","cause":"The Conversations API is not ZDR eligible","fix":"Use stateless replay with `store: false`, or change the policy knowingly"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t11-s1","title":"A ZDR organisation builds a reasoning agent","situation":"A company with zero data retention builds a multi-turn agent on a reasoning model with tools. Policy forbids storing conversation content with the provider.","constraints":["ZDR applies","Reasoning model with tools","Conversation spans many turns"],"unknowns":["How large do histories grow?","Is compaction allowed under the policy?"],"question":"How do you manage state?","options":[{"name":"Use the Conversations API for durable threads","verdict":"poor","why":"The Conversations API is not ZDR eligible, so it conflicts with the policy.","tradeoff":"It suits long-lived threads when no-retention rules do not apply."},{"name":"Replay only message and function items and drop the reasoning items","verdict":"good","why":"Simpler to store and it satisfies ZDR.","tradeoff":"The model loses its reasoning context between turns, so multi-step quality can fall."},{"name":"Use `store: false`, keep the history in your own store, and replay every output item including encrypted reasoning","verdict":"best","why":"It satisfies ZDR and keeps the model's reasoning context intact across turns.","tradeoff":"You manage trimming, ordering for caching and storage security."}],"flips":[{"if":"The policy allows provider-side storage","then":"`previous_response_id` or Conversations reduces code."},{"if":"Histories outgrow the window","then":"Add compaction, and check that it works with `store: false`."}],"takeaway":"Under ZDR you own the state and must replay encrypted reasoning."},{"id":"w01-t11-s2","title":"A short chat widget with low risk","situation":"A marketing site adds a chat widget. Sessions are about five turns, content is public, and a small team wants the least code.","constraints":["Low risk, public content","Five turns per session","Small team"],"unknowns":["Is a 30 day provider retention acceptable?","Is the cost of re-billed input tokens small?"],"question":"Which state approach do you choose?","options":[{"name":"`previous_response_id` with `instructions` sent every call","verdict":"best","why":"It is the least code, sessions are short so re-billed input is small, and 30 day retention is acceptable for public content.","tradeoff":"Earlier input tokens are billed again, and the chain is tied to stored responses."},{"name":"Conversations API","verdict":"good","why":"Durable and simple.","tradeoff":"Adds an object to manage and has no TTL, which is more than a five turn widget needs."},{"name":"Replay all items yourself with `store: false`","verdict":"poor","why":"More code and state handling for a case with no retention constraint.","tradeoff":"Right when ZDR applies or you want to control trimming and caching."}],"flips":[{"if":"Sessions grow to hundreds of turns","then":"Add compaction or trimming, and consider replaying yourself."},{"if":"Content becomes sensitive","then":"Review retention and move to stateless replay."}],"takeaway":"Take the least code that meets the retention rules."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t11-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A second call chained with `previous_response_id` ignores the system prompt. What is the cause?","options":["`store` was false","The model changed","The response expired after 24 hours","`instructions` are not carried over from the previous response"],"answer":3,"explain":"`previous_response_id` does not carry top-level `instructions`, so send them on each call. Responses live 30 days, not 24 hours."},{"id":"w01-t11-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which statements about retention are correct?","options":["Response objects are kept 30 days by default","The Conversations API is not ZDR eligible","`store: false` guarantees no provider logs","ZDR forces `store` to false"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Responses keep 30 days, Conversations are not ZDR eligible and ZDR forces `store` false. Abuse monitoring logs can keep content up to 30 days unless ZDR or Modified Abuse Monitoring applies, so `store: false` alone is not a guarantee."},{"id":"w01-t11-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match the Items to their role.","pairs":[{"l":"`message`","r":"Text from the user or assistant"},{"l":"`reasoning`","r":"Model thinking, replayable as encrypted content when stateless"},{"l":"`function_call`","r":"A tool call proposed by the model"},{"l":"`function_call_output`","r":"Your tool result sent back with the call id"}],"explain":"These four Items form the core loop of a tool-using conversation in Responses."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is an Item in the Responses API?","back":"A typed object such as `message`, `reasoning`, `function_call` or `function_call_output`. `output` can contain several, so never assume text is at index 0."},{"front":"What happens to `instructions` with `previous_response_id`?","back":"They are not carried over. Send them on every call."},{"front":"What do you replay in stateless mode?","back":"Every output item, including reasoning items with `encrypted_content`, so the model keeps its reasoning context."},{"front":"Is the Conversations API ZDR eligible?","back":"No. It was released on 2025-08-20, has no 30 day TTL and conflicts with no-retention policies."}],"related":["w01-t10","w01-t12","w01-t13","w01-l5","w11-t01"],"refs":["r6","r7","r10","r1","r34","r3","r4"]},{"id":"w01-t12","order":12,"group":"API styles","title":"Chat Completions vs Responses: choosing and migrating","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Choose the API from four dials: model family, state ownership, portability and retention. Migrating means changing message shapes, reading output Items and deciding where state lives.","useIf":"You pick an API for a new build, plan a migration, or map an Assistants or Azure app to current APIs.","avoidIf":"You treat Responses as a renamed Chat Completions. It changes state, tools, reasoning handling and several parameters.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Four dials decide the choice. **Model family**: reasoning models with tools on GPT-5.4 and later need Responses. **State ownership**: your app resends history (Chat Completions), or the server holds it (Responses with `previous_response_id` or Conversations). **Portability**: Chat-style messages are the common format across vendors. **Retention**: server state conflicts with a no-retention policy unless you replay statelessly [[r6]] [[r7]] [[r10]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Equivalent concepts (check date 2026-10-13)","head":["Concept","Chat Completions","Responses","Anthropic Messages"],"compact":true,"rows":[["Input","`messages` array","`input` string or Items","`messages` with typed content blocks"],["System text","A `system` message","Top-level `instructions`","Top-level `system`"],["Structured output","`response_format`","`text.format`","`output_config.format`"],["Output cap","`max_completion_tokens`","`max_output_tokens`","`max_tokens` (required)"],["Stop signal","`finish_reason`","`status`, `incomplete_details`","`stop_reason`"],["State","None","`store`, `previous_response_id`, Conversations","None, resend history"],["Reasoning control","`reasoning_effort`","`reasoning.effort`","`output_config.effort`, adaptive thinking"]]},{"t":"flow","title":"Migration steps from Chat Completions to Responses","direction":"v","steps":[{"title":"1. Reshape input","md":"Move the `system` message to `instructions` and the rest to `input`."},{"title":"2. Rename formats","md":"`response_format` becomes `text.format`. Read all `output` Items, not index 0."},{"title":"3. Port the tool loop","md":"Handle `function_call` and `function_call_output` Items. Check strict behaviour."},{"title":"4. Choose state","md":"Replay yourself, `previous_response_id`, or Conversations. Review retention."},{"title":"5. Drop what is gone","md":"Responses has no `seed`, `stop`, `n`, penalties, `logit_bias` or `top_k`. Move to prompts, schemas and tests."},{"title":"6. Re-evaluate","md":"Run your evals. Caching, reasoning context and tool behaviour can shift results."}]},{"t":"p","md":"**Azure**: since August 2025 you can opt in to the v1 API. Use the `OpenAI()` client with a base URL ending `/openai/v1`, and `api-version` is no longer required. Azure recommends Responses for Azure OpenAI models and keeps responses 30 days with deletion by ID [[r14]] [[r34]]. **Anthropic**: Messages is stateless, `max_tokens` is required, content is typed blocks (`text`, `tool_use`, `tool_result`, `thinking`) and caching uses `cache_control` [[r19]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"The Assistants API is gone on OpenAI","md":"Deprecation was announced on 2025-08-26 and the API was sunset on 2026-08-26. Threads map to Conversations and Runs to Responses. The migration guide maps Assistants to Prompts, but reusable prompts are themselves deprecated with shutdown on 2026-11-30, so hold instructions and tools in your code or config. Azure OpenAI Assistants are documented to retire on 2026-08-26 with migration to Foundry Agent Service. Azure status after that date is unverified [[r51]] [[r11]] [[r49]]."}],"whereToUse":["Architecture reviews that choose the API for a new assistant or agent","Migration plans from Chat Completions or Assistants","Multi-cloud designs that include Azure OpenAI and Claude","Retention reviews, where state placement matters","Interviews: justify an API choice from dials, not preference"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Responses for new builds, reasoning plus tools, built-in tools, background jobs and server state","Chat Completions for portability, simple stateless chat and `seed` or penalties","Anthropic Messages for Claude features, behind an adapter if you are multi-vendor"],"avoid":["Migrating everything at once without evals","Standardising on Responses when most traffic goes to vendors that lack it","Depending on Assistants or reusable prompts"],"flips":["A model upgrade breaks tools with reasoning on Chat: move that route to Responses","A no-retention policy appears: switch to stateless replay with encrypted reasoning","You drop all non-OpenAI vendors: simplify to Responses"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Choosing the API surface","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Chat Completions","Portability, simple stateless chat","Reasoning with tools on newer models","You own state; features lag"],["Responses","New work, tools with reasoning, built-in tools","Many vendors need one format","Migration effort, retention choices"],["Anthropic Messages","Claude 1M window, adaptive thinking","You need one SDK shape across vendors","Separate body and rules"],["Assistants API","Nothing, retired on 2026-08-26","Always","Broken app"],["Gateway with adapters","Several vendors and teams","Single vendor, small team","You maintain adapters and a control matrix"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Migrated code returns empty text","cause":"It reads `output[0]`, which may be a reasoning or tool Item","fix":"Iterate Items or use the SDK helper for output text"},{"symptom":"Behaviour changes after migration","cause":"`seed`, `stop`, penalties and `logit_bias` do not exist in Responses","fix":"Replace them with prompts, schemas and repeated-run tests"},{"symptom":"Costs rise after adopting `previous_response_id`","cause":"All earlier input tokens are billed again","fix":"Trim, compact or cache a stable prefix, and measure per conversation"},{"symptom":"Old Azure samples fail or look wrong","cause":"They use `AzureOpenAI()` with dated `api-version` strings","fix":"Use the v1 API with `OpenAI()` and a `/openai/v1` base URL"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t12-s1","title":"An Assistants app after the sunset","situation":"A support product built on the OpenAI Assistants API (threads, runs, a stored assistant with tools) starts failing after 2026-08-26.","constraints":["OpenAI platform","Existing threads hold history","Tools must keep working"],"unknowns":["How many threads need to be carried over?","Where do instructions live today?"],"question":"What is the migration path?","options":[{"name":"Move to Responses, map threads to Conversations and runs to Responses, and keep instructions and tool definitions in code or config","verdict":"best","why":"It follows the documented mapping and avoids Prompts, which are deprecated with a 2026-11-30 shutdown.","tradeoff":"You rebuild the run loop and test tool behaviour."},{"name":"Map each assistant to a reusable Prompt","verdict":"good","why":"It is the documented mapping in the migration guide.","tradeoff":"Reusable prompts shut down on 2026-11-30, so you migrate twice."},{"name":"Keep calling the Assistants API and wait for a notice","verdict":"poor","why":"OpenAI sunset it on 2026-08-26 and says it is no longer available.","tradeoff":"Waiting is only reasonable on a platform with a later published date."}],"flips":[{"if":"The app runs on Azure","then":"Check Azure's current notice and consider Foundry Agent Service, since post-date status is unverified."},{"if":"Retention policy forbids stored conversations","then":"Replay statelessly instead of using Conversations."}],"takeaway":"Move state to Conversations and keep definitions in your own code."},{"id":"w01-t12-s2","title":"One feature needs GPT-6 tools, the rest is portable","situation":"A product calls several vendors through Chat Completions. One new feature needs web search and tools with a GPT-6 reasoning model.","constraints":["Most traffic is portable chat","One feature needs built-in web search and reasoning with tools","Team of six"],"unknowns":["How much traffic does the new feature carry?","Will other vendors gain similar tools?"],"question":"How do you proceed?","options":[{"name":"Rewrite the whole product on Responses at once","verdict":"poor","why":"It puts portable traffic at risk without evals and does not help vendors that lack Responses.","tradeoff":"A full move is right when OpenAI is the only vendor."},{"name":"Add a Responses path for the new feature behind an adapter and keep Chat elsewhere","verdict":"best","why":"It isolates platform features, limits risk and keeps portability for the rest.","tradeoff":"Two code paths to maintain and test."},{"name":"Stay on Chat Completions with effort `none` and write your own search tool","verdict":"good","why":"One API and no migration.","tradeoff":"You give up reasoning and built-in tools, and you own the search tool."}],"flips":[{"if":"The new feature becomes most of the traffic","then":"Move the main path to Responses."},{"if":"Quality tests show reasoning adds nothing","then":"The Chat plus own tool path is cheaper to run."}],"takeaway":"Isolate platform-specific features behind an adapter."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t12-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each Assistants concept to its replacement.","pairs":[{"l":"Threads","r":"Conversations"},{"l":"Runs","r":"Responses"},{"l":"Assistants","r":"Prompts (reusable prompts, themselves deprecated)"},{"l":"Azure OpenAI Assistants","r":"Foundry Agent Service"}],"explain":"OpenAI's migration guide maps threads to Conversations, runs to Responses and assistants to Prompts, which are also deprecated. Azure documents migration to Foundry Agent Service."},{"id":"w01-t12-q2","type":"multi","tag":"bug","q":"Which Chat Completions parameters have no equivalent in the Responses body?","options":["`seed`","`stop`","`temperature`","`logit_bias`"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Responses has `temperature` but no `seed`, `stop`, `n`, penalties, `logit_bias` or `top_k`."},{"id":"w01-t12-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which change most strongly pushes a Chat Completions agent to Responses?","options":["Lower latency targets","Adopting GPT-5.4 or later with reasoning effort above `none` and tools","A larger team","A need for `seed`"],"answer":1,"explain":"From GPT-5.4, Chat Completions does not support tools with reasoning effort other than `none`. `seed` exists only on Chat."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the four dials for choosing Chat Completions or Responses.","back":"Model family, state ownership, portability across vendors and data retention."},{"front":"When was the OpenAI Assistants API sunset?","back":"2026-08-26, after deprecation was announced on 2025-08-26. Threads map to Conversations and Runs to Responses."},{"front":"How do you call Azure OpenAI with the v1 API?","back":"Use the `OpenAI()` client with a base URL ending `/openai/v1`. `api-version` is no longer required."}],"related":["w01-t10","w01-t11","w01-l5","w00-t05","w11-t01"],"refs":["r6","r7","r10","r14","r34","r19","r51","r11","r49"]},{"id":"w01-t13","order":13,"group":"API styles","title":"Streaming and background mode","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Streaming sends tokens as events so users see output early. Background mode runs a long call on the server while you poll. Batch handles bulk offline work.","useIf":"A user waits on a response, a call outlasts an HTTP timeout, or you process large volumes offline.","avoidIf":"You act on partial output. Never execute a tool call or parse JSON before the item is complete.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Streaming** returns server-sent events as the model generates. OpenAI Responses emits `response.created`, `response.in_progress`, `response.output_item.added`, `response.content_part.added`, repeated `response.output_text.delta`, then `response.output_text.done`, `response.content_part.done`, `response.output_item.done`, `response.completed`, and `error` on failure [[tr1]]. Anthropic sends `message_start`, then per block `content_block_start`, `content_block_delta` (`text_delta`, `input_json_delta`, `thinking_delta`) and `content_block_stop`, then `message_delta` with cumulative usage and `message_stop`. A `ping` can appear anywhere [[tr2]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w01-t13-d1","title":"Responses stream, in order","alt":"Events flow from response created through item added, content part added, repeated text deltas, text done, item done and response completed.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[response.created] --> B[output_item.added]\n  B --> C[content_part.added]\n  C --> D[output_text.delta x many]\n  D --> E[output_text.done]\n  E --> F[output_item.done]\n  F --> G[response.completed]"},{"t":"p","md":"Anthropic streams tool input one complete key and value at a time, so pauses are normal and you should not treat them as stalls. Buffer deltas and act when the item or block is done."},{"t":"p","md":"**Background mode** sets `background: true`. The call returns at once and you poll while the status is `queued` or `in_progress` [[r35]]. Under zero data retention a background response runs with `store=false` and its data sits on disk for roughly 10 minutes so you can poll, so fetch and persist the result promptly. Projects with Modified Abuse Monitoring keep a background response beyond polling only if `store=true` is set explicitly. On Azure, cancel is idempotent, you can resume a stream with `sequence_number`, and combining background with streaming has a known performance issue [[r34]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Pick by who is waiting","md":"A person watching: stream. A request that exceeds a timeout: background and poll. Nobody waiting: Batch at 50% of the Standard price with a 24 hour window, separate rate limits and no streaming on Anthropic batches [[r37]] [[r39]]."}],"whereToUse":["Chat and assistant UIs, to cut perceived latency with earlier first tokens","Voice and live captions, where partial text drives the next step","Deep research or long reasoning calls that run for minutes","Web apps behind gateways with short HTTP timeouts","Bulk nightly processing with Batch instead of per-request calls"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Streaming when a person reads output as it appears","Background mode when a single call may outlast a timeout","Batch when results can wait up to 24 hours"],"avoid":["Acting on partial tool arguments or partial JSON","Streaming where a downstream system needs only the final object","Background mode for bulk work that Batch can do at half price"],"flips":["A ZDR project uses background mode: poll within the roughly 10 minute window and store the result yourself","Users disconnect often: use background with polling instead of one long stream","Streaming plus background on Azure shows slowdowns: poll without streaming"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to deliver a long or large response","head":["Mode","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Streaming","A person is waiting","The result is machine-consumed","More client code, partial-state bugs"],["Non-streaming call","Short outputs, simple code","Long outputs or UIs","User waits for the whole answer"],["Background plus polling","Calls longer than timeouts, resumable work","You need instant feedback","Polling logic, retention rules"],["Batch API","Offline bulk, cost sensitive","Interactive use","Up to 24 hours, 50% discount, separate quota"],["Flex processing","Non-urgent work at Batch rates","Capacity must be certain","Beta, may be unavailable [[r38]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"An email is sent with an incomplete address","cause":"The app acted on tool arguments before the call finished streaming","fix":"Execute tools only after the item is done and the arguments validate"},{"symptom":"A stream seems stalled during a tool call on Claude","cause":"Tool input arrives one complete key and value at a time","fix":"Wait for `content_block_stop`, and keep a heartbeat timeout longer than the pauses"},{"symptom":"A background result is gone under ZDR","cause":"Data stays on disk only for roughly 10 minutes for polling","fix":"Poll early and write the result to your own store"},{"symptom":"Background plus streaming feels slow on Azure","cause":"A known performance issue when the two combine","fix":"Use background with polling, or stream without background"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t13-s1","title":"A streaming chat that also calls tools","situation":"A chat UI streams text and the model sometimes calls a tool that sends an email. Users complain that the first token is slow, and an incident sent an email with a truncated address.","constraints":["Interactive UI","A tool has side effects","Streaming is wanted for first-token speed"],"unknowns":["Which tools have side effects?","Is a confirmation step acceptable?"],"question":"How do you design the stream handling?","options":[{"name":"Run the tool as soon as its arguments start to arrive, to save time","verdict":"poor","why":"Partial arguments caused the incident. A tool must not run before the call item is complete.","tradeoff":"Prefetching read-only data from partial hints can be safe when it has no side effects."},{"name":"Stream text to the UI, buffer tool calls, validate arguments after the item is done, then execute with a confirmation for side effects","verdict":"best","why":"It keeps the fast first token and removes the partial-action risk.","tradeoff":"A short delay before tools run and more state handling."},{"name":"Turn streaming off whenever tools are enabled","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and safe.","tradeoff":"Users lose the early tokens, so perceived latency rises."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tools are read-only","then":"You can start fetching earlier, still validating before use."},{"if":"The consumer is a program","then":"Skip streaming and take the final object."}],"takeaway":"Stream for humans, act on completed items only."},{"id":"w01-t13-s2","title":"A 20 minute research run behind a 60 second gateway","situation":"A web app runs a deep research call that takes about 20 minutes. The load balancer cuts connections at 60 seconds. The company is under zero data retention.","constraints":["Gateway timeout is 60 seconds","ZDR applies","A user wants the result in the same session"],"unknowns":["Can the UI show progress through polling?","Where can results be stored?"],"question":"What do you build?","options":[{"name":"Hold one streaming connection open for 20 minutes","verdict":"poor","why":"The gateway cuts it at 60 seconds, so the run is lost or must restart.","tradeoff":"Long streams work only when no intermediary enforces a short timeout."},{"name":"Submit through Batch and notify later","verdict":"good","why":"Cheaper and resilient.","tradeoff":"Up to 24 hours is too slow for a user waiting in session."},{"name":"Use `background: true`, poll for status with a progress UI, and persist the result to your own store within the retention window","verdict":"best","why":"It survives the timeout, and storing the result yourself respects ZDR's short polling window.","tradeoff":"You build polling, error handling and a result store."}],"flips":[{"if":"The user can come back tomorrow","then":"Batch halves the price."},{"if":"No ZDR and results may live with the provider","then":"Keep `store=true` and poll the stored response."}],"takeaway":"Timeouts push long work to background and polling."},{"id":"w01-t13-s3","title":"Two million documents overnight","situation":"A nightly job classifies two million short documents. Results are needed by morning.","constraints":["Offline","Cost sensitive","Documents are independent"],"unknowns":["Does the policy allow Batch for this data?","Is the 50,000 request limit per file acceptable?"],"question":"Which delivery mode do you use?","options":[{"name":"Batch API, split into files within the request and size limits","verdict":"best","why":"It gives a 50% discount, a separate rate-limit pool and a 24 hour window that fits the morning deadline [[r37]].","tradeoff":"No interactive feedback, and OpenAI batches are not ZDR eligible."},{"name":"Background mode for each document","verdict":"poor","why":"It targets long single calls, and two million polls add load without a discount.","tradeoff":"Background fits a few long calls that exceed a timeout."},{"name":"Synchronous calls with high concurrency","verdict":"good","why":"Fast and simple.","tradeoff":"Standard price, rate-limit pressure and retry handling."}],"flips":[{"if":"The data is under ZDR","then":"Batch is not eligible, so use synchronous calls with careful rate limiting."},{"if":"Results are needed within minutes","then":"Use synchronous calls or a priority tier."}],"takeaway":"When nobody waits, batch it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t13-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order these OpenAI Responses stream events.","items":["response.created","response.output_item.added","response.output_text.delta","response.output_text.done","response.completed"],"explain":"A response is created, an item is added, text deltas arrive, the text completes and then the response completes."},{"id":"w01-t13-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A Claude stream pauses for several seconds while a tool call is being generated. What is the likely cause?","options":["Tool input arrives one complete key and value at a time","The connection dropped","The context window is full","`ping` events stopped"],"answer":0,"explain":"Anthropic streams tool input one complete key and value at a time, so pauses are normal. A `ping` event may also appear in any position."},{"id":"w01-t13-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which practices make streaming with tools safe?","options":["Run side-effect tools only after the item is complete and validated","Execute tools from partial arguments to cut latency","Buffer deltas until a block ends","Confirm risky actions with the user"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"Complete items, buffering and confirmation prevent partial-action incidents. Executing from partial arguments is the cause of them."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How long does ZDR background data stay on disk for polling?","back":"Roughly 10 minutes. Poll promptly and persist the result yourself."},{"front":"Which Anthropic events carry tool input deltas?","back":"`content_block_delta` with `input_json_delta`, arriving one complete key and value at a time."},{"front":"Which mode do you pick when nobody is waiting?","back":"Batch: 50% of Standard, a 24 hour window and a separate rate-limit pool."}],"related":["w01-t09","w01-t11","w02-t02","w16-t06","w01-l5"],"refs":["tr1","tr2","r35","r34","r37","r39","r38"]},{"id":"w01-t14","order":14,"group":"Adapting models","title":"Prompting vs RAG vs long context vs fine-tuning","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Prompting and context carry facts at run time, RAG fetches them per question, long context preloads them, and fine-tuning changes behaviour. Evidence says use fine-tuning for style, not for facts.","useIf":"You decide how a model gets knowledge or a behaviour it does not have.","avoidIf":"You pick a method before measuring corpus size, update rate, permissions, volume and accuracy needs.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Separate two needs. **Knowledge** (facts the model lacks) belongs in the context, supplied by a prompt, a preloaded long context or retrieval. **Behaviour** (format, tone, tool use, a smaller model) can be learned by fine-tuning. OpenAI lists fine-tuning benefits as format, shorter prompts, smaller models and private data [[r48]], and Azure says fine-tuning does not replace retrieval for current information [[r17]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Cache-augmented generation (CAG)** preloads documents into a long context and caches the runtime state, so there is no retrieval step. The paper says it suits a limited, manageable knowledge base [[r24]]. Anthropic says a knowledge base under about 200,000 tokens (about 500 pages, an estimate) can go straight into the prompt, and prompt caching makes that cheaper and faster [[r42]]. Past that, or when access differs by user, retrieval wins."},{"t":"dials","id":"w01-t14-d1","title":"How should the model get this knowledge?","prompt":"Flip the dials. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"size","label":"Corpus size","values":[{"id":"small","label":"Small, under about 200K tokens"},{"id":"large","label":"Large"}]},{"id":"rate","label":"Update rate","values":[{"id":"stable","label":"Stable"},{"id":"fast","label":"Frequent"}]},{"id":"perm","label":"Permissions","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One audience"},{"id":"user","label":"Per user"}]}],"options":[{"id":"ctx","name":"Long context plus caching (CAG)","summary":"Preload the documents and cache the prefix."},{"id":"rag","name":"RAG","summary":"Retrieve the relevant chunks per question."},{"id":"ft","name":"Fine-tune on the documents","summary":"Train facts into the weights."}],"scores":{"ctx":{"size":{"small":{"s":2,"why":"Fits the window and needs no retrieval stack."},"large":{"s":-2,"why":"Exceeds the window or degrades deep in context."}},"rate":{"stable":{"s":1,"why":"The cached prefix keeps hitting."},"fast":{"s":-1,"why":"Every change invalidates the cache."}},"perm":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"One shared prefix is safe."},"user":{"s":-2,"why":"A shared prefix cannot hold per-user content."}}},"rag":{"size":{"small":{"s":0,"why":"Works, with more moving parts than needed."},"large":{"s":2,"why":"Retrieves only what matters."}},"rate":{"stable":{"s":0,"why":"Fine, no special gain."},"fast":{"s":2,"why":"Re-index changed content, no retraining."}},"perm":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"No special gain."},"user":{"s":2,"why":"Filters trim results per caller."}}},"ft":{"size":{"small":{"s":-1,"why":"New facts are learned slowly and can raise hallucination."},"large":{"s":-1,"why":"Weights cannot reliably hold a large corpus."}},"rate":{"stable":{"s":-1,"why":"Still weaker than retrieval for facts."},"fast":{"s":-2,"why":"Retraining for each change is impractical."}},"perm":{"one":{"s":-1,"why":"No citations, hard to audit."},"user":{"s":-2,"why":"Weights cannot enforce per-user access."}}}},"takeaway":"Fine-tuning scores low here because this board is about knowledge. It wins when the goal is format, tone, tool use or a smaller model. Combine RAG with a tuned model when you need both facts and style."},{"t":"p","md":"Evidence behind the dials: Chroma tested 18 LLMs and found performance grows unreliable as input grows [[r22]]. RULER found only about half of models claiming 32K or more stayed satisfactory at 32K [[r23]], and in NoLiMa 11 of 13 models fell below 50% of their short-context baseline at 32K [[r41]]. RAG consistently beat unsupervised fine-tuning for knowledge [[r43]], and new facts learned by fine-tuning raise hallucination [[r44]]."}],"whereToUse":["Internal handbooks and policy sets: long context when small, RAG when large or permissioned","Product and support knowledge that changes weekly: RAG with incremental indexing","Brand voice, report formats and tool-use habits: fine-tuning or few-shot prompting","Cost control at volume: fine-tune a smaller model to replace a long prompt","Interview answers about grounding an assistant, using the dials above"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Long context plus caching when the corpus is small, stable and one audience","RAG when the corpus is large, changes often or access differs by user","Fine-tuning when you need behaviour or format and have examples","Hybrid when you need current facts and a consistent style"],"avoid":["Fine-tuning to teach facts","Stuffing a large corpus into a prompt because the window allows it","RAG for a 50-page stable handbook that fits in a cached prompt"],"flips":["Corpus outgrows about 200,000 tokens, or accuracy drops deep in context: move to RAG","Permissions become per user: choose retrieval with access filters","A long few-shot prompt costs too much at volume: consider fine-tuning a smaller model, if the platform allows"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Four ways to adapt a model","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Prompt and long context (CAG)","Small, stable corpus, one audience, repeated prefix","Large or per-user content, deep-context accuracy needs","Long-prompt price steps, context rot"],["RAG","Large or changing corpus, citations, per-user access","Poor retrieval, whole-document reasoning","Ingestion, index and ranking work"],["Fine-tuning","Format, tone, tool use, smaller model, private data","You want to add facts or current information","Data labelling, platform limits, retirement dates"],["Hybrid (RAG plus tuned model)","Both facts and behaviour matter","Either alone suffices","Two systems to run and evaluate"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The assistant answers confidently from outdated facts after fine-tuning","cause":"Facts were trained into weights and the source changed","fix":"Move facts to retrieval and keep fine-tuning for behaviour"},{"symptom":"Answers miss facts placed deep in a very long prompt","cause":"Accuracy falls as input grows and with position in the context","fix":"Test with answers at different depths, shorten the context or use retrieval"},{"symptom":"A shared cached prompt exposes content to the wrong users","cause":"Per-user content was put into a shared prefix","fix":"Keep per-user content out of shared prefixes and use filtered retrieval"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t14-s1","title":"Large, fast-changing, permissioned knowledge","situation":"An assistant answers from several million tokens of documents that change hourly. Each employee may see only some documents, and answers need citations.","constraints":["Corpus is large","Hourly updates","Per-user permissions","Citations required"],"unknowns":["How fast must a revocation apply?","What accuracy is expected on exact identifiers?"],"question":"How does the model get the knowledge?","options":[{"name":"Fine-tune a model on the documents weekly","verdict":"poor","why":"Facts go stale between runs, weights cannot enforce per-user access and there are no citations.","tradeoff":"Fine-tuning is right for style or format on stable examples."},{"name":"Long context with a cached prefix of the most-used documents","verdict":"good","why":"Fast for popular documents.","tradeoff":"It cannot hold the corpus or enforce per-user access in a shared prefix."},{"name":"RAG with incremental indexing and per-user security filters","verdict":"best","why":"Handles size, hourly changes, permissions and citations together.","tradeoff":"You build and tune ingestion, retrieval and access control."}],"flips":[{"if":"Revocation must apply within seconds","then":"Add a live permission check on hits."},{"if":"Only a small slice is needed and is public","then":"A cached prompt for that slice is simpler."}],"takeaway":"Size, churn and permissions point to retrieval."},{"id":"w01-t14-s2","title":"A 150,000 token handbook with a tight latency target","situation":"A team builds an HR helper over a single 150,000 token handbook. It changes quarterly, has one audience and needs answers within 3 seconds at p95.","constraints":["Small, stable corpus","One audience","p95 of 3 seconds","Low budget"],"unknowns":["Does accuracy hold at the deepest handbook positions?","How many questions share the prefix?"],"question":"Which approach do you start with?","options":[{"name":"Preload the handbook and cache the prefix","verdict":"best","why":"It fits the roughly 200,000 token guidance, skips retrieval and keeps latency low [[r42]] [[r24]].","tradeoff":"Test accuracy at different depths, and expect the cache to reset on each quarterly update."},{"name":"RAG with hybrid search and a reranker","verdict":"good","why":"Scales if the handbook grows.","tradeoff":"Adds ingestion, an index and tuning the constraints do not need."},{"name":"Fine-tune a model on the handbook","verdict":"poor","why":"Facts are learned slowly, can raise hallucination and give no citations.","tradeoff":"Tuning suits tone or format."}],"flips":[{"if":"The handbook grows well past the window","then":"Move to RAG."},{"if":"Different roles see different sections","then":"Use retrieval with filters."}],"takeaway":"Small and stable favours the simplest option that works."},{"id":"w01-t14-s3","title":"A 6,000 token prompt run two million times a month","situation":"A support tool needs a strict five-section reply in a brand voice. A prompt with 20 examples works but costs too much at two million requests a month. The team has 2,000 approved replies and runs on Azure OpenAI.","constraints":["Behaviour and format, not new facts","High volume","Azure OpenAI","Approved examples exist"],"unknowns":["Does a smaller tuned model match the prompt baseline?","When does the base model retire?"],"question":"What do you try?","options":[{"name":"Keep the long few-shot prompt and rely on prompt caching","verdict":"good","why":"No training and quick to run.","tradeoff":"Pays for the cached prefix each call and relies on cache hits."},{"name":"Fine-tune a smaller supported model with SFT on the approved replies, compare it with the prompt baseline, and check its retirement date","verdict":"best","why":"The need is format and tone with data in hand, and a shorter prompt cuts cost at volume.","tradeoff":"Training effort, a retirement date to track, and re-tuning if the style changes."},{"name":"Use RAG over past replies to teach the tone","verdict":"poor","why":"Retrieval supplies facts and examples per query. It is a weak way to enforce a consistent format.","tradeoff":"RAG fits when the examples are knowledge, such as past answers to specific cases."}],"flips":[{"if":"The platform is OpenAI directly and the org has no fine-tuning history","then":"New fine-tuning jobs may be blocked, so use prompting or Azure."},{"if":"The style changes monthly","then":"Prompting is cheaper than repeated tuning."}],"takeaway":"Tune for behaviour at volume, and verify the platform still offers it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t14-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which approach does the evidence favour for adding new facts to a model?","options":["Unsupervised fine-tuning","A higher temperature","A smaller model","Retrieval or context supplied at run time"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ovadia et al. found RAG consistently beat unsupervised fine-tuning, and Gekhman et al. found new facts learned through fine-tuning raise hallucination."},{"id":"w01-t14-q2","type":"multi","tag":"flip","q":"Which dial changes push you from a cached long prompt to RAG?","options":["The corpus outgrows about 200,000 tokens","Permissions become per user","The corpus changes hourly","The team prefers shorter prompts"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Size, per-user access and frequent change all break a shared cached prefix. A preference for short prompts does not change the answer."},{"id":"w01-t14-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each approach to its best-fit job.","pairs":[{"l":"Cached long context (CAG)","r":"Small, stable corpus, one audience"},{"l":"RAG","r":"Large, changing or permissioned corpus with citations"},{"l":"SFT fine-tuning","r":"Format, tone and tool-use behaviour"},{"l":"Distillation","r":"Moving a large model's behaviour to a small model"}],"explain":"Each approach has a primary job. Mixing them up leads to fine-tuning for facts or RAG for tone."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does fine-tuning change best?","back":"Behaviour: format, tone, tool use, shorter prompts and smaller models. It is weak for adding facts."},{"front":"What is CAG and when does it fit?","back":"Cache-augmented generation preloads documents and caches runtime state. It fits a small, stable corpus, roughly under 200,000 tokens per Anthropic's guidance."},{"front":"Name three studies behind the long-context caution.","back":"Chroma Context Rot (18 LLMs), RULER (17 models) and NoLiMa (13 models, 11 below 50% of baseline at 32K)."}],"related":["w01-t15","w01-t16","w01-l6","w05-t15","w02-t14"],"refs":["r48","r17","r24","r42","r22","r23","r41","r43","r44"]},{"id":"w01-t15","order":15,"group":"Adapting models","title":"Fine-tuning methods: SFT, DPO, RFT and LoRA","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"SFT learns from correct answers, DPO from preferred and rejected pairs, RFT from a grader, and LoRA is a cheap way to train adapters. Platform availability is the first question.","useIf":"You need a consistent format, tone, tool-use behaviour or a smaller model and you have examples.","avoidIf":"You want to add facts or current information. Evidence favours retrieval for that.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**SFT** (supervised fine-tuning) trains on input and ideal-output examples. OpenAI needs at least 10 examples, often sees gains at 50 to 100, and lists the `gpt-4.1` family [[r52]]. **DPO** (direct preference optimization) trains on preferred and rejected pairs with a `beta` between 0 and 2, where higher stays closer to the base model. It replaces the RLHF reward model and RL loop with a classification loss [[r53]] [[r46]]. **RFT** (reinforcement fine-tuning) scores outputs with a grader you write, and OpenAI lists `o4-mini` only [[r54]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**LoRA** freezes the weights and trains small low-rank matrices. Against full Adam fine-tuning of GPT-3 175B it cut trainable parameters 10,000 times and GPU memory 3 times, with no added inference latency [[r45]]. **QLoRA** fine-tunes a 65B model on one 48GB GPU using 4-bit NormalFloat, double quantization and paged optimizers [[r25]]. These are paper results, not product limits."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w01-t15-d1","title":"Which method for which signal","alt":"Decision flow: if you need facts use retrieval; otherwise choose SFT with correct answers, DPO with preference pairs, RFT with a reliable grader, or LoRA and QLoRA for open weights you host.","src":"flowchart TD\n  A[What do you need] -->|Facts| B[RAG or context]\n  A -->|Behaviour| C{What signal do you have}\n  C -->|Correct answers| D[SFT]\n  C -->|Preferred and rejected pairs| E[DPO]\n  C -->|A reliable grader| F[RFT]\n  C -->|Open weights you host| G[LoRA or QLoRA]"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"OpenAI is winding down self-serve fine-tuning","md":"Notice date 2026-05-07. Organisations that never ran fine-tuning cannot create jobs. From 2026-07-02, organisations with no fine-tuned inference in 60 days cannot. On 2027-01-06 active customers can no longer create jobs. Fine-tuned inference ends only when its base model is deprecated, several fine-tuned ids end on 2026-10-23, and the GPT-6 Astra page marks the fine-tuning endpoint unsupported [[r11]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Azure** keeps managed SFT, DPO and RFT. Interactive training is Preview and needs approval. Training types are Standard, Data zone (US only), Global and Developer (preemptible, no SLA). Deployment options are Serverless, Managed compute (Preview, LoRA adapters) and Fireworks on Foundry (Preview). Models include the `gpt-4.1` family (SFT, DPO), `gpt-4o` (SFT, DPO), `gpt-4o-mini` (SFT), `o4-mini` (RFT) and open-weight models, with `gpt-5` RFT by invitation only. Training on the `gpt-4.1` family ends no earlier than 2027-04-14 for existing customers [[r17]] [[r13]]. Azure retires `o4-mini`, the RFT model, on 2026-11-19, so plan a replacement [[tr5]]."}],"whereToUse":["A strict output format or house style that few-shot prompts follow unreliably","Tool-use habits, such as always calling a tool in a certain situation","Replacing a long few-shot prompt with a tuned smaller model at high volume","Preference alignment, such as concise versus verbose answers (DPO)","Self-hosted open-weight models adapted with LoRA or QLoRA"],"whenToUse":{"use":["SFT when you can show the right answer and have hundreds of clean examples","DPO when you can compare two outputs but not write the ideal one","RFT when a reliable programmatic grader exists","LoRA or QLoRA when you host open weights and GPU memory is limited"],"avoid":["Fine-tuning to teach facts or keep knowledge current","Tuning on a base model that retires soon without a migration plan","RFT with a noisy or gameable grader"],"flips":["The platform blocks new jobs: use structured outputs and few-shot prompting, or use Azure or open weights","The prompt baseline already meets the bar: do not tune","Data is small and noisy: improve data or prompts before tuning"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Methods compared","head":["Method","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["SFT","You can show correct answers","Examples are few or inconsistent","Needs clean data, can overfit"],["DPO","You hold preferred and rejected pairs","Only single gold answers exist","Pair quality and `beta` tuning"],["RFT","A reliable grader scores outputs","Quality is subjective","Few models, grader gaming"],["LoRA or QLoRA","You host open weights on limited GPUs","You use a managed API only","Hosting and serving operations"],["Prompting and structured outputs","A prompt baseline nearly works","Behaviour must be baked into a small model","Longer prompts, higher run cost"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A new organisation cannot start a fine-tuning job on OpenAI","cause":"Since 2026-05-07 orgs that never ran fine-tuning cannot create jobs","fix":"Check eligibility first, or use Azure, open weights or prompting"},{"symptom":"The tuned model repeats old facts","cause":"Facts were trained into weights and the source changed","fix":"Keep facts in retrieval and tune behaviour only"},{"symptom":"A tuned model disappears from a deployment plan","cause":"Fine-tuned inference ends when its base model is deprecated","fix":"Record the base-model retirement date and schedule a re-tune"},{"symptom":"RFT improves the grader score and not the task","cause":"The grader is gameable or measures a proxy","fix":"Audit the grader on held-out samples and add checks"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t15-s1","title":"A new team wants SFT for a format task","situation":"A team with no fine-tuning history on the OpenAI API has 1,500 approved examples and wants a model that always produces a five-section report. There is no hard platform constraint.","constraints":["Behaviour and format, not facts","1,500 clean examples","No prior fine-tuning on OpenAI direct"],"unknowns":["Does a strict schema plus few-shot prompting already meet the bar?","When does the candidate base model retire?"],"question":"What is the plan?","options":[{"name":"Run SFT on Azure Foundry on a supported model after confirming its retirement date, and keep a prompt baseline to beat","verdict":"best","why":"The need is format with data in hand, and Azure offers managed SFT while OpenAI blocks new organisations [[r17]] [[r11]].","tradeoff":"Training effort, a retirement date to track (`gpt-4.1` training ends no earlier than 2027-04-14) and a re-tune later."},{"name":"Use structured outputs and a few-shot prompt, and skip training","verdict":"good","why":"Nothing to train or retire, and may already pass.","tradeoff":"Longer prompts and weaker format consistency at volume."},{"name":"Plan on OpenAI fine-tuning next quarter","verdict":"poor","why":"Organisations that never ran fine-tuning have been blocked from creating jobs since 2026-05-07.","tradeoff":"It remains valid for orgs with eligible fine-tuning history."}],"flips":[{"if":"The prompt baseline already meets the bar","then":"Skip tuning."},{"if":"Data must stay in a specific region","then":"Pick the Azure training type that matches residency."}],"takeaway":"Check platform availability and the baseline before you collect data."},{"id":"w01-t15-s2","title":"Shifting style from rater preferences","situation":"Raters compared pairs of answers and chose the better one in 2,000 cases. There is no ideal answer for each prompt, and quality is subjective.","constraints":["Preference pairs exist","No single gold answer","No programmatic grader"],"unknowns":["How consistent are the raters?","Which base model is supported for DPO?"],"question":"Which method fits?","options":[{"name":"SFT on the chosen answers only","verdict":"good","why":"Easy to run and uses part of the signal.","tradeoff":"It discards the rejected examples that teach what to avoid."},{"name":"DPO on the pairs, tuning `beta`","verdict":"best","why":"It uses the preferred and rejected signal directly, and `beta` controls how far the model moves from the base [[r53]].","tradeoff":"Pair quality matters and results need an eval set."},{"name":"RFT with a grader","verdict":"poor","why":"Subjective quality has no reliable programmatic grader, so the model may chase the grader.","tradeoff":"RFT suits tasks with checkable answers, such as structured correctness."}],"flips":[{"if":"A reliable grader exists","then":"RFT becomes an option on supported models."},{"if":"You gather ideal answers","then":"SFT is simpler."}],"takeaway":"Match the method to the signal you have."},{"id":"w01-t15-s3","title":"Adapting an open-weight model on one GPU","situation":"A team hosts an open-weight 65B model and has one 48GB GPU. They need domain-specific behaviour and cannot send data to a hosted API.","constraints":["Self-hosted","One 48GB GPU","Data cannot leave the environment"],"unknowns":["How many training examples?","Is a 4-bit base acceptable for quality?"],"question":"How do you train it?","options":[{"name":"Full fine-tuning of all weights","verdict":"poor","why":"It needs far more memory than a single 48GB GPU provides for a 65B model.","tradeoff":"Full tuning suits a small model or a large cluster."},{"name":"LoRA on a 16-bit base","verdict":"good","why":"Adapters cut trainable parameters and memory.","tradeoff":"The 16-bit base itself may not fit a single 48GB GPU at this size."},{"name":"QLoRA with a 4-bit base","verdict":"best","why":"It fine-tuned a 65B model on one 48GB GPU in the paper, with LoRA adapters [[r25]].","tradeoff":"Paper results are not a product guarantee, and 4-bit quantization may cost some quality."}],"flips":[{"if":"A multi-GPU node is available","then":"LoRA on a higher-precision base is an option."},{"if":"Data may go to a managed service","then":"Azure managed fine-tuning is simpler."}],"takeaway":"QLoRA trades some precision for fitting big models on small GPUs."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t15-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each method to the signal it needs.","pairs":[{"l":"SFT","r":"Input and ideal-output examples"},{"l":"DPO","r":"Preferred and rejected pairs"},{"l":"RFT","r":"A reliable grader"},{"l":"QLoRA","r":"A 4-bit base with LoRA adapters on limited GPU memory"}],"explain":"Each method consumes a different training signal. LoRA and QLoRA are parameter-efficient ways to train, and are not signals themselves."},{"id":"w01-t15-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A team that never ran fine-tuning on the OpenAI API tries to create a job and is refused. Why?","options":["The dataset is too small","OpenAI wound down self-serve fine-tuning for orgs without prior use, from 2026-05-07","The model is unsupported by SFT","Jobs need an invitation on every platform"],"answer":1,"explain":"Notice date 2026-05-07: organisations that never ran fine-tuning cannot create jobs. Dataset size and model support are separate issues, and Azure offers managed methods."},{"id":"w01-t15-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"LoRA cut trainable parameters 10,000 times against full Adam fine-tuning of GPT-3 175B. Roughly how many parameters does that leave to train?","answer":17500000,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"parameters","explain":"175,000,000,000 divided by 10,000 is 17,500,000 trainable parameters. The ratio is a paper result for that setup, not a general constant."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"When do you choose DPO over SFT?","back":"When you have preferred and rejected pairs but no single ideal answer per prompt. `beta` (0 to 2) sets how far the model moves from the base."},{"front":"What did the QLoRA paper show?","back":"A 65B model fine-tuned on one 48GB GPU using 4-bit NormalFloat, double quantization and paged optimizers."},{"front":"When does OpenAI stop new fine-tuning jobs for active customers?","back":"2027-01-06. Fine-tuned inference ends when the base model is deprecated."},{"front":"Which methods does Azure offer for managed fine-tuning?","back":"SFT, DPO and RFT, with Standard, Data zone, Global and Developer training types."}],"related":["w01-t14","w01-t16","w01-l6","w18-t01"],"refs":["r52","r53","r54","r46","r45","r25","r11","r17","r13","tr5"]},{"id":"w01-t16","order":16,"group":"Adapting models","title":"Distillation and small models","minutes":7,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Distillation trains a small student model on a large teacher's outputs. Small models cost far less per token, and they win on narrow, high-volume, latency-bound tasks when evals confirm quality.","useIf":"Volume, latency or cost per task rules out the largest model and the task is narrow enough for a small one.","avoidIf":"The task is open-ended, rare and high stakes, or you cannot build an eval set to compare student and teacher.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Distillation** trains a small model to imitate a large one. The idea comes from Hinton et al. (2015) and works by training on the teacher's outputs [[r47]]. OpenAI describes the pattern as tuning a prompt on a large model, then fine-tuning a smaller model on its outputs [[r48]] [[r52]]. You then serve the student at a fraction of the price and latency."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"GPT-6 Astra","value":"$10 in, $50 out","note":"USD per 1M tokens [[r1]]"},{"label":"GPT-6.1 Sol and GPT-6 Sol","value":"$2 in, $10 out","note":"Same 1,050,000 token window [[r1]]"},{"label":"GPT-6 Luna","value":"$0.10 in, $0.50 out","note":"100 times cheaper than Astra on both sides"}]},{"t":"flow","title":"A distillation pipeline","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Define","md":"Fix the task and an eval set the student must pass."},{"title":"Generate","md":"The teacher answers many diverse inputs."},{"title":"Filter","md":"Verify or score outputs and drop bad ones."},{"title":"Train","md":"Fine-tune the student, for example with SFT or LoRA."},{"title":"Compare","md":"Test the student against the teacher and the prompt baseline."},{"title":"Serve","md":"Deploy with a fallback to the teacher for hard cases."}]},{"t":"p","md":"A **small model** is often enough without any training. Try it first at low effort and let evals decide. OpenAI says Astra can cost less per task when it uses fewer output tokens, which is a vendor claim, so compare cost per task, not price per token [[r2]]. Check the provider's terms before training on another model's outputs."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Check that tuning is available","md":"OpenAI is winding down self-serve fine-tuning, with no new jobs for active customers after 2027-01-06, while Azure keeps SFT, DPO and RFT on supported models and open-weight models support LoRA [[r11]] [[r17]]. A distilled student is only useful if you can still train and host it."}],"whereToUse":["High-volume classification, extraction and routing where a narrow task repeats","Latency-critical steps, such as the first hop of a voice or chat flow","Cost caps on features with millions of daily calls","Private or offline hosting of an open-weight student","Cascades: a small model handles most traffic and a large one handles escalations"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The task is narrow, stable and well covered by an eval set","Volume makes a 10 to 100 times price gap decisive","You can generate or collect teacher outputs and verify them"],"avoid":["Distilling an open-ended assistant, where the student cannot cover the long tail","Skipping the baseline: a small model with a good prompt may already pass","Training on unfiltered teacher mistakes"],"flips":["The student misses the bar on hard cases: add a cascade with escalation to the teacher","Volume falls: the engineering cost may exceed the savings","The teacher changes: re-run the evals and consider regenerating data"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to cut cost per task","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Small model plus prompt","Narrow task, evals pass","Hard multi-step reasoning","Cheapest to try"],["Distilled student","High volume, narrow task, teacher outputs available","Low volume, shifting task","Data pipeline, training, platform limits"],["Cascade (small, then large)","Mixed difficulty, a reliable escalation signal","No signal, or tight latency on escalations","Evaluate the router itself"],["Large model with caching and batch","Quality first, prefixes repeat","Volume is extreme","Still the highest price per token"],["Large model at low effort","Small models fail, tokens are the cost","Latency is tight","Check cost per task"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The student matches the teacher on average but fails on rare cases","cause":"Training data lacked the long tail","fix":"Sample hard and rare inputs, keep a cascade to the teacher"},{"symptom":"Savings vanish after the migration","cause":"A weaker model needed longer prompts or more retries","fix":"Measure cost per accepted task, not price per token"},{"symptom":"The distilled model degrades after a teacher update","cause":"The data was generated by an older teacher version","fix":"Pin the teacher, version datasets and re-run evals on change"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t16-s1","title":"Extraction at 50 million calls a day","situation":"A pipeline extracts six fields from short messages, 50 million a day. A large model passes the 97% accuracy bar and a small model with a good prompt reaches 94%. Latency target is 300 ms.","constraints":["Narrow, stable task","100,000 unlabelled messages available","Fine-tuning is available on the platform","Volume is very high"],"unknowns":["Does the 3 point gap come from a few hard cases?","What does a wrong extraction cost?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Move everything to the large model","verdict":"poor","why":"It meets accuracy but at about 100 times the token price at this volume, and latency suffers.","tradeoff":"Right when volume is low and each error is costly."},{"name":"Distill: the large model labels the 100,000 messages, you verify a sample, fine-tune the small model, and compare it with the teacher on a held-out set","verdict":"best","why":"The task is narrow and the data is available, and the student can close the gap at small-model price and latency.","tradeoff":"A data pipeline, training and a fallback for hard cases."},{"name":"Cascade: small model first, escalate low-confidence cases to the large model","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap to build and protects accuracy.","tradeoff":"Needs a reliable confidence signal, and escalations add latency and cost."}],"flips":[{"if":"Fine-tuning is unavailable on your platform","then":"Use the cascade, or host an open-weight student."},{"if":"Accuracy of 94% is acceptable","then":"Ship the small model with the prompt."}],"takeaway":"At extreme volume, close the quality gap with training, not with a larger model."},{"id":"w01-t16-s2","title":"A few hundred high-stakes analyses a day","situation":"Analysts request about 200 deep contract comparisons a day. Errors are costly, answers are reviewed by humans, and cost per request is a minor concern.","constraints":["Low volume","High stakes","Open-ended reasoning"],"unknowns":["How much better is the large model on the eval set?","What latency do analysts accept?"],"question":"Which model strategy do you choose?","options":[{"name":"A small model, to keep the cost low","verdict":"poor","why":"The savings are tiny at 200 requests a day while the quality risk is large.","tradeoff":"Small models fit narrow, high-volume tasks."},{"name":"A mid-size model at `medium` effort","verdict":"good","why":"A balanced cost and quality.","tradeoff":"May miss subtle issues the large model catches."},{"name":"The large model at high effort, with human review","verdict":"best","why":"At this volume the price gap is small, and the quality ceiling matters most.","tradeoff":"Higher cost per task and slower answers."}],"flips":[{"if":"Volume grows to millions of requests","then":"Revisit a cascade or a distilled student."},{"if":"Evals show the mid-size model equals the large one","then":"Use the mid-size model."}],"takeaway":"At low volume, buy quality. Optimise cost where volume makes it matter."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t16-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A workload uses 500M input tokens and 100M output tokens a month on GPT-6 Astra ($10 per 1M input, $50 per 1M output). What is the monthly cost in USD?","answer":10000,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"USD","explain":"Input: 500 x $10 = $5,000. Output: 100 x $50 = $5,000. Total $10,000. On GPT-6 Luna ($0.10 and $0.50) the same usage costs $100."},{"id":"w01-t16-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"When does a small model most clearly beat a large one?","options":["A narrow, high-volume task that passes your evals","Rare, open-ended, high-stakes analysis","Tasks that need the largest possible context","Any task, because small models are always cheaper per task"],"answer":0,"explain":"Narrow, high-volume tasks that pass evals are where the price and latency gap pays. Open-ended high-stakes work favours the large model, and cost per task can differ from price per token."},{"id":"w01-t16-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the distillation pipeline.","items":["Define the task and eval set","Teacher generates outputs","Filter and verify outputs","Train the student","Compare with teacher and baseline","Serve with a fallback"],"explain":"You need an eval before you generate data, filtering before training, and a comparison before you deploy."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is distillation?","back":"Training a small student model on a large teacher's outputs (Hinton et al., 2015), so the student imitates the teacher at lower cost and latency."},{"front":"How big is the price gap between GPT-6 Astra and GPT-6 Luna?","back":"About 100 times on both sides: $10 and $50 against $0.10 and $0.50 per 1M input and output tokens."},{"front":"What should you compare, price per token or cost per task?","back":"Cost per task. A model with fewer output tokens or retries can cost less per accepted task."}],"related":["w01-t15","w01-t17","w01-t03","w16-t03","w01-l3"],"refs":["r47","r48","r52","r1","r2","r11","r17"]},{"id":"w01-t17","order":17,"group":"Adapting models","title":"Model routing and cascades","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A router picks a model per request, and a cascade tries a cheap model first and escalates on low confidence or a failed check. Both save cost only if you evaluate them against single-model baselines.","useIf":"Traffic mixes easy and hard requests and one model is either too costly or too weak for all of it.","avoidIf":"Traffic is uniform, volume is low, or you have no eval set. A single model also gives the best prompt-cache reuse.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Routing comes in four forms. **Rules** use task type or input length. A **learned router** is a classifier that predicts which model will do. An **LLM router** asks a small model to choose. A **cascade** runs a cheap model first and escalates when a validator fails or confidence is low. A managed option is the **Azure model router**, a deployable model that picks from a pool per request [[r16]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w01-t17-d1","title":"A confidence-based cascade","alt":"A request goes to a small model. If the output is valid and confident it is returned, otherwise the request goes to a large model whose answer is returned.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Request] --> S[Small model]\n  S --> V{Valid and confident}\n  V -->|Yes| A[Answer]\n  V -->|No| L[Large model]\n  L --> A"},{"t":"table","caption":"Azure model router facts (check date 2026-10-13)","head":["Topic","Detail"],"compact":true,"rows":[["Versions","`2025-11-18` is active and updated in place. `2025-08-07` and `2025-05-19` are frozen"],["Modes","Balanced (default, cheapest model within about 1% to 2% of top quality), Cost (about 5% to 6%), Quality. Microsoft gives the bands as examples"],["Subset and failover","Model subset limits the pool and needs at least two models for failover. Failover is on by default"],["Deployment types","Global Standard and Data Zone Standard only"],["Pool","OpenAI (including `gpt-6-astra`), Anthropic, xAI, DeepSeek, Meta and Fireworks models. Claude models must be deployed separately first"],["Side effects","Effective context window is the smallest in the pool. Prompt caching helps only when the same model serves consecutive requests. Session affinity for Chat Completions is Preview"]]},{"t":"p","md":"A cascade you build yourself needs an **escalation signal**: a schema or rule check, a logprob threshold on a label (see the logprobs topic), a verifier model or agreement across samples. You must evaluate it, because the router itself can be wrong in two ways: it sends hard requests to the small model, or it escalates easy ones and wastes money."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Evaluate a router like a model","md":"Replay the same traffic through always-small, always-large and the router. Report quality at equal cost (or cost at equal quality), escalation rate, router errors by segment, p95 latency including escalations, and cache hit rate. A router that wins on average can lose on one customer segment."}],"whereToUse":["Support and chat traffic where most questions are simple and a few are hard","Extraction pipelines with a validator that can reject a weak answer","Platforms that front several teams and want one cost-controlled endpoint","Failover across models in a pool when one is rate limited","Interview answers about cost control without lowering the quality ceiling"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Difficulty varies widely and you can measure it","A cheap check can catch a bad answer","Volume is high enough that the cost gap matters","You accept a managed router's pool and deployment limits"],"avoid":["Routing without a baseline comparison","Long-context requests through a pool that includes smaller windows","Cache-heavy workloads where model switching breaks prefix reuse"],"flips":["You need regional or provisioned deployments: the Azure router does not fit, so use rules or your own cascade","Prompt-cache savings exceed routing savings: pin one model","Requests exceed the smallest window in the pool: restrict the subset or route by length"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Router types compared","head":["Type","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Single model","Traffic is uniform, caching matters","Difficulty varies widely","Simplest, best cache reuse"],["Rule-based routing","Task type or length predicts difficulty","Difficulty is hidden in content","Rules drift, brittle"],["Cascade with validator","A cheap check detects weak answers","No reliable check, tight latency","Escalations add latency and cost"],["Learned or LLM router","You have labelled routing data","Routes are few or unstable","The router itself needs evals and adds a hop"],["Azure model router","Global or Data Zone Standard, low ops","Regional or provisioned deployments, long contexts","Pool changes in place, smaller windows, cache loss"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Large requests fail only through the router","cause":"The effective window is the smallest model in the pool","fix":"Use a model subset with large-window models, or route long inputs by rule"},{"symptom":"Cache hit rate and savings drop after adopting a router","cause":"Prompt caching helps only when the same model serves consecutive requests","fix":"Use session affinity where available (Chat Completions, Preview) or pin one model for cache-heavy flows"},{"symptom":"Behaviour changes without a deployment change","cause":"Version `2025-11-18` is updated in place and the pool can change","fix":"Pin a subset, monitor quality continuously and re-run evals on a schedule"},{"symptom":"Costs rise after adding a cascade","cause":"Too many requests escalate, paying for both models","fix":"Tune the threshold on labelled data and track the escalation rate"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w01-t17-s1","title":"Mixed traffic with a managed option available","situation":"A support assistant sees mostly simple questions and a tail of hard ones. The team runs on Azure with a Global Standard deployment and wants low operational overhead.","constraints":["Global Standard deployment is allowed","Quality must stay within about 2% of the large model","Small ops team","Eval set exists"],"unknowns":["Which models may be in the pool?","How cache-heavy is the traffic?"],"question":"What do you adopt?","options":[{"name":"Send everything to the large model","verdict":"poor","why":"It meets quality but pays the top price for easy traffic.","tradeoff":"Right for low volume, or when nothing meets the quality bar."},{"name":"Build your own cascade with validators","verdict":"good","why":"Full control over thresholds and models.","tradeoff":"More engineering, and you evaluate and maintain the router."},{"name":"Azure model router in Balanced mode with a model subset limited to models that pass your evals, then compare it with the large-only baseline","verdict":"best","why":"Balanced picks the cheapest model within about 1% to 2% of top quality and adds failover, with little ops work [[r16]].","tradeoff":"The pool can change in place, prompt-cache reuse drops and the window is the smallest in the pool."}],"flips":[{"if":"Traffic is dominated by repeated long prefixes","then":"Pin one model to protect caching."},{"if":"A regional deployment is mandatory","then":"The router is unavailable, so use a cascade."}],"takeaway":"A managed router trades control for low operations, so measure it against baselines."},{"id":"w01-t17-s2","title":"Regional deployment required","situation":"A regulated team must run on regional Standard deployments in one country. Traffic has a clear easy and hard split, and a schema check catches weak answers.","constraints":["Regional deployments only","Validator available","Cost pressure"],"unknowns":["Escalation rate at the chosen threshold","Latency budget for escalated requests"],"question":"How do you route?","options":[{"name":"Own two-tier cascade: small model first, escalate when the schema check fails, evaluated against a large-only baseline","verdict":"best","why":"The managed router is limited to Global Standard and Data Zone Standard, while a cascade runs on any deployment and has a clear escalation signal.","tradeoff":"You build, tune and monitor it, and escalated requests pay both models."},{"name":"A single mid-size model for all traffic","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and cache-friendly.","tradeoff":"Overpays for easy traffic or underperforms on hard traffic."},{"name":"Azure model router","verdict":"poor","why":"It supports only Global Standard and Data Zone Standard deployments.","tradeoff":"It suits teams that can use those deployment types."}],"flips":[{"if":"Data zone residency satisfies the regulation","then":"The managed router becomes an option."},{"if":"The validator misses many errors","then":"Add a logprob or verifier signal before escalating."}],"takeaway":"Residency can rule out managed routing, and a cascade is the portable fallback."},{"id":"w01-t17-s3","title":"Occasional 600,000 token requests","situation":"Most requests are short, but about 2% carry 600,000 tokens of documents. The router pool includes models with 400,000 token windows.","constraints":["Long requests must succeed","Most traffic is short","Cost matters"],"unknowns":["Can long requests be routed by length before the router?","Which pool models have 1M windows?"],"question":"How do you keep long requests working?","options":[{"name":"Let the router decide with no restrictions","verdict":"poor","why":"The effective window is the smallest in the pool, so large calls can fail when a small-window model is picked.","tradeoff":"Fine when every request fits the smallest window."},{"name":"Restrict the model subset to large-window models, or route long inputs by rule to a pinned large-window model","verdict":"best","why":"It guarantees a model that can take the input, and short traffic keeps the cheaper path.","tradeoff":"Long requests pay a higher price, and the subset reduces routing options."},{"name":"Truncate every input to fit the smallest window","verdict":"good","why":"No failures.","tradeoff":"Drops content the task needs."}],"flips":[{"if":"Long requests are 20% of traffic","then":"Reassess retrieval to shorten inputs."},{"if":"All pool models have 1M windows","then":"No restriction is needed."}],"takeaway":"Check the smallest window in the pool before you route long inputs."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w01-t17-q1","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Which are true about the Azure model router?","options":["The effective window is the smallest in the pool","It works on regional Standard deployments","Prompt caching helps only when the same model serves consecutive requests","Claude models must be deployed separately before they can be in the pool"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"The window is the smallest in the pool, caching needs the same model and Claude models need a separate deployment. The router supports only Global Standard and Data Zone Standard."},{"id":"w01-t17-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"What is the strongest evidence that a cascade saves money without hurting quality?","options":["The small model is cheaper","A replay of the same traffic shows equal quality at lower cost against always-large","The router has a high escalation rate","The large model is fast"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only a same-traffic comparison with baselines shows quality at cost. A high escalation rate can increase cost, and price alone says nothing about quality."},{"id":"w01-t17-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each Azure router mode to its description.","pairs":[{"l":"Balanced (default)","r":"Cheapest model within about 1% to 2% of top quality"},{"l":"Cost","r":"Cheapest model within about 5% to 6%"},{"l":"Quality","r":"Favours the best model"},{"l":"Model subset","r":"Limits the pool, at least two models for failover"}],"explain":"Microsoft gives the quality bands as examples. A subset restricts the pool, and failover needs at least two models in it."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name four router types.","back":"Rules, a learned router, an LLM router and a cascade. Azure also offers a managed model router."},{"front":"Which deployments support the Azure model router?","back":"Global Standard and Data Zone Standard only."},{"front":"How do you evaluate a router?","back":"Replay the same traffic through always-small, always-large and the router, then compare quality at cost, escalation rate, router errors by segment, p95 latency and cache hit rate."}],"related":["w01-t16","w01-t07","w01-t03","w16-t03","w02-t14","w01-l3"],"refs":["r16","r13","r15"]}]});
